x_execute_file\nmcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index\nmcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_index\nmcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_insight\nmcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_purge\nmcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search\nmcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_stats\nmcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_upgrade\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__create_directory\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__edit_block\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__force_terminate\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__get_config\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__get_file_info\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__get_more_search_results\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__get_prompts\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__get_recent_tool_calls\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__get_usage_stats\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__give_feedback_to_desktop_commander\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__interact_with_process\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__kill_process\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__list_directory\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__list_processes\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__list_searches\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__list_sessions\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__move_file\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__read_file\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__read_multiple_files\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__read_process_output\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__set_config_value\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__start_process\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__start_search\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__stop_search\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__write_file\nmcp__plugin_desktop-commander_desktop-commander__write_pdf\nmcp__plugin_exa_exa__authenticate\nmcp__plugin_exa_exa__complete_authentication\nmcp__remarkable__remarkable_browse\nmcp__remarkable__remarkable_canvas\nmcp__remarkable__remarkable_image\nmcp__remarkable__remarkable_read\nmcp__remarkable__remarkable_recent\nmcp__remarkable__remarkable_search\nmcp__remarkable__remarkable_status\nmcp__zotero-research__audit_promotion_queue\nmcp__zotero-research__build_research_evidence_pack\nmcp__zotero-research__get_paper\nmcp__zotero-research__get_paper_pages\nmcp__zotero-research__list_library_backlog\nmcp__zotero-research__list_promotion_queue\nmcp__zotero-research__prepare_paper_brief\nmcp__zotero-research__save_paper_brief\nmcp__zotero-research__save_reference_stub\nmcp__zotero-research__search_paper_evidence\nmcp__zotero-research__search_papers\nmcp__zotero-research__sync_zotero_library\nmcp__zotero-research__zotero_health\n</system-reminder>"}],"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"1b11aa49-a84a-4f3f-a01e-740256805cae","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"agent_listing_delta","addedTypes":["claude","claude-code-guide","codex:codex-rescue","Explore","general-purpose","Plan","statusline-setup"],"addedLines":["- claude: Catch-all for any task that doesn't fit a more specific agent. FleetView's default when no agent name is typed. (Tools: *)","- claude-code-guide: Use this agent when the user asks questions (\"Can Claude...\", \"Does Claude...\", \"How do I...\") about: (1) Claude Code (the CLI tool) - features, hooks, slash commands, MCP servers, settings, IDE integrations, keyboard shortcuts; (2) Claude Agent SDK - building custom agents; (3) Claude API (formerly Anthropic API) - Messages API for directly passing messages to Claude, Tool Runner (`client.beta.messages.tool_runner`) for running an agentic loop over your own tools, manual tool-use loops, Managed Agents for server-hosted agents with a managed sandbox, prompt caching, and general Anthropic SDK usage; (4) Claude Tag (Claude in Slack) - what it is, setting it up for a Slack workspace, `/install-slack-app`; (5) `claude plugin eval` (writing and running plugin eval suites, its JSON/report, sandbox, CI) and the `/skill-doctor` report. **IMPORTANT:** Before spawning a new agent, check if there is already a running or recently completed claude-code-guide agent that you can continue via SendMessage. (Tools: Glob, Grep, Read, WebFetch, WebSearch)","- codex:codex-rescue: Proactively use when Claude Code is stuck, wants a second implementation or diagnosis pass, needs a deeper root-cause investigation, or should hand a substantial coding task to Codex through the shared runtime (Tools: Bash)","- Explore: Read-only search agent for broad fan-out searches — when answering means sweeping many files, directories, or naming conventions and you only need the conclusion, not the file dumps. It reads excerpts rather than whole files, so it locates code; it doesn't review or audit it. Specify search breadth: \"medium\" for moderate exploration, \"very thorough\" for multiple locations and naming conventions. (Tools: All tools except Agent, Artifact, ArtifactComments, ArtifactData, ArtifactCheck, ExitPlanMode, Edit, Write, NotebookEdit)","- general-purpose: General-purpose agent for researching complex questions, searching for code, and executing multi-step tasks. When you are searching for a keyword or file and are not confident that you will find the right match in the first few tries use this agent to perform the search for you. (Tools: *)","- Plan: Software architect agent for designing implementation plans. Use this when you need to plan the implementation strategy for a task. Returns step-by-step plans, identifies critical files, and considers architectural trade-offs. (Tools: All tools except Agent, Artifact, ArtifactComments, ArtifactData, ArtifactCheck, ExitPlanMode, Edit, Write, NotebookEdit)","- statusline-setup: Use this agent to configure the user's Claude Code status line setting. (Tools: Read, Edit)"],"removedTypes":[],"isInitial":true,"showConcurrencyNote":true},"type":"attachment","uuid":"fc43c1ce-2633-45cd-941d-727c8b369807","timestamp":"2026-09-28T12:59:21.004Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nAvailable agent types for the Agent tool:\n- claude: Catch-all for any task that doesn't fit a more specific agent. FleetView's default when no agent name is typed. (Tools: *)\n- claude-code-guide: Use this agent when the user asks questions (\"Can Claude...\", \"Does Claude...\", \"How do I...\") about: (1) Claude Code (the CLI tool) - features, hooks, slash commands, MCP servers, settings, IDE integrations, keyboard shortcuts; (2) Claude Agent SDK - building custom agents; (3) Claude API (formerly Anthropic API) - Messages API for directly passing messages to Claude, Tool Runner (`client.beta.messages.tool_runner`) for running an agentic loop over your own tools, manual tool-use loops, Managed Agents for server-hosted agents with a managed sandbox, prompt caching, and general Anthropic SDK usage; (4) Claude Tag (Claude in Slack) - what it is, setting it up for a Slack workspace, `/install-slack-app`; (5) `claude plugin eval` (writing and running plugin eval suites, its JSON/report, sandbox, CI) and the `/skill-doctor` report. **IMPORTANT:** Before spawning a new agent, check if there is already a running or recently completed claude-code-guide agent that you can continue via SendMessage. (Tools: Glob, Grep, Read, WebFetch, WebSearch)\n- codex:codex-rescue: Proactively use when Claude Code is stuck, wants a second implementation or diagnosis pass, needs a deeper root-cause investigation, or should hand a substantial coding task to Codex through the shared runtime (Tools: Bash)\n- Explore: Read-only search agent for broad fan-out searches — when answering means sweeping many files, directories, or naming conventions and you only need the conclusion, not the file dumps. It reads excerpts rather than whole files, so it locates code; it doesn't review or audit it. Specify search breadth: \"medium\" for moderate exploration, \"very thorough\" for multiple locations and naming conventions. (Tools: All tools except Agent, Artifact, ArtifactComments, ArtifactData, ArtifactCheck, ExitPlanMode, Edit, Write, NotebookEdit)\n- general-purpose: General-purpose agent for researching complex questions, searching for code, and executing multi-step tasks. When you are searching for a keyword or file and are not confident that you will find the right match in the first few tries use this agent to perform the search for you. (Tools: *)\n- Plan: Software architect agent for designing implementation plans. Use this when you need to plan the implementation strategy for a task. Returns step-by-step plans, identifies critical files, and considers architectural trade-offs. (Tools: All tools except Agent, Artifact, ArtifactComments, ArtifactData, ArtifactCheck, ExitPlanMode, Edit, Write, NotebookEdit)\n- statusline-setup: Use this agent to configure the user's Claude Code status line setting. (Tools: Read, Edit)\n\nWhen you launch multiple agents for independent work, send them in a single message with multiple tool uses so they run concurrently.\n</system-reminder>"}],"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"fc43c1ce-2633-45cd-941d-727c8b369807","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"mcp_instructions_delta","addedNames":["claude-in-chrome","claude.ai Claude Docs","claude.ai Firecrawl","claude.ai HyperFrames by HeyGen","plugin:browser-use:browser-use","remarkable","zotero-research"],"addedBlocks":["## claude-in-chrome\n**IMPORTANT: If the Chrome browser tools are deferred (must be loaded via ToolSearch before use), load them with ToolSearch before calling them, and batch every tool you expect to need into ONE ToolSearch call (the select query accepts a comma-separated list). Do NOT load tools one at a time; each separate ToolSearch call wastes a full round-trip.**\n\nStart a browser task whose tools are not yet loaded with a single call loading the core set:\n\nToolSearch with query \"select:mcp__claude-in-chrome__tabs_context_mcp,mcp__claude-in-chrome__navigate,mcp__claude-in-chrome__computer,mcp__claude-in-chrome__read_page,mcp__claude-in-chrome__tabs_create_mcp,mcp__claude-in-chrome__tabs_close_mcp\"\n\nAdd task-specific tools to the same call when the task obviously needs them: read_console_messages / read_network_requests for debugging, form_input for forms, gif_creator for recordings, javascript_tool for page scripting. Only issue a second ToolSearch if the task later needs a tool you did not anticipate.","## claude.ai Claude Docs\nClaude Docs: living docs you create and edit here. A docs skill your client lists → load it before any docs call — also before a `read`, comment or tab change on a claude.ai …/artifact/… link (the link is a doc; never web-fetch it). No docs skill or guide text loaded → `guide( items = [\"topic.index\"] )` alone before any docs call but a doc's birth. Make a doc here — not a local file, even when coding — only when the user asks for one, and make it FIRST: the turn's first tool call is its skeleton (title, byline, a `pending` block per section) — a reflex: send it before any search, file read, plan, `guide` or thinking it through; think once it is open — `batch( container = {\"kind\":\"project\",\"create\":{\"name\":\"<title>\",\"doc\":{\"blocks\":{\"asof\":{\"type\":\"date\",\"value\":\"<today>\"},\"me\":{\"type\":\"mention\",\"user\":\"me\"},\"s1\":{\"type\":\"pending\",\"intent\":\"Goals: the three outcomes this quarter commits to\"},\"s2\":{…}},\"markdown\":\"# <title>\\n\\n<?claude block asof?> · <?claude block me?>\\n\\n<?claude block s1?>\\n\\n<?claude block s2?>\"}}}, batch = [] )` (`<?claude block k?>` ↔ `blocks.k`); its ack links the doc → `open` it with your Artifact tool (none → start your next message with the link, once); they're likely watching it fill — keep them posted in a short line naming what you're on (outline up; now <topic>); findings go in the doc, not chat; then `guide( items = [\"topic.index\"] )`, research, and fill each section: `replace` its pending id with `## <heading>` + body; end with one line + the link, never the document. Summoned by a doc comment (turn headed `[Artifact comment sent to Claude]`, `;thread=<root id>`): answer ONLY with a doc comment under that root (`create` an utterance, parent `<root id>`) — no artifact/platform comment tool: that relay thread is resolved and never reaches the doc; an edit asked there → `update` with `answering: \"<root id>\"`.","## claude.ai Firecrawl\nAuthenticated search combines web results, semantic tool summaries and domain matches. Use sources: [\"alexandria\"] for semantic tools only. A search with sources: [\"web\"] omits semantic provider discovery; domainTools: true can still return website-matched tools. Web-only results use domainTools: false. Tool matches describe available structured-data capabilities, not executed data. toolDetail: \"compact\" (default) returns only provider, capability and description; \"summary\" adds metadata; \"full\" includes their input and output contracts. firecrawl_scrape with an alexandria body executes a selected capability; firecrawl_find_tools provides catalogue browsing and full contracts. Firecrawl provides web, developer, and research search, and executes catalogued Alexandria data providers. Use firecrawl_search to find relevant results across the web and specialized indexes; authenticated searches also return matching Alexandria providers in data.tools. firecrawl_find_tools provides catalogue browsing and provider contracts; firecrawl_scrape with an alexandria body executes a selected capability; firecrawl_scrape with a url retrieves one supplied page. For a programming question, firecrawl_developer_search searches indexed public repositories, GitHub issues, merged pull requests, READMEs, and code documentation and returns the matched passages, and skills: \"only\" narrows it to agent-skill files; firecrawl_search with categories: [\"developer\"] reaches the same index beside ordinary web results, returning the hits in the web group rather than as passages and offering no skills filter. For a biomedical, life-science, clinical, or arXiv literature question, the firecrawl_research_* tools search the paper index, while categories: [\"research\"] on firecrawl_search filters ordinary web results to research-affiliated websites. Use the firecrawl_research_* tools to search academic and research literature, expand from anchor papers via the citation graph, and read full-text passages from a specific paper. Search and discovery tools … [truncated]","## claude.ai HyperFrames by HeyGen\n\n[HeyGen HyperFrames MCP] — Programmable HTML video projects.\n\n## When to use this MCP\n\nThis MCP is the primary HyperFrames surface for hosted chat clients (Claude.ai\nweb/desktop, ChatGPT, Grok, and similar), where the user has no local filesystem\nand the agent cannot author HyperFrames code directly.\n\nFrom a CLI/IDE agent on the user's machine (Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, the\nlocalhost MCP Inspector, etc.), **`compose` and `render_video` are disabled** — these clients have a local\nfilesystem, so author HyperFrames with the local HyperFrames skills instead, which\nproduce standalone HTML compositions the user can edit, diff, and commit. Calls to\nthose two tools from such clients are rejected with a pointer to install them:\n\n    npx skills add heygen-com/hyperframes\n\nThe read tools (list_projects, get_project, get_project_status, get_render_status)\nstay available to every client.\n\nAfter installation they expose slash commands like `/hyperframes` (compose),\n`/hyperframes-cli` (init, lint, preview, render), and `/hyperframes-media`\n(TTS, transcribe, remove-background). See\nhttps://github.com/heygen-com/hyperframes for the full skill list and docs.\n\nBut the skills and this MCP are *not* drop-in equivalents:\n\n- Local skills → standalone HTML/GSAP files on disk. No `project_id`, no hosted\n  artifact, no integrated cloud render pipeline.\n- This MCP → hosted HeyGen project with a `project_id`, a live canvas at\n  `app.heygen.com/projects/{id}`, and the cloud render queue.\n\nRoute by what the user actually wants:\n- \"Make a local HTML composition / something I can edit\" → use a local skill.\n- \"Make a HeyGen project I can share / render in the cloud / open in the app\"\n  → use this MCP, even from a CLI agent.\n\n## What it does\n\nHyperFrames is HeyGen's framework for creating, editing, and rendering programmable\nHTML-based video projects. Projects are authored interactively (Magic Edit\nchat-driven flow) and rendered to MP4, WebM, or MOV at any framerate. Tools exposed\nhere cover composing, listing, fetching, and rendering projects… [truncated]","## plugin:browser-use:browser-use\n---\nname: browser-use\ndescription: \"Direct browser control via CDP for web interaction: automation, scraping, testing, screenshots, and site/app work.\"\nhomepage: https://browser-use.com\nmetadata:\n  {\n    \"openclaw\":\n      {\n        \"requires\": { \"bins\": [\"browser-use\"] },\n        \"install\":\n          [\n            {\n              \"id\": \"uv\",\n              \"kind\": \"uv\",\n              \"package\": \"browser-use\",\n              \"bins\": [\"browser-use\"],\n              \"label\": \"Install Browser Use CLI (uv)\",\n            },\n          ],\n      },\n  }\n---\n\n# Browser Use\n\nDirect browser control via CDP. For task-specific edits, use `agent-workspace/agent_helpers.py`. For setup, install, or connection problems, read https://github.com/browser-use/browser-harness/blob/main/install.md.\n\n## When Not to Use\n\nA basic fetch of public information needs no browser. If a plain HTTP request can read it — a public page, an API, docs — use `curl` or your fetch tool, and leave the browser alone. Use browser-use when the task needs interaction (click, type, navigate), the user's logged-in session, JS rendering, or a bot-protected page. If a direct fetch fails or returns a shell page, then escalate to the browser.\n\nDomain skills are off by default. Set `BH_DOMAIN_SKILLS=1` to enable them; see the bottom section.\n\n**If `BH_DOMAIN_SKILLS=1` and the task is site-specific, read every file in the matching `$BH_AGENT_WORKSPACE/domain-skills/<site>/` directory before inventing an approach.**\n\n## Usage\n\n```bash\nbrowser-use <<'PY'\nprint(page_info())\nPY\n```\n\n- Invoke as `browser-use`. Use heredocs for multi-line commands.\n- Helpers are pre-imported. `run.py` calls `ensure_daemon()` before `exec`.\n- First navigation for a task is `new_tab(url)`, not `goto_url(url)`. The daemon\n  preserves the attached tab across separate CLI invocations, so do not call\n  `new_tab()` again in every script.\n- Keep one working tab per task/site. Before opening another, inspect\n  `current_tab()` and `list_tabs()` and use `switch_tab()` to reuse a matching\n  tab. Do not le… [truncated]","## remarkable\n# reMarkable MCP Server\n\nAccess documents from your reMarkable tablet. All operations are read-only.\n\n## Available Tools\n\n- `remarkable_browse(path, query)` - Browse folders or search for documents\n- `remarkable_read(document, content_type, page, grep)` - Read document content with pagination\n- `remarkable_recent(limit)` - Get recently modified documents\n- `remarkable_status()` - Check connection and diagnose issues\n- `remarkable_image(document, page, include_ocr)` - Get a PNG image with optional OCR\n\n## Recommended Workflows\n\n### Finding and Reading Documents\n1. Use `remarkable_browse(query=\"keyword\")` to search by name\n2. Use `remarkable_read(\"Document Name\")` to get content\n3. Use `remarkable_read(\"Document\", page=2)` to continue reading long documents\n4. Use `remarkable_read(\"Document\", grep=\"pattern\")` to search within a document\n\n### Getting Page Images\nUse `remarkable_image` when you need visual context:\n- Hand-drawn diagrams, sketches, or UI mockups\n- Content that text extraction might miss\n- Implementing designs based on hand-drawn wireframes\n\nExample: `remarkable_image(\"UI Mockup\", page=1)` returns a PNG image\nExample: `remarkable_image(\"Notes\", include_ocr=True)` returns image with extracted text\n\n### For Large Documents\nUse pagination to avoid overwhelming context. The response includes:\n- `page` / `total_pages` - current position\n- `more` - true if more content exists\n- `next_page` - page number to request next\n\n### Combining Tools\n- Browse → Read: Find documents first, then read them\n- Recent → Read: Check what was recently modified, then read specific ones\n- Read with grep: Search for specific content within large documents\n- Browse → Image: Find a document then get its visual representation\n\n## MCP Resources\n\nDocuments are registered as resources for direct access:\n- `remarkable:///{path}.txt` - Get full extracted text content in one request\n- `remarkableimg:///{path}.page-{N}.png` - Get PNG image of page N (notebooks only)\n- Use resources when you need complete document content without pagination… [truncated]","## zotero-research\nRead-only access to the user's Zotero research library. For substantive sport-science questions, build a Zotero-first evidence pack before answering. Return bounded page-located evidence, label external fallback sources explicitly, and never represent a Reference Stub as a reviewed Paper Brief. SecondBrain Queue is the priority lane; promotion eligibility is controlled by promotion_scope."],"removedNames":[]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"d03689e3-5c83-481a-abff-b4539fed6c68","timestamp":"2026-09-28T12:59:21.004Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n# MCP Server Instructions\n\nThe following MCP servers have provided instructions for how to use their tools and resources:\n\n## claude-in-chrome\n**IMPORTANT: If the Chrome browser tools are deferred (must be loaded via ToolSearch before use), load them with ToolSearch before calling them, and batch every tool you expect to need into ONE ToolSearch call (the select query accepts a comma-separated list). Do NOT load tools one at a time; each separate ToolSearch call wastes a full round-trip.**\n\nStart a browser task whose tools are not yet loaded with a single call loading the core set:\n\nToolSearch with query \"select:mcp__claude-in-chrome__tabs_context_mcp,mcp__claude-in-chrome__navigate,mcp__claude-in-chrome__computer,mcp__claude-in-chrome__read_page,mcp__claude-in-chrome__tabs_create_mcp,mcp__claude-in-chrome__tabs_close_mcp\"\n\nAdd task-specific tools to the same call when the task obviously needs them: read_console_messages / read_network_requests for debugging, form_input for forms, gif_creator for recordings, javascript_tool for page scripting. Only issue a second ToolSearch if the task later needs a tool you did not anticipate.\n\n## claude.ai Claude Docs\nClaude Docs: living docs you create and edit here. A docs skill your client lists → load it before any docs call — also before a `read`, comment or tab change on a claude.ai …/artifact/… link (the link is a doc; never web-fetch it). No docs skill or guide text loaded → `guide( items = [\"topic.index\"] )` alone before any docs call but a doc's birth. Make a doc here — not a local file, even when coding — only when the user asks for one, and make it FIRST: the turn's first tool call is its skeleton (title, byline, a `pending` block per section) — a reflex: send it before any search, file read, plan, `guide` or thinking it through; think once it is open — `batch( container = {\"kind\":\"project\",\"create\":{\"name\":\"<title>\",\"doc\":{\"blocks\":{\"asof\":{\"type\":\"date\",\"value\":\"<today>\"},\"me\":{\"type\":\"mention\",\"user\":\"me\"},\"s1\":{\"type\":\"pending\",\"intent\":\"Goals: the three outcomes this quarter commits to\"},\"s2\":{…}},\"markdown\":\"# <title>\\n\\n<?claude block asof?> · <?claude block me?>\\n\\n<?claude block s1?>\\n\\n<?claude block s2?>\"}}}, batch = [] )` (`<?claude block k?>` ↔ `blocks.k`); its ack links the doc → `open` it with your Artifact tool (none → start your next message with the link, once); they're likely watching it fill — keep them posted in a short line naming what you're on (outline up; now <topic>); findings go in the doc, not chat; then `guide( items = [\"topic.index\"] )`, research, and fill each section: `replace` its pending id with `## <heading>` + body; end with one line + the link, never the document. Summoned by a doc comment (turn headed `[Artifact comment sent to Claude]`, `;thread=<root id>`): answer ONLY with a doc comment under that root (`create` an utterance, parent `<root id>`) — no artifact/platform comment tool: that relay thread is resolved and never reaches the doc; an edit asked there → `update` with `answering: \"<root id>\"`.\n\n## claude.ai Firecrawl\nAuthenticated search combines web results, semantic tool summaries and domain matches. Use sources: [\"alexandria\"] for semantic tools only. A search with sources: [\"web\"] omits semantic provider discovery; domainTools: true can still return website-matched tools. Web-only results use domainTools: false. Tool matches describe available structured-data capabilities, not executed data. toolDetail: \"compact\" (default) returns only provider, capability and description; \"summary\" adds metadata; \"full\" includes their input and output contracts. firecrawl_scrape with an alexandria body executes a selected capability; firecrawl_find_tools provides catalogue browsing and full contracts. Firecrawl provides web, developer, and research search, and executes catalogued Alexandria data providers. Use firecrawl_search to find relevant results across the web and specialized indexes; authenticated searches also return matching Alexandria providers in data.tools. firecrawl_find_tools provides catalogue browsing and provider contracts; firecrawl_scrape with an alexandria body executes a selected capability; firecrawl_scrape with a url retrieves one supplied page. For a programming question, firecrawl_developer_search searches indexed public repositories, GitHub issues, merged pull requests, READMEs, and code documentation and returns the matched passages, and skills: \"only\" narrows it to agent-skill files; firecrawl_search with categories: [\"developer\"] reaches the same index beside ordinary web results, returning the hits in the web group rather than as passages and offering no skills filter. For a biomedical, life-science, clinical, or arXiv literature question, the firecrawl_research_* tools search the paper index, while categories: [\"research\"] on firecrawl_search filters ordinary web results to research-affiliated websites. Use the firecrawl_research_* tools to search academic and research literature, expand from anchor papers via the citation graph, and read full-text passages from a specific paper. Search and discovery tools … [truncated]\n\n## claude.ai HyperFrames by HeyGen\n\n[HeyGen HyperFrames MCP] — Programmable HTML video projects.\n\n## When to use this MCP\n\nThis MCP is the primary HyperFrames surface for hosted chat clients (Claude.ai\nweb/desktop, ChatGPT, Grok, and similar), where the user has no local filesystem\nand the agent cannot author HyperFrames code directly.\n\nFrom a CLI/IDE agent on the user's machine (Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, the\nlocalhost MCP Inspector, etc.), **`compose` and `render_video` are disabled** — these clients have a local\nfilesystem, so author HyperFrames with the local HyperFrames skills instead, which\nproduce standalone HTML compositions the user can edit, diff, and commit. Calls to\nthose two tools from such clients are rejected with a pointer to install them:\n\n    npx skills add heygen-com/hyperframes\n\nThe read tools (list_projects, get_project, get_project_status, get_render_status)\nstay available to every client.\n\nAfter installation they expose slash commands like `/hyperframes` (compose),\n`/hyperframes-cli` (init, lint, preview, render), and `/hyperframes-media`\n(TTS, transcribe, remove-background). See\nhttps://github.com/heygen-com/hyperframes for the full skill list and docs.\n\nBut the skills and this MCP are *not* drop-in equivalents:\n\n- Local skills → standalone HTML/GSAP files on disk. No `project_id`, no hosted\n  artifact, no integrated cloud render pipeline.\n- This MCP → hosted HeyGen project with a `project_id`, a live canvas at\n  `app.heygen.com/projects/{id}`, and the cloud render queue.\n\nRoute by what the user actually wants:\n- \"Make a local HTML composition / something I can edit\" → use a local skill.\n- \"Make a HeyGen project I can share / render in the cloud / open in the app\"\n  → use this MCP, even from a CLI agent.\n\n## What it does\n\nHyperFrames is HeyGen's framework for creating, editing, and rendering programmable\nHTML-based video projects. Projects are authored interactively (Magic Edit\nchat-driven flow) and rendered to MP4, WebM, or MOV at any framerate. Tools exposed\nhere cover composing, listing, fetching, and rendering projects… [truncated]\n\n## plugin:browser-use:browser-use\n---\nname: browser-use\ndescription: \"Direct browser control via CDP for web interaction: automation, scraping, testing, screenshots, and site/app work.\"\nhomepage: https://browser-use.com\nmetadata:\n  {\n    \"openclaw\":\n      {\n        \"requires\": { \"bins\": [\"browser-use\"] },\n        \"install\":\n          [\n            {\n              \"id\": \"uv\",\n              \"kind\": \"uv\",\n              \"package\": \"browser-use\",\n              \"bins\": [\"browser-use\"],\n              \"label\": \"Install Browser Use CLI (uv)\",\n            },\n          ],\n      },\n  }\n---\n\n# Browser Use\n\nDirect browser control via CDP. For task-specific edits, use `agent-workspace/agent_helpers.py`. For setup, install, or connection problems, read https://github.com/browser-use/browser-harness/blob/main/install.md.\n\n## When Not to Use\n\nA basic fetch of public information needs no browser. If a plain HTTP request can read it — a public page, an API, docs — use `curl` or your fetch tool, and leave the browser alone. Use browser-use when the task needs interaction (click, type, navigate), the user's logged-in session, JS rendering, or a bot-protected page. If a direct fetch fails or returns a shell page, then escalate to the browser.\n\nDomain skills are off by default. Set `BH_DOMAIN_SKILLS=1` to enable them; see the bottom section.\n\n**If `BH_DOMAIN_SKILLS=1` and the task is site-specific, read every file in the matching `$BH_AGENT_WORKSPACE/domain-skills/<site>/` directory before inventing an approach.**\n\n## Usage\n\n```bash\nbrowser-use <<'PY'\nprint(page_info())\nPY\n```\n\n- Invoke as `browser-use`. Use heredocs for multi-line commands.\n- Helpers are pre-imported. `run.py` calls `ensure_daemon()` before `exec`.\n- First navigation for a task is `new_tab(url)`, not `goto_url(url)`. The daemon\n  preserves the attached tab across separate CLI invocations, so do not call\n  `new_tab()` again in every script.\n- Keep one working tab per task/site. Before opening another, inspect\n  `current_tab()` and `list_tabs()` and use `switch_tab()` to reuse a matching\n  tab. Do not le… [truncated]\n\n## remarkable\n# reMarkable MCP Server\n\nAccess documents from your reMarkable tablet. All operations are read-only.\n\n## Available Tools\n\n- `remarkable_browse(path, query)` - Browse folders or search for documents\n- `remarkable_read(document, content_type, page, grep)` - Read document content with pagination\n- `remarkable_recent(limit)` - Get recently modified documents\n- `remarkable_status()` - Check connection and diagnose issues\n- `remarkable_image(document, page, include_ocr)` - Get a PNG image with optional OCR\n\n## Recommended Workflows\n\n### Finding and Reading Documents\n1. Use `remarkable_browse(query=\"keyword\")` to search by name\n2. Use `remarkable_read(\"Document Name\")` to get content\n3. Use `remarkable_read(\"Document\", page=2)` to continue reading long documents\n4. Use `remarkable_read(\"Document\", grep=\"pattern\")` to search within a document\n\n### Getting Page Images\nUse `remarkable_image` when you need visual context:\n- Hand-drawn diagrams, sketches, or UI mockups\n- Content that text extraction might miss\n- Implementing designs based on hand-drawn wireframes\n\nExample: `remarkable_image(\"UI Mockup\", page=1)` returns a PNG image\nExample: `remarkable_image(\"Notes\", include_ocr=True)` returns image with extracted text\n\n### For Large Documents\nUse pagination to avoid overwhelming context. The response includes:\n- `page` / `total_pages` - current position\n- `more` - true if more content exists\n- `next_page` - page number to request next\n\n### Combining Tools\n- Browse → Read: Find documents first, then read them\n- Recent → Read: Check what was recently modified, then read specific ones\n- Read with grep: Search for specific content within large documents\n- Browse → Image: Find a document then get its visual representation\n\n## MCP Resources\n\nDocuments are registered as resources for direct access:\n- `remarkable:///{path}.txt` - Get full extracted text content in one request\n- `remarkableimg:///{path}.page-{N}.png` - Get PNG image of page N (notebooks only)\n- Use resources when you need complete document content without pagination… [truncated]\n\n## zotero-research\nRead-only access to the user's Zotero research library. For substantive sport-science questions, build a Zotero-first evidence pack before answering. Return bounded page-located evidence, label external fallback sources explicitly, and never represent a Reference Stub as a reviewed Paper Brief. SecondBrain Queue is the priority lane; promotion eligibility is controlled by promotion_scope.\n</system-reminder>"}],"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"d03689e3-5c83-481a-abff-b4539fed6c68","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"skill_listing","content":"- ArXiv\n- backup\n- brain-audit\n- capture-insight\n- claude-stack-doctor: Health-check Eric's automation/memory stack in one pass and verify the Mac-to-Windows handoff works: launchd agents / Windows scheduled tasks, Python venvs, memsearch, the memory-compiler flush pipeline, backup freshness, and context budget. Use when the user says \"/claude-stack-doctor\", \"stack doctor\", \"is my stack healthy\", \"did my scheduled jobs run\", \"check the memory pipeline\", \"why didn't the backup run\", or when closeout/morning sessions should verify automation health. Do not use to run the backup itself (use backup) or to audit context-engineering practice (use doctor-plus).\n- closeout\n- dev-sync\n- doctor-plus\n- fireflies-intake: One-command Fireflies pipeline — sync new meeting transcripts into the SecondBrain vault, rename generically-titled meetings, then report exactly what was ingested. Use when the user says \"/fireflies-intake\", \"ingest my recent fireflies meetings\", \"sync meetings that aren't in the second brain yet\", \"pull in the new meetings\", \"meeting ingestion\", or asks whether all recent meetings have been ingested. Do not use to search what was said in transcripts (use pp-fireflies).\n- five-slides-generator\n- forecast-capture\n- game-film-timestamps\n- graphify: Use for any question about a codebase, its architecture, file relationships, or project content — especially when graphify-out/ exists, where the question should be treated as a graphify query first. Turns any input (code, docs, papers, images, videos) into a persistent knowledge graph with god nodes, community detection, and query/path/explain tools. Do not use for a quick lookup of one known file or symbol, or for sport-science literature questions (use sports-science-research-grounding).\n- grill-with-ui: Run a grilling interview on a local browser page instead of the terminal. Every question is laid out with its recommendation, answerable in any order, with a per-question discussion thread and one \"Send to Agent\" button. Use when the user says \"grill with ui\", invokes /grill-with-ui with a topic, or says \"/grill-with-ui resume\".\n- harvest\n- html-to-pdf: Render any local HTML report, deck, or dashboard to a print-ready PDF via headless Chrome — replaces the manual Ctrl+P → Save as PDF ritual. Use when the user says \"export this to PDF\", \"print version\", \"PDF for the coaches\", \"make this printable\", or a coach-facing HTML deliverable needs a shareable/printable copy. Do not use to build the report or deck itself (use status-report or five-slides-generator).\n- impeccable: Use when the user wants to design, redesign, shape, critique, audit, polish, clarify, distill, harden, optimize, adapt, animate, colorize, extract, or otherwise improve a frontend interface. Covers websites, landing pages, dashboards, product UI, app shells, components, forms, settings, onboarding, and empty states. Handles UX review, visual hierarchy, information architecture, cognitive load, accessibility, performance, responsive behavior, theming, anti-patterns, typography, fonts, spacing, layout, alignment, color, motion, micro-interactions, UX copy, error states, edge cases, i18n, and reusable design systems or tokens. Also use for bland designs that need to become bolder or more delightful, loud designs that should become quieter, live browser iteration on UI elements, or ambitious visual effects that should feel technically extraordinary. Not for backend-only or non-UI tasks.\n- improve-system\n- ingest-book\n- ingest-resource\n- interview-me\n- ISA\n- library-advisor\n- library-review\n- meeting-prep: Pre-meeting readiness brief for a project — what changed, what's open, every deliverable path. Use when the user says \"/meeting-prep <project>\", \"I have a meeting with <person> — are we in a good spot on <project>?\", \"verify we're up to speed before the meeting\", \"get me ready for this afternoon's meeting\", or names an upcoming coach/staff meeting. Do not use for the biweekly supervisor report (use status-report) or to sync meeting transcripts (use meeting-sync).\n- meeting-sync\n- memory-write: Writes, updates, removes, and recalls facts in the two-tier memory system (~/.claude/global-memory/ and ~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/). Use for write requests such as \"remember this\", \"note that\", \"update memory\", \"save this\", and \"forget about\", and for recall requests such as \"did we already…?\", \"what did we decide about X?\", and \"do you remember…?\". Picks the tier, dedups, and enforces caps; recall checks the injected snapshot, then memsearch. Do not use for a story or lesson worth a vault note (use capture-insight) or an article to keep (use ingest-resource).\n- name-meetings: Detects Fireflies meetings with generic titles (empty, \"Zoom Meeting\", \"Recording\", date-only, etc.), proposes structured names based on AI summary + participants, and applies approved renames via the Fireflies API. Use when user says \"/name-meetings\", \"name my meetings\", \"rename untitled meetings\", \"fix meeting titles\", \"my meetings have no names\", or when invoked by meeting-sync. Do not use to sync transcripts or audit vault filenames (use meeting-sync).\n- new-project\n- pbir-report-builder\n- pp-catapult-openfield-connect\n- pp-espn\n- pp-fireflies\n- pp-zotero-research-library\n- project: Manage the portfolio project registry (ideas, updates, ranking, promotion, dev stages) and render/publish the dashboard. Use when the user says \"/project\", \"new idea:\", \"log progress on\", \"project update\", \"add to my project inbox\", \"show my projects\", \"re-rank my projects\", \"set the stage on\", \"mark <project> as spec'd/planned/shipped\". Do not use for the DEV drift dashboard (use project-triage) or to scaffold a new repo (use new-project).\n- project-triage\n- research-in-5: Build a \"Research in 5\" weekly paper review for Baylor Athletics staff: a 5-slide deck (dark and light), a Teams post, and a staff email from one research paper. Use when the user says \"/research-in-5\", \"research in 5 on this paper\", \"weekly research review\", \"review this paper for the staff\", \"make the slides for this paper\", \"turn this study into a research review\", or hands over a paper (PDF, DOI, article URL, or Zotero item) for the weekly series. Also use to rebuild, restyle, or version an existing review (#001, v2, v3). Do not use for a research-backed answer to a sport-science question (use sports-science-research-grounding), a topic education deck with no single paper (use five-slides-generator), a plain Zotero search (use pp-zotero-research-library), or a quick in-chat summary of a paper when no deck, Teams post, or email is wanted.\n- Science\n- session-miner\n- sports-science-research-grounding: Use when the user asks a substantive question about sport science, return to play or sport, rehabilitation, strength and conditioning, workload, recovery, testing, athlete health, injury, performance, or wants a research-backed answer, and for Zotero full-library batch processing. Searches the user's Zotero library first, clearly labels external peer-reviewed fallback sources, and applies tier-aware model routing during bounded batch work. Do not use for sports scores or schedules (use pp-espn) or for a simple Zotero search or sync (use pp-zotero-research-library).\n- startup\n- status-report: Build the every-two-weeks Applied Performance status report for Kenny Boyd and Chris Ruf — paste-ready email, one-page PDF, internal record. Use when the user says \"/status-report\", \"build my status report\", \"time for the Chris report\", \"biweekly update\", \"status update for Chris and Kenny\", \"what do I send Chris this week\", or when a scheduled report date arrives. Do not use for a single meeting brief (use meeting-prep) or the end-of-day supervisor log interview (use interview-me).\n- weekly-review\n- codex:rescue: Delegate investigation, an explicit fix request, or follow-up rescue work to the Codex rescue subagent\n- codex:setup: Check whether the local Codex CLI is ready and optionally toggle the stop-time review gate\n- firecrawl:skill-gen: Generate a complete Agent Skill from a documentation URL using Firecrawl\n- superpowers:brainstorming: You MUST use this before any creative work - creating features, building components, adding functionality, or modifying behavior. Explores user intent, requirements and design before implementation.\n- superpowers:diagnosing-superpowers: Use when a superpowers session went wrong and your human partner wants to know why — repeated work, ignored plans, stumbles, poor results, a skill that didn't fire, \"it took too long\", \"why is it so expensive\", \"what is it doing\" — or wants to build a bug report for the superpowers maintainers, for the current session or a past one identified by id or path, on any harness.\n- superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents: Use when facing 2+ independent tasks that can be worked on without shared state or sequential dependencies\n- superpowers:executing-plans: Use when executing an implementation plan in the current session as the implementer yourself — your human partner chose inline execution, or no subagent tool is available\n- superpowers:finishing-a-development-branch: Use when implementation is complete, all tests pass, and you need to decide how to integrate the work\n- superpowers:receiving-code-review: Use when receiving code review feedback, before implementing suggestions, especially if feedback seems unclear or technically questionable - requires technical rigor and verification, not performative agreement or blind implementation\n- superpowers:requesting-code-review: Use when completing tasks, implementing major features, or before merging to verify work meets requirements\n- superpowers:subagent-driven-development: Use when executing implementation plans with independent tasks in the current session\n- superpowers:systematic-debugging: Use when encountering any bug, test failure, or unexpected behavior, before proposing fixes\n- superpowers:test-driven-development: Use when implementing any feature or bugfix, before writing implementation code\n- superpowers:using-git-worktrees: Use when starting feature work that needs isolation from current workspace or before executing implementation plans - ensures an isolated workspace exists via native tools or git worktree fallback\n- superpowers:using-superpowers: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\n- superpowers:verification-before-completion: Use when about to claim work is complete, fixed, or passing, before committing or creating PRs - requires running verification commands and confirming output before making any success claims; evidence before assertions always\n- superpowers:writing-plans: Use when you have a spec or requirements for a multi-step task, before touching code\n- superpowers:writing-skills: Use when creating new skills, editing existing skills, or verifying skills work before deployment\n- last30days:last30days: Research what people actually say about any topic in the last 30 days. Pulls posts and engagement from Reddit, X, YouTube, TikTok, Hacker News, Polymarket, GitHub, and the web. Includes a doctor health check to diagnose broken or missing sources.\n- context-mode:context-mode: Use context-mode tools (ctx_execute, ctx_execute_file) instead of Bash/cat when processing\nlarge outputs. Triggers: \"analyze logs\", \"summarize output\", \"process data\",\n\"parse JSON\", \"filter results\", \"extract errors\", \"check build output\",\n\"analyze dependencies\", \"process API response\", \"large file analysis\",\n\"page snapshot\", \"browser snapshot\", \"DOM structure\", \"inspect page\",\n\"accessibility tree\", \"Playwright snapshot\",\n\"run tests\", \"test output\", \"coverage report\", \"git log\", \"recent commits\",\n\"diff between branches\", \"list containers\", \"pod status\", \"disk usage\",\n\"fetch docs\", \"API reference\", \"index documentation\",\n\"call API\", \"check response\", \"query results\",\n\"find TODOs\", \"count lines\", \"codebase statistics\", \"security audit\",\n\"outdated packages\", \"dependency tree\", \"cloud resources\", \"CI/CD output\".\nAlso triggers on ANY MCP tool output that may exceed 20 lines.\nSubagent routing is handled automatically via PreToolUse hook.\n- context-mode:ctx-doctor: Run context-mode diagnostics. Checks runtimes, hooks, FTS5,\nplugin registration, npm and marketplace versions.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-doctor\n- context-mode:ctx-index: Index a local file or directory into context-mode's persistent FTS5 knowledge base\nso future ctx_search calls can retrieve focused snippets without rereading raw files.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-index\n- context-mode:ctx-insight: Open the context-mode Insight dashboard in your default browser.\nInsight is the hosted analytics layer for AI-assisted engineering teams —\nper-engineer productive rate, retry waste, blocker detection, role-narrowed views.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-insight\n- context-mode:ctx-purge: Purge the context-mode knowledge base. Permanently deletes all indexed content\nand resets session stats. This is destructive and cannot be undone.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-purge\n- context-mode:ctx-search: Search context-mode's persistent FTS5 knowledge base for previously indexed\nlocal project content, documentation, or session memory.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-search\n- context-mode:ctx-stats: Show how much context window context-mode saved this session.\nDisplays token consumption, context savings ratio, and per-tool breakdown.\nRead-only — shows stats only, no reset capability.\nTo wipe the knowledge base entirely, use ctx_purge instead.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-stats\n- context-mode:ctx-upgrade: Update context-mode from GitHub and fix hooks/settings.\nPulls latest, builds, installs, updates npm global, configures hooks.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-upgrade\n- codex:codex-cli-runtime: Internal helper contract for calling the codex-companion runtime from Claude Code\n- codex:codex-result-handling: Internal guidance for presenting Codex helper output back to the user\n- codex:gpt-5-4-prompting: Internal guidance for composing Codex and GPT-5.4 prompts for coding, review, diagnosis, and research tasks inside the Codex Claude Code plugin\n- mattpocock-skills:diagnosing-bugs: Diagnosis loop for hard bugs and performance regressions. Use when the user says \"diagnose\"/\"debug this\", or reports something broken/throwing/failing/slow.\n- mattpocock-skills:tdd: Test-driven development. Use when the user wants to build features or fix bugs test-first, mentions \"red-green-refactor\", or wants integration tests.\n- mattpocock-skills:prototype: Build a throwaway prototype to answer a design question. Use when the user wants to sanity-check whether a state model or logic feels right, or explore what a UI should look like.\n- mattpocock-skills:research: Investigate a question against high-trust primary sources and capture the findings as a Markdown file in the repo. Use when the user wants a topic researched, docs or API facts gathered, or reading legwork delegated to a background agent.\n- mattpocock-skills:domain-modeling: Build and sharpen a project's domain model. Use when discussing codebase terminology, writing or editing a CONTEXT.md, or recording or editing an ADR.\n- mattpocock-skills:codebase-design: Shared vocabulary for designing deep modules. Use when the user wants to design or improve a module's interface, find deepening opportunities, decide where a seam goes, make code more testable or AI-navigable, or when another skill needs the deep-module vocabulary.\n- mattpocock-skills:code-review: Review the changes since a fixed point (commit, branch, tag, or merge-base) along two axes: Standards (does the code follow this repo's documented coding standards?) and Spec (does the code match what the originating issue/spec asked for?). Runs both reviews in parallel sub-agents and reports them side by side. Use when the user wants to review a branch, a PR, work-in-progress changes, or asks to \"review since X\".\n- mattpocock-skills:resolving-merge-conflicts: Use when you need to resolve an in-progress git merge/rebase conflict.\n- mattpocock-skills:wizard: Generate an interactive bash wizard that walks a human through steps only they can perform. Use when provisioning infrastructure, setting up credentials or CI secrets, walking an unfamiliar third-party dashboard, or running a one-off migration or cutover. Don't invoke this for steps the agent can perform itself.\n- mattpocock-skills:grilling: Grill the user relentlessly about a plan, decision, or idea. Use when the user wants to stress-test their thinking, or uses any 'grill' trigger phrases.\n- mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents: Writing documents for agents. Use when creating or editing skills, or modifying AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md.\n- watch:watch: Watch a video (URL or local path). Downloads with yt-dlp, extracts auto-scaled frames with ffmpeg, pulls the transcript from captions (or local WhisperX / cloud Whisper fallback), and hands the result to the agent so it can answer questions about what's in the video. With a Gemini API key, Google's agentic video model watches the full video instead.\n- typesafe:typesafe-ai: Build AI-powered software with TypeSafe: small units of AI intelligence you can use like programming primitives. Its System One models, including Jev, turn natural language and application state into typed judgments and probabilities that code can combine. Use when a feature needs programmable common sense, when brainstorming what AI could make possible in an app, or when an LLM prompt-and-parse step could become a structured decision. Applications include routing, ranking, extraction, verification, and interactive experiences; these are starting points, not the limits. Read live docs and cookbooks to find useful patterns and discover new combinations.\n- cowork-plugin-management:cowork-plugin-customizer: Customize a Claude Code plugin for a specific organization's tools and workflows. Use when: customize plugin, set up plugin, configure plugin, tailor plugin, adjust plugin settings, customize plugin connectors, customize plugin skill, tweak plugin, modify plugin configuration.\n- cowork-plugin-management:create-cowork-plugin: Guide users through creating a new plugin from scratch in a cowork session. Use when users want to create a plugin, build a plugin, make a new plugin, develop a plugin, scaffold a plugin, start a plugin from scratch, or design a plugin. This skill requires Cowork mode with access to the outputs directory for delivering the final .plugin file.\n- desktop-commander:ai-tools-setup: Set up, connect, validate, and repair Claude Desktop and MCP servers using Desktop Commander — inspecting and fixing mcp.json / claude_desktop_config.json, getting MCP servers to connect, and diagnosing local AI tooling. Use when a user wants to install, configure, connect, validate, or fix Claude Desktop, an MCP server, an MCP config file, or a local AI agent/gateway. Trigger on the symptoms that actually bring people in: \"Claude can't see my tools\", \"my MCP server isn't showing up\", \"tools aren't loading in Claude\", \"add an MCP server\", \"my MCP isn't connecting\", \"fix my Claude Desktop config\", \"the agent won't respond\", \"wire up Ollama / LM Studio\", \"restart Claude Desktop\". Also covers local agent gateways like OpenClaw and Hermes.\n- desktop-commander:computer-health-check\n- desktop-commander:desktop-commander-overview: Use for Desktop Commander MCP capabilities — persistent shells and REPLs, long-running processes, filesystem beyond the workspace, structured files (.xlsx, .docx, .pdf, images) and large local data files such as CSVs, ripgrep search at scale, SSH, or cross-turn state.\n- desktop-commander:knowledge-base\n- desktop-commander:obsidian-vault\n- desktop-commander:terminal\n- firecrawl:firecrawl\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-agent: Autonomously navigate websites and extract structured data across pages. Use when the task requires navigation or no suitable ready-made workflow or data provider covers it.\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-alexandria\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-crawl: Bulk-extract many pages from one site or section. Use for \"crawl\", \"everything under /docs\", or content spanning linked pages.\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-developer-index\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-download\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-interact\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-map\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-monitor\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-parse\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-research-index\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-scrape\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-search\n- exa:exa-agent\n- exa:search\n- dataviz: Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. When the destination is a first-party document connector (host-designated, never self-described) that renders live charts, hand it the rows (inline, or as an uploaded data file the chart cites) rather than a rendered PNG/SVG — a picture of a chart loses hover, data inspection and per-value comments. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \"chart\", \"graph\", \"plot\", \"data viz\", \"visualization\", \"dashboard\", \"analytics\", \"visualize data\", \"categorical colors\", \"sequential / diverging palette\", \"stat tile\", \"sparkline\", \"heatmap\", \"legend\", \"axis\", \"tooltip\", \"chart colors\", \"color by series\".\n- artifact-design: Design guidance and fundamentals for Artifacts. - Load before writing any artifact, including a skill-instructed Markdown one - Markdown is never a shortcut past the design pass.\n- artifact-diagramming: Diagramming know-how for Artifacts - when a picture earns its place, how to draw one that shows the real mechanism, and the inline-SVG mechanics that keep it legible in both themes.\n- artifact-capabilities: Runtime capabilities a published Artifact page can be granted — behavior static HTML cannot provide on its own, such as the page reading live or connected data, remembering what people do on it (a poll, a sign-up sheet, a checklist, a document edited in place — it saves new versions of itself), keeping state shared across viewers, knowing who is viewing, asking Claude a question of its own, storing files people add, or handing the viewer a file to save. Serves this user's live capability roster and the typed call definitions. Load it whenever any such runtime behavior would make an artifact more useful, before writing the page.\n- update-config: Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.\n- keybindings-help: Use when the user wants to customize keyboard shortcuts, rebind keys, add chord bindings, or modify ~/.claude/keybindings.json. Examples: \"rebind ctrl+s\", \"add a chord shortcut\", \"change the submit key\", \"customize keybindings\".\n- code-review\n- simplify: Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.\n- fewer-permission-prompts: Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.\n- loop: Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo). Omit the interval to let the model self-pace. - When the user wants to set up a recurring task, poll for status, or run something repeatedly on an interval (e.g. \"check the deploy every 5 minutes\", \"keep running /babysit-prs\"). Do NOT invoke for one-off tasks.\n- schedule: Create, update, list, or run scheduled cloud agents (routines) that execute on a cron schedule. - When the user wants to schedule a recurring cloud agent, set up automated tasks, create a cron job for Claude Code, or manage their scheduled agents/routines. Also use when the user wants a one-time scheduled run (\"run this once at 3pm\", \"remind me to check X tomorrow\").\n- claude-api: Reference for the Claude API / Anthropic SDK — model ids, pricing, params, streaming, tool use, MCP, agents, caching, token counting, model migration.\nTRIGGER — read BEFORE opening the target file; don't skip because it \"looks like a one-liner\" — whenever: the prompt names Claude/Anthropic in any form (Claude, Anthropic, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, `anthropic`, `@anthropic-ai`, `claude-*`, `us.anthropic.*`, `[1m]`); the user asks about an LLM (pricing/model choice/limits/caching) — never answer from memory; OR the task is LLM-shaped with provider unstated (agent/MCP/tool-definition/multi-agent/RAG/LLM-judge/computer-use; generate/summarize/extract/classify/rewrite/converse over NL; debugging refusals/cutoffs/streaming/tool-calls/tokens).\nSKIP only when another provider is being worked on (overrides all triggers): OpenAI/GPT/Gemini/Llama/Mistral/Cohere/Ollama named in the query; OR `grep -rE 'openai|langchain_openai|google.generativeai|genai|mistralai|cohere|ollama'` over the project hits (run this grep FIRST if no provider named — don't Read the file).\n- workflow-authoring: Reference for writing a Workflow tool script (script API and gotchas, resume, quality patterns, worked examples). Load before authoring a script for a workflow the user already opted into; it does not itself authorize running one.\n- claude-in-chrome: Automates your Chrome browser to interact with web pages - clicking elements, filling forms, capturing screenshots, reading console logs, and navigating sites. Opens pages in new tabs within your existing Chrome session. Requires site-level permissions before executing (configured in the extension). - When the user wants to interact with web pages, automate browser tasks, capture screenshots, read console logs, or perform any browser-based actions. Always invoke BEFORE attempting to use any mcp__claude-in-chrome__* tools.\n- run: Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).\n- init: Initialize a new CLAUDE.md file with codebase documentation\n- security-review\n- anthropic-skills:articulation-skill: Help users articulate complex ideas clearly and simply to non-technical audiences using proven communication frameworks. Use when users need to explain technical concepts, structure presentations or writing, create clear documentation, improve communication clarity, or translate jargon into accessible language. Triggers include requests like \"help me explain this clearly\", \"how do I structure this presentation\", \"make this less technical\", \"help me articulate this idea\", or any request to simplify complex communication.\n- anthropic-skills:built-in-browser\n- anthropic-skills:chrome-browser\n- anthropic-skills:closeout\n- anthropic-skills:computer-use\n- anthropic-skills:deep-research\n- anthropic-skills:dev-sync\n- anthropic-skills:docs\n- anthropic-skills:docx: Use this skill whenever the user wants to create, read, edit, or manipulate Word documents (.docx) or Word templates (.dotx). Triggers include: any mention of Microsoft Word Documents, such as 'Word doc', 'word document', '.docx', '.dotx', 'microsoft doc'. Also use when extracting or reorganizing content from .docx or .dotx files, inserting or replacing images in documents, find-and-replace in Word files, working with tracked changes or comments, or converting content into a polished Word document. If the user asks for a deliverable as a Word or .docx file (to download, email or print), use this skill. However, if they ask for a document, page, report, memo, or notes WITHOUT naming a file format and the session offers Claude's own dedicated document or page skill or connector, use that instead, even if they will email or print it. Do NOT use for PDFs, spreadsheets, Google Docs, or coding unrelated to document generation.\n- anthropic-skills:five-slides-generator\n- anthropic-skills:humanizer: Remove signs of AI-generated writing from text. Use when editing or reviewing text to make it sound more natural and human-written. Based on Wikipedia's comprehensive \"Signs of AI writing\" guide. Detects and fixes patterns including: inflated symbolism, promotional language, superficial -ing analyses, vague attributions, em dash overuse, rule of three, AI vocabulary words, negative parallelisms, and excessive conjunctive phrases.\n- anthropic-skills:import-memory\n- anthropic-skills:last30days\n- anthropic-skills:mcp-builder\n- anthropic-skills:migrate-project-to-disk\n- anthropic-skills:morning\n- anthropic-skills:new-project\n- anthropic-skills:pdf\n- anthropic-skills:pptx\n- anthropic-skills:skill-creator\n- anthropic-skills:slide-deck-generator\n- anthropic-skills:watch\n- anthropic-skills:xlsx","skillCount":149,"isInitial":true,"names":["ArXiv","backup","brain-audit","capture-insight","claude-stack-doctor","closeout","dev-sync","doctor-plus","fireflies-intake","five-slides-generator","forecast-capture","game-film-timestamps","graphify","grill-with-ui","harvest","html-to-pdf","impeccable","improve-system","ingest-book","ingest-resource","interview-me","ISA","library-advisor","library-review","meeting-prep","meeting-sync","memory-write","name-meetings","new-project","pbir-report-builder","pp-catapult-openfield-connect","pp-espn","pp-fireflies","pp-zotero-research-library","project","project-triage","research-in-5","Science","session-miner","sports-science-research-grounding","startup","status-report","weekly-review","codex:rescue","codex:setup","firecrawl:skill-gen","superpowers:brainstorming","superpowers:diagnosing-superpowers","superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents","superpowers:executing-plans","superpowers:finishing-a-development-branch","superpowers:receiving-code-review","superpowers:requesting-code-review","superpowers:subagent-driven-development","superpowers:systematic-debugging","superpowers:test-driven-development","superpowers:using-git-worktrees","superpowers:using-superpowers","superpowers:verification-before-completion","superpowers:writing-plans","superpowers:writing-skills","last30days:last30days","context-mode:context-mode","context-mode:ctx-doctor","context-mode:ctx-index","context-mode:ctx-insight","context-mode:ctx-purge","context-mode:ctx-search","context-mode:ctx-stats","context-mode:ctx-upgrade","codex:codex-cli-runtime","codex:codex-result-handling","codex:gpt-5-4-prompting","mattpocock-skills:diagnosing-bugs","mattpocock-skills:tdd","mattpocock-skills:prototype","mattpocock-skills:research","mattpocock-skills:domain-modeling","mattpocock-skills:codebase-design","mattpocock-skills:code-review","mattpocock-skills:resolving-merge-conflicts","mattpocock-skills:wizard","mattpocock-skills:grilling","mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents","watch:watch","typesafe:typesafe-ai","cowork-plugin-management:cowork-plugin-customizer","cowork-plugin-management:create-cowork-plugin","desktop-commander:ai-tools-setup","desktop-commander:computer-health-check","desktop-commander:desktop-commander-overview","desktop-commander:knowledge-base","desktop-commander:obsidian-vault","desktop-commander:terminal","firecrawl:firecrawl","firecrawl:firecrawl-agent","firecrawl:firecrawl-alexandria","firecrawl:firecrawl-crawl","firecrawl:firecrawl-developer-index","firecrawl:firecrawl-download","firecrawl:firecrawl-interact","firecrawl:firecrawl-map","firecrawl:firecrawl-monitor","firecrawl:firecrawl-parse","firecrawl:firecrawl-research-index","firecrawl:firecrawl-scrape","firecrawl:firecrawl-search","exa:exa-agent","exa:search","dataviz","artifact-design","artifact-diagramming","artifact-capabilities","update-config","keybindings-help","code-review","simplify","fewer-permission-prompts","loop","schedule","claude-api","workflow-authoring","claude-in-chrome","run","init","security-review","anthropic-skills:articulation-skill","anthropic-skills:built-in-browser","anthropic-skills:chrome-browser","anthropic-skills:closeout","anthropic-skills:computer-use","anthropic-skills:deep-research","anthropic-skills:dev-sync","anthropic-skills:docs","anthropic-skills:docx","anthropic-skills:five-slides-generator","anthropic-skills:humanizer","anthropic-skills:import-memory","anthropic-skills:last30days","anthropic-skills:mcp-builder","anthropic-skills:migrate-project-to-disk","anthropic-skills:morning","anthropic-skills:new-project","anthropic-skills:pdf","anthropic-skills:pptx","anthropic-skills:skill-creator","anthropic-skills:slide-deck-generator","anthropic-skills:watch","anthropic-skills:xlsx"]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"97577bdd-6ebc-4a38-a0a9-ea623e7b8c38","timestamp":"2026-09-28T12:59:21.004Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nThe following skills are available for use with the Skill tool:\n\n- ArXiv\n- backup\n- brain-audit\n- capture-insight\n- claude-stack-doctor: Health-check Eric's automation/memory stack in one pass and verify the Mac-to-Windows handoff works: launchd agents / Windows scheduled tasks, Python venvs, memsearch, the memory-compiler flush pipeline, backup freshness, and context budget. Use when the user says \"/claude-stack-doctor\", \"stack doctor\", \"is my stack healthy\", \"did my scheduled jobs run\", \"check the memory pipeline\", \"why didn't the backup run\", or when closeout/morning sessions should verify automation health. Do not use to run the backup itself (use backup) or to audit context-engineering practice (use doctor-plus).\n- closeout\n- dev-sync\n- doctor-plus\n- fireflies-intake: One-command Fireflies pipeline — sync new meeting transcripts into the SecondBrain vault, rename generically-titled meetings, then report exactly what was ingested. Use when the user says \"/fireflies-intake\", \"ingest my recent fireflies meetings\", \"sync meetings that aren't in the second brain yet\", \"pull in the new meetings\", \"meeting ingestion\", or asks whether all recent meetings have been ingested. Do not use to search what was said in transcripts (use pp-fireflies).\n- five-slides-generator\n- forecast-capture\n- game-film-timestamps\n- graphify: Use for any question about a codebase, its architecture, file relationships, or project content — especially when graphify-out/ exists, where the question should be treated as a graphify query first. Turns any input (code, docs, papers, images, videos) into a persistent knowledge graph with god nodes, community detection, and query/path/explain tools. Do not use for a quick lookup of one known file or symbol, or for sport-science literature questions (use sports-science-research-grounding).\n- grill-with-ui: Run a grilling interview on a local browser page instead of the terminal. Every question is laid out with its recommendation, answerable in any order, with a per-question discussion thread and one \"Send to Agent\" button. Use when the user says \"grill with ui\", invokes /grill-with-ui with a topic, or says \"/grill-with-ui resume\".\n- harvest\n- html-to-pdf: Render any local HTML report, deck, or dashboard to a print-ready PDF via headless Chrome — replaces the manual Ctrl+P → Save as PDF ritual. Use when the user says \"export this to PDF\", \"print version\", \"PDF for the coaches\", \"make this printable\", or a coach-facing HTML deliverable needs a shareable/printable copy. Do not use to build the report or deck itself (use status-report or five-slides-generator).\n- impeccable: Use when the user wants to design, redesign, shape, critique, audit, polish, clarify, distill, harden, optimize, adapt, animate, colorize, extract, or otherwise improve a frontend interface. Covers websites, landing pages, dashboards, product UI, app shells, components, forms, settings, onboarding, and empty states. Handles UX review, visual hierarchy, information architecture, cognitive load, accessibility, performance, responsive behavior, theming, anti-patterns, typography, fonts, spacing, layout, alignment, color, motion, micro-interactions, UX copy, error states, edge cases, i18n, and reusable design systems or tokens. Also use for bland designs that need to become bolder or more delightful, loud designs that should become quieter, live browser iteration on UI elements, or ambitious visual effects that should feel technically extraordinary. Not for backend-only or non-UI tasks.\n- improve-system\n- ingest-book\n- ingest-resource\n- interview-me\n- ISA\n- library-advisor\n- library-review\n- meeting-prep: Pre-meeting readiness brief for a project — what changed, what's open, every deliverable path. Use when the user says \"/meeting-prep <project>\", \"I have a meeting with <person> — are we in a good spot on <project>?\", \"verify we're up to speed before the meeting\", \"get me ready for this afternoon's meeting\", or names an upcoming coach/staff meeting. Do not use for the biweekly supervisor report (use status-report) or to sync meeting transcripts (use meeting-sync).\n- meeting-sync\n- memory-write: Writes, updates, removes, and recalls facts in the two-tier memory system (~/.claude/global-memory/ and ~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/). Use for write requests such as \"remember this\", \"note that\", \"update memory\", \"save this\", and \"forget about\", and for recall requests such as \"did we already…?\", \"what did we decide about X?\", and \"do you remember…?\". Picks the tier, dedups, and enforces caps; recall checks the injected snapshot, then memsearch. Do not use for a story or lesson worth a vault note (use capture-insight) or an article to keep (use ingest-resource).\n- name-meetings: Detects Fireflies meetings with generic titles (empty, \"Zoom Meeting\", \"Recording\", date-only, etc.), proposes structured names based on AI summary + participants, and applies approved renames via the Fireflies API. Use when user says \"/name-meetings\", \"name my meetings\", \"rename untitled meetings\", \"fix meeting titles\", \"my meetings have no names\", or when invoked by meeting-sync. Do not use to sync transcripts or audit vault filenames (use meeting-sync).\n- new-project\n- pbir-report-builder\n- pp-catapult-openfield-connect\n- pp-espn\n- pp-fireflies\n- pp-zotero-research-library\n- project: Manage the portfolio project registry (ideas, updates, ranking, promotion, dev stages) and render/publish the dashboard. Use when the user says \"/project\", \"new idea:\", \"log progress on\", \"project update\", \"add to my project inbox\", \"show my projects\", \"re-rank my projects\", \"set the stage on\", \"mark <project> as spec'd/planned/shipped\". Do not use for the DEV drift dashboard (use project-triage) or to scaffold a new repo (use new-project).\n- project-triage\n- research-in-5: Build a \"Research in 5\" weekly paper review for Baylor Athletics staff: a 5-slide deck (dark and light), a Teams post, and a staff email from one research paper. Use when the user says \"/research-in-5\", \"research in 5 on this paper\", \"weekly research review\", \"review this paper for the staff\", \"make the slides for this paper\", \"turn this study into a research review\", or hands over a paper (PDF, DOI, article URL, or Zotero item) for the weekly series. Also use to rebuild, restyle, or version an existing review (#001, v2, v3). Do not use for a research-backed answer to a sport-science question (use sports-science-research-grounding), a topic education deck with no single paper (use five-slides-generator), a plain Zotero search (use pp-zotero-research-library), or a quick in-chat summary of a paper when no deck, Teams post, or email is wanted.\n- Science\n- session-miner\n- sports-science-research-grounding: Use when the user asks a substantive question about sport science, return to play or sport, rehabilitation, strength and conditioning, workload, recovery, testing, athlete health, injury, performance, or wants a research-backed answer, and for Zotero full-library batch processing. Searches the user's Zotero library first, clearly labels external peer-reviewed fallback sources, and applies tier-aware model routing during bounded batch work. Do not use for sports scores or schedules (use pp-espn) or for a simple Zotero search or sync (use pp-zotero-research-library).\n- startup\n- status-report: Build the every-two-weeks Applied Performance status report for Kenny Boyd and Chris Ruf — paste-ready email, one-page PDF, internal record. Use when the user says \"/status-report\", \"build my status report\", \"time for the Chris report\", \"biweekly update\", \"status update for Chris and Kenny\", \"what do I send Chris this week\", or when a scheduled report date arrives. Do not use for a single meeting brief (use meeting-prep) or the end-of-day supervisor log interview (use interview-me).\n- weekly-review\n- codex:rescue: Delegate investigation, an explicit fix request, or follow-up rescue work to the Codex rescue subagent\n- codex:setup: Check whether the local Codex CLI is ready and optionally toggle the stop-time review gate\n- firecrawl:skill-gen: Generate a complete Agent Skill from a documentation URL using Firecrawl\n- superpowers:brainstorming: You MUST use this before any creative work - creating features, building components, adding functionality, or modifying behavior. Explores user intent, requirements and design before implementation.\n- superpowers:diagnosing-superpowers: Use when a superpowers session went wrong and your human partner wants to know why — repeated work, ignored plans, stumbles, poor results, a skill that didn't fire, \"it took too long\", \"why is it so expensive\", \"what is it doing\" — or wants to build a bug report for the superpowers maintainers, for the current session or a past one identified by id or path, on any harness.\n- superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents: Use when facing 2+ independent tasks that can be worked on without shared state or sequential dependencies\n- superpowers:executing-plans: Use when executing an implementation plan in the current session as the implementer yourself — your human partner chose inline execution, or no subagent tool is available\n- superpowers:finishing-a-development-branch: Use when implementation is complete, all tests pass, and you need to decide how to integrate the work\n- superpowers:receiving-code-review: Use when receiving code review feedback, before implementing suggestions, especially if feedback seems unclear or technically questionable - requires technical rigor and verification, not performative agreement or blind implementation\n- superpowers:requesting-code-review: Use when completing tasks, implementing major features, or before merging to verify work meets requirements\n- superpowers:subagent-driven-development: Use when executing implementation plans with independent tasks in the current session\n- superpowers:systematic-debugging: Use when encountering any bug, test failure, or unexpected behavior, before proposing fixes\n- superpowers:test-driven-development: Use when implementing any feature or bugfix, before writing implementation code\n- superpowers:using-git-worktrees: Use when starting feature work that needs isolation from current workspace or before executing implementation plans - ensures an isolated workspace exists via native tools or git worktree fallback\n- superpowers:using-superpowers: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\n- superpowers:verification-before-completion: Use when about to claim work is complete, fixed, or passing, before committing or creating PRs - requires running verification commands and confirming output before making any success claims; evidence before assertions always\n- superpowers:writing-plans: Use when you have a spec or requirements for a multi-step task, before touching code\n- superpowers:writing-skills: Use when creating new skills, editing existing skills, or verifying skills work before deployment\n- last30days:last30days: Research what people actually say about any topic in the last 30 days. Pulls posts and engagement from Reddit, X, YouTube, TikTok, Hacker News, Polymarket, GitHub, and the web. Includes a doctor health check to diagnose broken or missing sources.\n- context-mode:context-mode: Use context-mode tools (ctx_execute, ctx_execute_file) instead of Bash/cat when processing\nlarge outputs. Triggers: \"analyze logs\", \"summarize output\", \"process data\",\n\"parse JSON\", \"filter results\", \"extract errors\", \"check build output\",\n\"analyze dependencies\", \"process API response\", \"large file analysis\",\n\"page snapshot\", \"browser snapshot\", \"DOM structure\", \"inspect page\",\n\"accessibility tree\", \"Playwright snapshot\",\n\"run tests\", \"test output\", \"coverage report\", \"git log\", \"recent commits\",\n\"diff between branches\", \"list containers\", \"pod status\", \"disk usage\",\n\"fetch docs\", \"API reference\", \"index documentation\",\n\"call API\", \"check response\", \"query results\",\n\"find TODOs\", \"count lines\", \"codebase statistics\", \"security audit\",\n\"outdated packages\", \"dependency tree\", \"cloud resources\", \"CI/CD output\".\nAlso triggers on ANY MCP tool output that may exceed 20 lines.\nSubagent routing is handled automatically via PreToolUse hook.\n- context-mode:ctx-doctor: Run context-mode diagnostics. Checks runtimes, hooks, FTS5,\nplugin registration, npm and marketplace versions.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-doctor\n- context-mode:ctx-index: Index a local file or directory into context-mode's persistent FTS5 knowledge base\nso future ctx_search calls can retrieve focused snippets without rereading raw files.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-index\n- context-mode:ctx-insight: Open the context-mode Insight dashboard in your default browser.\nInsight is the hosted analytics layer for AI-assisted engineering teams —\nper-engineer productive rate, retry waste, blocker detection, role-narrowed views.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-insight\n- context-mode:ctx-purge: Purge the context-mode knowledge base. Permanently deletes all indexed content\nand resets session stats. This is destructive and cannot be undone.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-purge\n- context-mode:ctx-search: Search context-mode's persistent FTS5 knowledge base for previously indexed\nlocal project content, documentation, or session memory.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-search\n- context-mode:ctx-stats: Show how much context window context-mode saved this session.\nDisplays token consumption, context savings ratio, and per-tool breakdown.\nRead-only — shows stats only, no reset capability.\nTo wipe the knowledge base entirely, use ctx_purge instead.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-stats\n- context-mode:ctx-upgrade: Update context-mode from GitHub and fix hooks/settings.\nPulls latest, builds, installs, updates npm global, configures hooks.\nTrigger: /context-mode:ctx-upgrade\n- codex:codex-cli-runtime: Internal helper contract for calling the codex-companion runtime from Claude Code\n- codex:codex-result-handling: Internal guidance for presenting Codex helper output back to the user\n- codex:gpt-5-4-prompting: Internal guidance for composing Codex and GPT-5.4 prompts for coding, review, diagnosis, and research tasks inside the Codex Claude Code plugin\n- mattpocock-skills:diagnosing-bugs: Diagnosis loop for hard bugs and performance regressions. Use when the user says \"diagnose\"/\"debug this\", or reports something broken/throwing/failing/slow.\n- mattpocock-skills:tdd: Test-driven development. Use when the user wants to build features or fix bugs test-first, mentions \"red-green-refactor\", or wants integration tests.\n- mattpocock-skills:prototype: Build a throwaway prototype to answer a design question. Use when the user wants to sanity-check whether a state model or logic feels right, or explore what a UI should look like.\n- mattpocock-skills:research: Investigate a question against high-trust primary sources and capture the findings as a Markdown file in the repo. Use when the user wants a topic researched, docs or API facts gathered, or reading legwork delegated to a background agent.\n- mattpocock-skills:domain-modeling: Build and sharpen a project's domain model. Use when discussing codebase terminology, writing or editing a CONTEXT.md, or recording or editing an ADR.\n- mattpocock-skills:codebase-design: Shared vocabulary for designing deep modules. Use when the user wants to design or improve a module's interface, find deepening opportunities, decide where a seam goes, make code more testable or AI-navigable, or when another skill needs the deep-module vocabulary.\n- mattpocock-skills:code-review: Review the changes since a fixed point (commit, branch, tag, or merge-base) along two axes: Standards (does the code follow this repo's documented coding standards?) and Spec (does the code match what the originating issue/spec asked for?). Runs both reviews in parallel sub-agents and reports them side by side. Use when the user wants to review a branch, a PR, work-in-progress changes, or asks to \"review since X\".\n- mattpocock-skills:resolving-merge-conflicts: Use when you need to resolve an in-progress git merge/rebase conflict.\n- mattpocock-skills:wizard: Generate an interactive bash wizard that walks a human through steps only they can perform. Use when provisioning infrastructure, setting up credentials or CI secrets, walking an unfamiliar third-party dashboard, or running a one-off migration or cutover. Don't invoke this for steps the agent can perform itself.\n- mattpocock-skills:grilling: Grill the user relentlessly about a plan, decision, or idea. Use when the user wants to stress-test their thinking, or uses any 'grill' trigger phrases.\n- mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents: Writing documents for agents. Use when creating or editing skills, or modifying AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md.\n- watch:watch: Watch a video (URL or local path). Downloads with yt-dlp, extracts auto-scaled frames with ffmpeg, pulls the transcript from captions (or local WhisperX / cloud Whisper fallback), and hands the result to the agent so it can answer questions about what's in the video. With a Gemini API key, Google's agentic video model watches the full video instead.\n- typesafe:typesafe-ai: Build AI-powered software with TypeSafe: small units of AI intelligence you can use like programming primitives. Its System One models, including Jev, turn natural language and application state into typed judgments and probabilities that code can combine. Use when a feature needs programmable common sense, when brainstorming what AI could make possible in an app, or when an LLM prompt-and-parse step could become a structured decision. Applications include routing, ranking, extraction, verification, and interactive experiences; these are starting points, not the limits. Read live docs and cookbooks to find useful patterns and discover new combinations.\n- cowork-plugin-management:cowork-plugin-customizer: Customize a Claude Code plugin for a specific organization's tools and workflows. Use when: customize plugin, set up plugin, configure plugin, tailor plugin, adjust plugin settings, customize plugin connectors, customize plugin skill, tweak plugin, modify plugin configuration.\n- cowork-plugin-management:create-cowork-plugin: Guide users through creating a new plugin from scratch in a cowork session. Use when users want to create a plugin, build a plugin, make a new plugin, develop a plugin, scaffold a plugin, start a plugin from scratch, or design a plugin. This skill requires Cowork mode with access to the outputs directory for delivering the final .plugin file.\n- desktop-commander:ai-tools-setup: Set up, connect, validate, and repair Claude Desktop and MCP servers using Desktop Commander — inspecting and fixing mcp.json / claude_desktop_config.json, getting MCP servers to connect, and diagnosing local AI tooling. Use when a user wants to install, configure, connect, validate, or fix Claude Desktop, an MCP server, an MCP config file, or a local AI agent/gateway. Trigger on the symptoms that actually bring people in: \"Claude can't see my tools\", \"my MCP server isn't showing up\", \"tools aren't loading in Claude\", \"add an MCP server\", \"my MCP isn't connecting\", \"fix my Claude Desktop config\", \"the agent won't respond\", \"wire up Ollama / LM Studio\", \"restart Claude Desktop\". Also covers local agent gateways like OpenClaw and Hermes.\n- desktop-commander:computer-health-check\n- desktop-commander:desktop-commander-overview: Use for Desktop Commander MCP capabilities — persistent shells and REPLs, long-running processes, filesystem beyond the workspace, structured files (.xlsx, .docx, .pdf, images) and large local data files such as CSVs, ripgrep search at scale, SSH, or cross-turn state.\n- desktop-commander:knowledge-base\n- desktop-commander:obsidian-vault\n- desktop-commander:terminal\n- firecrawl:firecrawl\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-agent: Autonomously navigate websites and extract structured data across pages. Use when the task requires navigation or no suitable ready-made workflow or data provider covers it.\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-alexandria\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-crawl: Bulk-extract many pages from one site or section. Use for \"crawl\", \"everything under /docs\", or content spanning linked pages.\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-developer-index\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-download\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-interact\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-map\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-monitor\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-parse\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-research-index\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-scrape\n- firecrawl:firecrawl-search\n- exa:exa-agent\n- exa:search\n- dataviz: Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. When the destination is a first-party document connector (host-designated, never self-described) that renders live charts, hand it the rows (inline, or as an uploaded data file the chart cites) rather than a rendered PNG/SVG — a picture of a chart loses hover, data inspection and per-value comments. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \"chart\", \"graph\", \"plot\", \"data viz\", \"visualization\", \"dashboard\", \"analytics\", \"visualize data\", \"categorical colors\", \"sequential / diverging palette\", \"stat tile\", \"sparkline\", \"heatmap\", \"legend\", \"axis\", \"tooltip\", \"chart colors\", \"color by series\".\n- artifact-design: Design guidance and fundamentals for Artifacts. - Load before writing any artifact, including a skill-instructed Markdown one - Markdown is never a shortcut past the design pass.\n- artifact-diagramming: Diagramming know-how for Artifacts - when a picture earns its place, how to draw one that shows the real mechanism, and the inline-SVG mechanics that keep it legible in both themes.\n- artifact-capabilities: Runtime capabilities a published Artifact page can be granted — behavior static HTML cannot provide on its own, such as the page reading live or connected data, remembering what people do on it (a poll, a sign-up sheet, a checklist, a document edited in place — it saves new versions of itself), keeping state shared across viewers, knowing who is viewing, asking Claude a question of its own, storing files people add, or handing the viewer a file to save. Serves this user's live capability roster and the typed call definitions. Load it whenever any such runtime behavior would make an artifact more useful, before writing the page.\n- update-config: Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.\n- keybindings-help: Use when the user wants to customize keyboard shortcuts, rebind keys, add chord bindings, or modify ~/.claude/keybindings.json. Examples: \"rebind ctrl+s\", \"add a chord shortcut\", \"change the submit key\", \"customize keybindings\".\n- code-review\n- simplify: Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.\n- fewer-permission-prompts: Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.\n- loop: Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo). Omit the interval to let the model self-pace. - When the user wants to set up a recurring task, poll for status, or run something repeatedly on an interval (e.g. \"check the deploy every 5 minutes\", \"keep running /babysit-prs\"). Do NOT invoke for one-off tasks.\n- schedule: Create, update, list, or run scheduled cloud agents (routines) that execute on a cron schedule. - When the user wants to schedule a recurring cloud agent, set up automated tasks, create a cron job for Claude Code, or manage their scheduled agents/routines. Also use when the user wants a one-time scheduled run (\"run this once at 3pm\", \"remind me to check X tomorrow\").\n- claude-api: Reference for the Claude API / Anthropic SDK — model ids, pricing, params, streaming, tool use, MCP, agents, caching, token counting, model migration.\nTRIGGER — read BEFORE opening the target file; don't skip because it \"looks like a one-liner\" — whenever: the prompt names Claude/Anthropic in any form (Claude, Anthropic, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, `anthropic`, `@anthropic-ai`, `claude-*`, `us.anthropic.*`, `[1m]`); the user asks about an LLM (pricing/model choice/limits/caching) — never answer from memory; OR the task is LLM-shaped with provider unstated (agent/MCP/tool-definition/multi-agent/RAG/LLM-judge/computer-use; generate/summarize/extract/classify/rewrite/converse over NL; debugging refusals/cutoffs/streaming/tool-calls/tokens).\nSKIP only when another provider is being worked on (overrides all triggers): OpenAI/GPT/Gemini/Llama/Mistral/Cohere/Ollama named in the query; OR `grep -rE 'openai|langchain_openai|google.generativeai|genai|mistralai|cohere|ollama'` over the project hits (run this grep FIRST if no provider named — don't Read the file).\n- workflow-authoring: Reference for writing a Workflow tool script (script API and gotchas, resume, quality patterns, worked examples). Load before authoring a script for a workflow the user already opted into; it does not itself authorize running one.\n- claude-in-chrome: Automates your Chrome browser to interact with web pages - clicking elements, filling forms, capturing screenshots, reading console logs, and navigating sites. Opens pages in new tabs within your existing Chrome session. Requires site-level permissions before executing (configured in the extension). - When the user wants to interact with web pages, automate browser tasks, capture screenshots, read console logs, or perform any browser-based actions. Always invoke BEFORE attempting to use any mcp__claude-in-chrome__* tools.\n- run: Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).\n- init: Initialize a new CLAUDE.md file with codebase documentation\n- security-review\n- anthropic-skills:articulation-skill: Help users articulate complex ideas clearly and simply to non-technical audiences using proven communication frameworks. Use when users need to explain technical concepts, structure presentations or writing, create clear documentation, improve communication clarity, or translate jargon into accessible language. Triggers include requests like \"help me explain this clearly\", \"how do I structure this presentation\", \"make this less technical\", \"help me articulate this idea\", or any request to simplify complex communication.\n- anthropic-skills:built-in-browser\n- anthropic-skills:chrome-browser\n- anthropic-skills:closeout\n- anthropic-skills:computer-use\n- anthropic-skills:deep-research\n- anthropic-skills:dev-sync\n- anthropic-skills:docs\n- anthropic-skills:docx: Use this skill whenever the user wants to create, read, edit, or manipulate Word documents (.docx) or Word templates (.dotx). Triggers include: any mention of Microsoft Word Documents, such as 'Word doc', 'word document', '.docx', '.dotx', 'microsoft doc'. Also use when extracting or reorganizing content from .docx or .dotx files, inserting or replacing images in documents, find-and-replace in Word files, working with tracked changes or comments, or converting content into a polished Word document. If the user asks for a deliverable as a Word or .docx file (to download, email or print), use this skill. However, if they ask for a document, page, report, memo, or notes WITHOUT naming a file format and the session offers Claude's own dedicated document or page skill or connector, use that instead, even if they will email or print it. Do NOT use for PDFs, spreadsheets, Google Docs, or coding unrelated to document generation.\n- anthropic-skills:five-slides-generator\n- anthropic-skills:humanizer: Remove signs of AI-generated writing from text. Use when editing or reviewing text to make it sound more natural and human-written. Based on Wikipedia's comprehensive \"Signs of AI writing\" guide. Detects and fixes patterns including: inflated symbolism, promotional language, superficial -ing analyses, vague attributions, em dash overuse, rule of three, AI vocabulary words, negative parallelisms, and excessive conjunctive phrases.\n- anthropic-skills:import-memory\n- anthropic-skills:last30days\n- anthropic-skills:mcp-builder\n- anthropic-skills:migrate-project-to-disk\n- anthropic-skills:morning\n- anthropic-skills:new-project\n- anthropic-skills:pdf\n- anthropic-skills:pptx\n- anthropic-skills:skill-creator\n- anthropic-skills:slide-deck-generator\n- anthropic-skills:watch\n- anthropic-skills:xlsx\n</system-reminder>"}],"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"97577bdd-6ebc-4a38-a0a9-ea623e7b8c38","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"auto_mode","autoModeConsentFlow":false,"bashFirst":true,"bashFirstSteer":"relaxed","steerOnly":true,"bypass":true},"type":"attachment","uuid":"2c79fc9c-5313-4e11-9238-010930382818","timestamp":"2026-09-28T12:59:21.004Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nWhile bypass permissions mode is active:\n\nYou can do much of your work through the Bash tool when it is the simpler route: read files with cat, head, or sed -n, search with grep and find, and make small, mechanical file changes with sed, heredocs, or short scripts instead of the dedicated Read, Edit, or Write tools. The choice is yours: prefer Edit or Write when a shell edit would be fragile, such as exact or multi-line replacements, or sed/awk flags that differ between GNU and BSD/macOS.\n</system-reminder>"}],"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"2c79fc9c-5313-4e11-9238-010930382818","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>15000000 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"aea99bf4-9717-45cd-9342-f57f7b79cf52","timestamp":"2026-09-28T12:59:21.004Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>15000000 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"aea99bf4-9717-45cd-9342-f57f7b79cf52","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_cancelled","hookName":"UserPromptSubmit","toolUseID":"c1583ebb-bc62-4af4-be58-bc612f1c0734","hookEvent":"UserPromptSubmit","command":"python3 \"$HOME/.claude/skill-router/router.py\"","durationMs":5995,"timedOut":true,"timeoutMs":5000},"type":"attachment","uuid":"2efa3a78-07e4-4027-9982-aefbb864f48d","timestamp":"2026-09-28T12:59:27.003Z","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"2efa3a78-07e4-4027-9982-aefbb864f48d","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"instructions","files":[{"path":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\.claude\\CLAUDE.md","type":"User","content":"# Machine\r\n\r\nMachine-specific shell, paths, and traps live in `~/.claude/machine.md` (gitignored; copy `machine.macos.md` or `machine.windows.md` to `machine.md` on a new machine). It is imported below.\r\n\r\n@machine.md\r\n\r\nTwo machines share this setup: the MacBook (`~/DEV`) and the Baylor Windows laptop (`~/Desktop/DEV`). Projects sync through GitHub (`erash11`), documents through Google Drive. Every project repo carries a Session routine in its `AGENTS.md`: `git pull` + read `docs/STATUS.md` at start; update `STATUS.md`, commit, `git push` at end.\r\n# Workflow Expectations\r\n\r\n- When asked to **create, merge, or consolidate an artifact** (calendar, deck, doc), the expected deliverable is always a produced file. Verify what exists first, then create the artifact regardless — never conclude with \"it already exists\" instead of delivering the file.\r\n- When the user types `/graphify`, invoke the Skill tool with `skill: \"graphify\"` before doing anything else.\r\n- Skills are tools, not a gate. Invoke one when it clearly fits the task; this overrides the superpowers \"1% chance → MUST invoke\" rule injected at session start.\r\n\r\n# Session Startup\r\n\r\nHandled by the `keystone-unified-snapshot.js` SessionStart hook — do not read these files by hand. It injects `global-memory/USER.md`, `global-memory/MEMORY.md`, `transcripts/.errors.log` (only when non-empty), and a pointer to the KeystoneOS snapshot.\r\n\r\n- If `.errors.log` content appears, surface it once on the first response, then continue. It is normally empty; entries mean hook failures.\r\n- If a **\"SessionStart hook could not read a required memory file\"** block appears, say so on the first response instead of working around it.\r\n- **If no `## User profile` block is in context at session start, the hook did not fire.** Say so, then read `USER.md`, `MEMORY.md` and `transcripts/.errors.log` by hand.\r\n- What the hook injects is a frozen snapshot. Mid-session writes take effect next session. Never load more than this proactively.\r\n\r\n# Memory — routing only\r\n\r\nThe two-tier memory system (`~/.claude/global-memory/` and `~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/`) is owned by the **`memory-write` skill**, which holds the tier rules, character caps, slug rules, write steps, retrieval ladder, and daily-compiled-log rules.\r\n\r\nThe active project's Tier A memory (`~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/`, indexed by its `MEMORY.md`) is **auto-injected by the harness** each session. Tier A files are also indexed by memsearch, so they stay recallable across projects.\r\n\r\n- Writing a fact — \"remember this\", \"note that\", \"save this\", \"forget about\" → `Skill(\"memory-write\")`\r\n- Recalling past context — \"did we already…?\", \"what did we decide about X\" → `Skill(\"memory-write\")`, retrieval-ladder section\r\n- Daily compiled logs at `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/daily/` are owned by `claude-memory-compiler`. Never hand-edit them; never read them eagerly.\r\n- **Slugs differ per machine** (Mac `-Users-kristinarash-DEV-<X>`, Windows `C--Users-eric-rash-Desktop-DEV-<X>`). Tier A written on one is not auto-injected on the other; memsearch still finds it. Durable project facts belong in the repo's `docs/` so both machines see them.\r\n\r\nDo not restate the memory procedure in this file. It has one home, and that home is the skill.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n`AGENTS.md` is the sibling of this file for harnesses that have no Skill tool (Codex and similar). It intentionally carries the memory rules as plain prose instead of routing to a skill. **The two files are not meant to be identical — do not \"fix\" the difference by symlinking them.**"},{"path":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\.claude\\machine.md","type":"User","content":"# Shell / Environment (Windows)\n\n- This is the Baylor-managed Windows laptop. Native shell is PowerShell 7 (`pwsh`) / Windows PowerShell; Claude Code hooks run under Git Bash, where `$HOME` is `/c/Users/eric_rash`.\n- Prefer Desktop Commander over Windows-MCP when both could do the job.\n- Tool paths: `~/.claude/TOOLPATHS.md` is Mac-centric; on this machine verify with `Get-Command <tool>` / `where.exe <tool>`.\n- Traps:\n  - `uv` lives at `~/.local/bin/uv.exe`. Hooks call `$HOME/.local/bin/uv`; do not relocate it.\n  - No admin rights by default. Anything needing elevation (Hyper-V, diskpart, services) has to be run by Eric in an elevated PowerShell.\n  - Docker Desktop (WSL2) hosts Milvus for memsearch. It must be running for memsearch hooks to work.\n  - `dcg` is not installed here; the PreToolUse hook is guarded and passes through.\n  - Paths with spaces are common (`G:\\My Drive\\...`, `OneDrive - Baylor University`). Quote them.\n  - Google Drive for Desktop backs up all of `Desktop\\DEV` and cannot follow junctions (each one is a sync error). So renv project libraries live outside DEV: user env var `RENV_PATHS_LIBRARY_ROOT` = `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\R\\renv-library` (set 2026-09-22). Do not undo it; restart RStudio after env changes. `DEV\\_DRIVE` and `LibraryProject\\ZoteroInjestion` are junctions kept on purpose (2 expected Drive errors).\n  - R is at `C:\\Program Files\\R\\R-4.5.3\\bin\\Rscript.exe` (not on PATH).\n- DEV projects live at `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV` (one root, one repo per project; Football/ sub-projects are their own repos).\n- Primary storage drive (documents, data, deliverables): `G:\\My Drive\\00_Primary_Storage_Drive` (Google Drive; moved off Baylor OneDrive 2026-09-10).\n- Legacy tool scripts for the migration live in `C:\\DEV\\_tools`."},{"path":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\CLAUDE.md","type":"Project","content":"# ~/DEV — workspace map\r\n\r\nEric Rash — Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Sport science and\r\nfootball analytics, plus the agent system that runs them.\r\n\r\nThis file is a **map, not a rulebook**. Behavior rules live in\r\n`~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`. Read only the index you need.\r\n\r\n## Areas\r\n\r\n| Area | Index | What lives there |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| Football | `FOOTBALL.md` | Game film, player analytics, CFB prediction. ~39k files — about two thirds of DEV. |\r\n| Performance | `PERFORMANCE.md` | Athlete monitoring, testing, load, risk. GPS, force plate, VALD, DEXA, S2. |\r\n| Research | `RESEARCH.md` | Literature, Zotero, book library, reading pipelines. |\r\n| Platform | `PLATFORM.md` | KeystoneOS, dashboards, CLIs, scheduled jobs. |\r\n| Program | `PROGRAM.md` | Staff reporting, curriculum, proposals, vetting, brand. |\r\n| Personal | `PERSONAL.md` | Side projects. Not Baylor work. |\r\n| Commands | `COMMANDS.md` | Desk reference: every report/project command Eric can run himself. |\r\n\r\n## Elsewhere on disk\r\n\r\n| Path | What it is |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `~/DEV/_ARCHIVE/` | Parked projects. Not a git repo, so the consolidation sweep skips it. Has its own `README.md`. |\r\n| `~/DEV/_INBOX/` | Loose files from the `~/DEV` root. Own repo. |\r\n| `~/SecondBrain/` | Obsidian vault, ~850 notes. Own `CLAUDE.md`. |\r\n| `~/.claude/skills/` | ~155 skills |\r\n| `~/go/bin/` | 8 custom CLIs built with `cli-printing-press` |\r\n| `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/` | ~230 compiled articles |\r\n\r\n## Layout rules\r\n\r\nFull rules — grouping depth, the depth-2 discovery limit, and the gitignored-subtree\r\ntrap — live in project memory: `project_dev-layout-and-discovery.md`.\r\n\r\n- 23 projects carry their own `CLAUDE.md`. Read it before working in one.\r\n\r\n## Handling care\r\n\r\n- `DexaProject/` holds identifiable health data. It lives on Google Drive, not in\r\n  `~/DEV`, on **both** machines — one copy, reached by each machine's own path:\r\n  - Mac: `~/Library/CloudStorage/GoogleDrive-eric.rash11@gmail.com/My Drive/00_Primary_Storage_Drive/DexaProject`\r\n  - Windows: `G:\\My Drive\\00_Primary_Storage_Drive\\DexaProject`\r\n\r\n  See `PERFORMANCE.md`.\r\n  Never paste its contents into chat, an artifact, or an external service.\r\n\r\n## Keeping this true\r\n\r\nWhen a file moves or a new project starts, **update the router in the same\r\nturn**. A stale pointer is worse than no pointer.\r\n\r\nThe counts above are approximate on purpose — they convey scale, not state, and\r\na `~` keeps ordinary growth from making this file wrong. Re-measure them only\r\nwhen one is off by enough to change a decision. Counts last checked 2026-09-05.\r\n\r\nMemory files named in these indexes (`project_*.md`, `user_*.md`) live at\r\n`~/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/memory/`. They auto-load only when\r\ncwd is `~/DEV` itself — from inside a sub-project, open them by path.\r\n\r\nSearch everything with `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`."},{"path":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\.claude\\projects\\C--Users-eric-rash-Desktop-DEV\\memory\\MEMORY.md","type":"AutoMem","content":"# Memory Index\n\nOne line per memory: title, file, and a hook that says when to open it.\nNever put memory content here — the file itself holds the facts.\n\n- [Printing Press spec traps](project_printing-press-spec-traps.md) — writing a CLI spec: pagination, envelope fields, fixtures, doc regen\n- [zsh shell traps](project_zsh-shell-traps.md) — reserved vars, word-splitting, pipeline exit codes\n- [Status report sourcing](project_status-report-sourcing.md) — which source counts as \"Shipped\", which OKR doc is live\n- [Fireflies CLI on Mac](project_fireflies-cli-broken-on-mac.md) — \"out of memory (14)\", getting transcript text, renames that don't stick\n- [gws Google Forms API](project_gws-forms-api.md) — scopes, create vs batchUpdate, duplicated items\n- [Kenny Boyd doc review](project_kenny-boyd-doc-review-preferences.md) — tone and labelling rules for review docs\n- [Terminal Fluency workspace](project_terminal-fluency-workspace.md) — resume protocol and how he learns CLI\n- [PATH shadowing and cleanup](project_path-shadowing-and-cleanup.md) — measuring PATH honestly; before deleting any binary\n- [Backup/sync system](project_backup-sync-system.md) — manifest routing, verify-by-receipt, schedule history\n- [CFB Analytics Lab](project_cfb-analytics-lab.md) — resume protocol and CFBD API traps\n- [CFB forecast capture](project_cfb-forecast-capture.md) — Big 12 weekly freeze; one wrapper command; is a gap really a missed week?\n- [Drive FUSE rsync loop](project_drive-fuse-rsync-mtime-loop.md) — rsync re-sending identical files to Google Drive\n- [SecondBrain vault location](project_secondbrain-vault-location.md) — current path and what hardcodes it\n- [Milvus lock misdiagnosis](project_milvus-lock-misdiagnosis.md) — Zotero/Milvus errors; diagnose before rebuilding\n- [Memory compiler pipeline](project_memory-compiler-pipeline.md) — dead-flush tells, backfilling a date, \"Indexed 0 chunks\"\n- [memsearch index path drift](project_memsearch-index-path-drift.md) — silent fallback to fewer indexed paths\n- [memsearch hook timeout cap](project_memsearch-hook-timeout-cap.md) — slow or timed-out index; check Mac sleep (`pmset -g log`) first, then corpus growth\n- [Morning briefing retired](project_morning-briefing-retired.md) — where the code, briefings and config went\n- [graphify runtime setup](project_graphify-runtime-setup.md) — venv location, install flags, rebuild triggers\n- [graphify semantic extraction](project_graphify-semantic-extraction.md) — quota traps and silent 429s\n- [Automation cadence](project_automation-cadence.md) — ritual times, reindex, audit due dates, gws/Gmail limits\n- [S2 Cognition analysis](project_s2-cognition-analysis.md) — norms scope, battery size, threshold discipline\n- [Sports teams](user_sports-teams.md) — default team for ambiguous WNBA asks\n- [Working-conditions bottleneck](user_working-conditions-bottleneck.md) — what limits him; the permission gate on acting unasked\n- [Zero-edit YouTube pipeline](reference_zero-edit-youtube-pipeline.md) — Jack Roberts' Claude Code video system\n- [DEV layout and discovery](project_dev-layout-and-discovery.md) — folder rules, depth-2 scan limit, gitignore trap\n- [KeystoneOS verification habits](project_keystoneos-verification-habits.md) — which state claims mislead; re-check before asserting; morning-cadence dies on a `C:\\` or URL `canonicalPath` in projects.json\n- [Portfolio registry provenance](project_portfolio-registry-write-provenance.md) — who writes portfolio.json; how to commit changes\n- [Command centre](project_command-centre.md) — localhost:7373 front door + the /os board, daemon health, backup default-deny\n- [Vendored skill install](project_vendored-skill-install.md) — installing a third-party skill for Claude Code + Codex + Cowork; the `-a` flag trap\n- [Vendored skill repair](project_vendored-skill-repair.md) — a skill returns empty output; which copy is actually live; patches that revert\n- [Personal site build routes](project_personal-site-build-routes.md) — Artifact constraints vs scroll-craft; where the standalone build lives\n- [Book scan page ordering](project_book-scan-page-ordering.md) — phone-scanned book pages out of order; folio OCR lies, read a contact sheet\n- [Ingest review gate](feedback_ingest-review-gate.md) — before ingest-book or any expensive atomization; what the arithmetic gate does and does not catch\n- [Dormant threads](project_dormant-threads.md) — Tezino CampPrep and FaceComps sim: where each stopped and what is still open\n- [RTP CLA course](project_rtp-cla-course.md) — book ingest state, the +13 page offset, the one rebuild still outstanding\n- [Post-merge cleanup](feedback_post-merge-cleanup.md) — standing OK to delete merged worktrees/branches unasked; verify-first rule; Windows read-only \"Permission denied\" fix\n- [SCN sprint course](project_scn-sprint-course.md) — Moyer/Natera sprint course: where the 58 library entries and review live, RTP-stack role, open decisions\n- [ATH Tech framework](project_ath-tech-framework.md) — the shipped 08-14 doc set, the five unwritten gaps, the ITS blocker\n- [Supervisor log pipeline](project_supervisor-log-pipeline.md) — nightly log feeding the Kenny/Chris report; action-items-are-not-outcomes trap\n- [dcg guard blocks](project_dcg-guard-blocks.md) — a Bash command was rejected outright; which shapes trip it and the rewrite that works\n- [Headless tool-deny gap](project_headless-claude-tool-deny-gap.md) — `claude -p --disallowedTools Bash` still runs shell via context-mode MCP; before calling a headless run read-only\n- [Config encoding damage from Windows](project_settings-json-bom-trap.md) — dcg cries \"hook missing\", or the statusline bars render as `â–“` junk; one bad write causes both, in settings.json AND CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md\n- [Tech inventory refresh](project_tech-inventory-refresh.md) — which forms platform is live, why gws cannot pull responses, the stale OneDrive copy, reminder sent 9/8, the refresh command\n- [Brain audit loop](project_brain-audit-loop.md) — scoring the second brain; why manual rituals die and cron ones live\n- [Hook two-copies trap](project_hook-two-copies-trap.md) — a hook patch \"reverts\"; `.claude/hooks/` and `.codex/hooks/` both need it\n- [Child dental plan](user_child-dental-plan.md) — 4yo cavities: pediatric dentist not yet booked, SDF vs n-HAp\n- [ELI5 output style](user_eli5-output-style.md) — concise plain-language output rules shared by Claude Code and Codex\n- [GitHub consolidation](project_github-consolidation.md) — ~/DEV on GitHub: the thin root repo, _ARCHIVE, where DexaProject went, merge traps\n- [Skill bloat and promotion](project_skill-bloat-and-promotion.md) — before touching ANY skill folder: which of the three copies is canonical, why `~/.claude/skills` IS a git repo, the sync script that deletes, the 2026-09-12 audit, the 40 name-only skills (9/14), Pi/pi.dev facts\n- [Prioritization slate](project_prioritization-slate.md) — Eric proposes a new project or idea; the cap, charters, slate-check timer, clearing a nag with mark-done\n- [Baylor department names](user_baylor-department-names.md) — Athletic Performance / Athletic Medicine / Applied Performance, never S&C / sports medicine / sport science\n- [Git Bash MSYS/AD stall](project_git-bash-msys-ad-stall.md) — why bash randomly dies with `add_item errno 1`: AD lookup exceeds MSYS2's 15s spinlock; fix via nsswitch files-only + `shell: powershell` hooks\n- [Backup task sleep-kill signature](project_backup-task-sleepkill-signature.md) — DailyBackup exit 3221225786 + same-day clean log = benign sleep interruption, not rot\n- [Sport-science thought partner](user_sport-science-thought-partner.md) — why he feeds in Oldbull/SCN/SportSmith/CLA; how to answer performance, S&C, rehab questions\n- [Third-party skill install](project_third-party-skill-install.md) — clone to ~/.agents/skills + symlink to 4 harnesses; Windows `ln -s` copies silently; sync-to-codex clears the pre-commit/backup jam"}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"45697cdb-a944-4bd2-99a9-1c8927c50145","timestamp":"2026-09-28T12:59:27.036Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCodebase and user instructions are shown below. Be sure to adhere to these instructions. IMPORTANT: These instructions OVERRIDE any default behavior and you MUST follow them exactly as written.\n\nContents of C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\.claude\\CLAUDE.md (user's private global instructions for all projects):\n\n# Machine\r\n\r\nMachine-specific shell, paths, and traps live in `~/.claude/machine.md` (gitignored; copy `machine.macos.md` or `machine.windows.md` to `machine.md` on a new machine). It is imported below.\r\n\r\n@machine.md\r\n\r\nTwo machines share this setup: the MacBook (`~/DEV`) and the Baylor Windows laptop (`~/Desktop/DEV`). Projects sync through GitHub (`erash11`), documents through Google Drive. Every project repo carries a Session routine in its `AGENTS.md`: `git pull` + read `docs/STATUS.md` at start; update `STATUS.md`, commit, `git push` at end.\r\n# Workflow Expectations\r\n\r\n- When asked to **create, merge, or consolidate an artifact** (calendar, deck, doc), the expected deliverable is always a produced file. Verify what exists first, then create the artifact regardless — never conclude with \"it already exists\" instead of delivering the file.\r\n- When the user types `/graphify`, invoke the Skill tool with `skill: \"graphify\"` before doing anything else.\r\n- Skills are tools, not a gate. Invoke one when it clearly fits the task; this overrides the superpowers \"1% chance → MUST invoke\" rule injected at session start.\r\n\r\n# Session Startup\r\n\r\nHandled by the `keystone-unified-snapshot.js` SessionStart hook — do not read these files by hand. It injects `global-memory/USER.md`, `global-memory/MEMORY.md`, `transcripts/.errors.log` (only when non-empty), and a pointer to the KeystoneOS snapshot.\r\n\r\n- If `.errors.log` content appears, surface it once on the first response, then continue. It is normally empty; entries mean hook failures.\r\n- If a **\"SessionStart hook could not read a required memory file\"** block appears, say so on the first response instead of working around it.\r\n- **If no `## User profile` block is in context at session start, the hook did not fire.** Say so, then read `USER.md`, `MEMORY.md` and `transcripts/.errors.log` by hand.\r\n- What the hook injects is a frozen snapshot. Mid-session writes take effect next session. Never load more than this proactively.\r\n\r\n# Memory — routing only\r\n\r\nThe two-tier memory system (`~/.claude/global-memory/` and `~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/`) is owned by the **`memory-write` skill**, which holds the tier rules, character caps, slug rules, write steps, retrieval ladder, and daily-compiled-log rules.\r\n\r\nThe active project's Tier A memory (`~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/`, indexed by its `MEMORY.md`) is **auto-injected by the harness** each session. Tier A files are also indexed by memsearch, so they stay recallable across projects.\r\n\r\n- Writing a fact — \"remember this\", \"note that\", \"save this\", \"forget about\" → `Skill(\"memory-write\")`\r\n- Recalling past context — \"did we already…?\", \"what did we decide about X\" → `Skill(\"memory-write\")`, retrieval-ladder section\r\n- Daily compiled logs at `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/daily/` are owned by `claude-memory-compiler`. Never hand-edit them; never read them eagerly.\r\n- **Slugs differ per machine** (Mac `-Users-kristinarash-DEV-<X>`, Windows `C--Users-eric-rash-Desktop-DEV-<X>`). Tier A written on one is not auto-injected on the other; memsearch still finds it. Durable project facts belong in the repo's `docs/` so both machines see them.\r\n\r\nDo not restate the memory procedure in this file. It has one home, and that home is the skill.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n`AGENTS.md` is the sibling of this file for harnesses that have no Skill tool (Codex and similar). It intentionally carries the memory rules as plain prose instead of routing to a skill. **The two files are not meant to be identical — do not \"fix\" the difference by symlinking them.**\n\nContents of C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\.claude\\machine.md (user's private global instructions for all projects):\n\n# Shell / Environment (Windows)\n\n- This is the Baylor-managed Windows laptop. Native shell is PowerShell 7 (`pwsh`) / Windows PowerShell; Claude Code hooks run under Git Bash, where `$HOME` is `/c/Users/eric_rash`.\n- Prefer Desktop Commander over Windows-MCP when both could do the job.\n- Tool paths: `~/.claude/TOOLPATHS.md` is Mac-centric; on this machine verify with `Get-Command <tool>` / `where.exe <tool>`.\n- Traps:\n  - `uv` lives at `~/.local/bin/uv.exe`. Hooks call `$HOME/.local/bin/uv`; do not relocate it.\n  - No admin rights by default. Anything needing elevation (Hyper-V, diskpart, services) has to be run by Eric in an elevated PowerShell.\n  - Docker Desktop (WSL2) hosts Milvus for memsearch. It must be running for memsearch hooks to work.\n  - `dcg` is not installed here; the PreToolUse hook is guarded and passes through.\n  - Paths with spaces are common (`G:\\My Drive\\...`, `OneDrive - Baylor University`). Quote them.\n  - Google Drive for Desktop backs up all of `Desktop\\DEV` and cannot follow junctions (each one is a sync error). So renv project libraries live outside DEV: user env var `RENV_PATHS_LIBRARY_ROOT` = `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\R\\renv-library` (set 2026-09-22). Do not undo it; restart RStudio after env changes. `DEV\\_DRIVE` and `LibraryProject\\ZoteroInjestion` are junctions kept on purpose (2 expected Drive errors).\n  - R is at `C:\\Program Files\\R\\R-4.5.3\\bin\\Rscript.exe` (not on PATH).\n- DEV projects live at `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV` (one root, one repo per project; Football/ sub-projects are their own repos).\n- Primary storage drive (documents, data, deliverables): `G:\\My Drive\\00_Primary_Storage_Drive` (Google Drive; moved off Baylor OneDrive 2026-09-10).\n- Legacy tool scripts for the migration live in `C:\\DEV\\_tools`.\n\nContents of C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\CLAUDE.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# ~/DEV — workspace map\r\n\r\nEric Rash — Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Sport science and\r\nfootball analytics, plus the agent system that runs them.\r\n\r\nThis file is a **map, not a rulebook**. Behavior rules live in\r\n`~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`. Read only the index you need.\r\n\r\n## Areas\r\n\r\n| Area | Index | What lives there |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| Football | `FOOTBALL.md` | Game film, player analytics, CFB prediction. ~39k files — about two thirds of DEV. |\r\n| Performance | `PERFORMANCE.md` | Athlete monitoring, testing, load, risk. GPS, force plate, VALD, DEXA, S2. |\r\n| Research | `RESEARCH.md` | Literature, Zotero, book library, reading pipelines. |\r\n| Platform | `PLATFORM.md` | KeystoneOS, dashboards, CLIs, scheduled jobs. |\r\n| Program | `PROGRAM.md` | Staff reporting, curriculum, proposals, vetting, brand. |\r\n| Personal | `PERSONAL.md` | Side projects. Not Baylor work. |\r\n| Commands | `COMMANDS.md` | Desk reference: every report/project command Eric can run himself. |\r\n\r\n## Elsewhere on disk\r\n\r\n| Path | What it is |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `~/DEV/_ARCHIVE/` | Parked projects. Not a git repo, so the consolidation sweep skips it. Has its own `README.md`. |\r\n| `~/DEV/_INBOX/` | Loose files from the `~/DEV` root. Own repo. |\r\n| `~/SecondBrain/` | Obsidian vault, ~850 notes. Own `CLAUDE.md`. |\r\n| `~/.claude/skills/` | ~155 skills |\r\n| `~/go/bin/` | 8 custom CLIs built with `cli-printing-press` |\r\n| `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/` | ~230 compiled articles |\r\n\r\n## Layout rules\r\n\r\nFull rules — grouping depth, the depth-2 discovery limit, and the gitignored-subtree\r\ntrap — live in project memory: `project_dev-layout-and-discovery.md`.\r\n\r\n- 23 projects carry their own `CLAUDE.md`. Read it before working in one.\r\n\r\n## Handling care\r\n\r\n- `DexaProject/` holds identifiable health data. It lives on Google Drive, not in\r\n  `~/DEV`, on **both** machines — one copy, reached by each machine's own path:\r\n  - Mac: `~/Library/CloudStorage/GoogleDrive-eric.rash11@gmail.com/My Drive/00_Primary_Storage_Drive/DexaProject`\r\n  - Windows: `G:\\My Drive\\00_Primary_Storage_Drive\\DexaProject`\r\n\r\n  See `PERFORMANCE.md`.\r\n  Never paste its contents into chat, an artifact, or an external service.\r\n\r\n## Keeping this true\r\n\r\nWhen a file moves or a new project starts, **update the router in the same\r\nturn**. A stale pointer is worse than no pointer.\r\n\r\nThe counts above are approximate on purpose — they convey scale, not state, and\r\na `~` keeps ordinary growth from making this file wrong. Re-measure them only\r\nwhen one is off by enough to change a decision. Counts last checked 2026-09-05.\r\n\r\nMemory files named in these indexes (`project_*.md`, `user_*.md`) live at\r\n`~/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/memory/`. They auto-load only when\r\ncwd is `~/DEV` itself — from inside a sub-project, open them by path.\r\n\r\nSearch everything with `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`.\n\nContents of C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\.claude\\projects\\C--Users-eric-rash-Desktop-DEV\\memory\\MEMORY.md (user's auto-memory, persists across conversations):\n\n# Memory Index\n\nOne line per memory: title, file, and a hook that says when to open it.\nNever put memory content here — the file itself holds the facts.\n\n- [Printing Press spec traps](project_printing-press-spec-traps.md) — writing a CLI spec: pagination, envelope fields, fixtures, doc regen\n- [zsh shell traps](project_zsh-shell-traps.md) — reserved vars, word-splitting, pipeline exit codes\n- [Status report sourcing](project_status-report-sourcing.md) — which source counts as \"Shipped\", which OKR doc is live\n- [Fireflies CLI on Mac](project_fireflies-cli-broken-on-mac.md) — \"out of memory (14)\", getting transcript text, renames that don't stick\n- [gws Google Forms API](project_gws-forms-api.md) — scopes, create vs batchUpdate, duplicated items\n- [Kenny Boyd doc review](project_kenny-boyd-doc-review-preferences.md) — tone and labelling rules for review docs\n- [Terminal Fluency workspace](project_terminal-fluency-workspace.md) — resume protocol and how he learns CLI\n- [PATH shadowing and cleanup](project_path-shadowing-and-cleanup.md) — measuring PATH honestly; before deleting any binary\n- [Backup/sync system](project_backup-sync-system.md) — manifest routing, verify-by-receipt, schedule history\n- [CFB Analytics Lab](project_cfb-analytics-lab.md) — resume protocol and CFBD API traps\n- [CFB forecast capture](project_cfb-forecast-capture.md) — Big 12 weekly freeze; one wrapper command; is a gap really a missed week?\n- [Drive FUSE rsync loop](project_drive-fuse-rsync-mtime-loop.md) — rsync re-sending identical files to Google Drive\n- [SecondBrain vault location](project_secondbrain-vault-location.md) — current path and what hardcodes it\n- [Milvus lock misdiagnosis](project_milvus-lock-misdiagnosis.md) — Zotero/Milvus errors; diagnose before rebuilding\n- [Memory compiler pipeline](project_memory-compiler-pipeline.md) — dead-flush tells, backfilling a date, \"Indexed 0 chunks\"\n- [memsearch index path drift](project_memsearch-index-path-drift.md) — silent fallback to fewer indexed paths\n- [memsearch hook timeout cap](project_memsearch-hook-timeout-cap.md) — slow or timed-out index; check Mac sleep (`pmset -g log`) first, then corpus growth\n- [Morning briefing retired](project_morning-briefing-retired.md) — where the code, briefings and config went\n- [graphify runtime setup](project_graphify-runtime-setup.md) — venv location, install flags, rebuild triggers\n- [graphify semantic extraction](project_graphify-semantic-extraction.md) — quota traps and silent 429s\n- [Automation cadence](project_automation-cadence.md) — ritual times, reindex, audit due dates, gws/Gmail limits\n- [S2 Cognition analysis](project_s2-cognition-analysis.md) — norms scope, battery size, threshold discipline\n- [Sports teams](user_sports-teams.md) — default team for ambiguous WNBA asks\n- [Working-conditions bottleneck](user_working-conditions-bottleneck.md) — what limits him; the permission gate on acting unasked\n- [Zero-edit YouTube pipeline](reference_zero-edit-youtube-pipeline.md) — Jack Roberts' Claude Code video system\n- [DEV layout and discovery](project_dev-layout-and-discovery.md) — folder rules, depth-2 scan limit, gitignore trap\n- [KeystoneOS verification habits](project_keystoneos-verification-habits.md) — which state claims mislead; re-check before asserting; morning-cadence dies on a `C:\\` or URL `canonicalPath` in projects.json\n- [Portfolio registry provenance](project_portfolio-registry-write-provenance.md) — who writes portfolio.json; how to commit changes\n- [Command centre](project_command-centre.md) — localhost:7373 front door + the /os board, daemon health, backup default-deny\n- [Vendored skill install](project_vendored-skill-install.md) — installing a third-party skill for Claude Code + Codex + Cowork; the `-a` flag trap\n- [Vendored skill repair](project_vendored-skill-repair.md) — a skill returns empty output; which copy is actually live; patches that revert\n- [Personal site build routes](project_personal-site-build-routes.md) — Artifact constraints vs scroll-craft; where the standalone build lives\n- [Book scan page ordering](project_book-scan-page-ordering.md) — phone-scanned book pages out of order; folio OCR lies, read a contact sheet\n- [Ingest review gate](feedback_ingest-review-gate.md) — before ingest-book or any expensive atomization; what the arithmetic gate does and does not catch\n- [Dormant threads](project_dormant-threads.md) — Tezino CampPrep and FaceComps sim: where each stopped and what is still open\n- [RTP CLA course](project_rtp-cla-course.md) — book ingest state, the +13 page offset, the one rebuild still outstanding\n- [Post-merge cleanup](feedback_post-merge-cleanup.md) — standing OK to delete merged worktrees/branches unasked; verify-first rule; Windows read-only \"Permission denied\" fix\n- [SCN sprint course](project_scn-sprint-course.md) — Moyer/Natera sprint course: where the 58 library entries and review live, RTP-stack role, open decisions\n- [ATH Tech framework](project_ath-tech-framework.md) — the shipped 08-14 doc set, the five unwritten gaps, the ITS blocker\n- [Supervisor log pipeline](project_supervisor-log-pipeline.md) — nightly log feeding the Kenny/Chris report; action-items-are-not-outcomes trap\n- [dcg guard blocks](project_dcg-guard-blocks.md) — a Bash command was rejected outright; which shapes trip it and the rewrite that works\n- [Headless tool-deny gap](project_headless-claude-tool-deny-gap.md) — `claude -p --disallowedTools Bash` still runs shell via context-mode MCP; before calling a headless run read-only\n- [Config encoding damage from Windows](project_settings-json-bom-trap.md) — dcg cries \"hook missing\", or the statusline bars render as `â–“` junk; one bad write causes both, in settings.json AND CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md\n- [Tech inventory refresh](project_tech-inventory-refresh.md) — which forms platform is live, why gws cannot pull responses, the stale OneDrive copy, reminder sent 9/8, the refresh command\n- [Brain audit loop](project_brain-audit-loop.md) — scoring the second brain; why manual rituals die and cron ones live\n- [Hook two-copies trap](project_hook-two-copies-trap.md) — a hook patch \"reverts\"; `.claude/hooks/` and `.codex/hooks/` both need it\n- [Child dental plan](user_child-dental-plan.md) — 4yo cavities: pediatric dentist not yet booked, SDF vs n-HAp\n- [ELI5 output style](user_eli5-output-style.md) — concise plain-language output rules shared by Claude Code and Codex\n- [GitHub consolidation](project_github-consolidation.md) — ~/DEV on GitHub: the thin root repo, _ARCHIVE, where DexaProject went, merge traps\n- [Skill bloat and promotion](project_skill-bloat-and-promotion.md) — before touching ANY skill folder: which of the three copies is canonical, why `~/.claude/skills` IS a git repo, the sync script that deletes, the 2026-09-12 audit, the 40 name-only skills (9/14), Pi/pi.dev facts\n- [Prioritization slate](project_prioritization-slate.md) — Eric proposes a new project or idea; the cap, charters, slate-check timer, clearing a nag with mark-done\n- [Baylor department names](user_baylor-department-names.md) — Athletic Performance / Athletic Medicine / Applied Performance, never S&C / sports medicine / sport science\n- [Git Bash MSYS/AD stall](project_git-bash-msys-ad-stall.md) — why bash randomly dies with `add_item errno 1`: AD lookup exceeds MSYS2's 15s spinlock; fix via nsswitch files-only + `shell: powershell` hooks\n- [Backup task sleep-kill signature](project_backup-task-sleepkill-signature.md) — DailyBackup exit 3221225786 + same-day clean log = benign sleep interruption, not rot\n- [Sport-science thought partner](user_sport-science-thought-partner.md) — why he feeds in Oldbull/SCN/SportSmith/CLA; how to answer performance, S&C, rehab questions\n- [Third-party skill install](project_third-party-skill-install.md) — clone to ~/.agents/skills + symlink to 4 harnesses; Windows `ln -s` copies silently; sync-to-codex clears the pre-commit/backup jam\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"45697cdb-a944-4bd2-99a9-1c8927c50145","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"session_context","context":{"userEmail":"The user's email address is eric.rash11@gmail.com. Use it only to identify the user, such as for authorship, attribution, or filtering their own work. Never send it to an unrelated service, such as in a request header, URL, or payload, unless the user explicitly asks.","gitStatus":"This is the git status at the start of the conversation. Note that this status is a snapshot in time, and will not update during the conversation.\n\nCurrent branch: main\n\nMain branch (you will usually use this for PRs): main\n\nGit user: Eric Rash\n\nStatus:\n(clean)\n\nRecent commits:\nbff0bed Route Flint in RESEARCH.md\n39c188e Record Codex hook timeout diagnosis\n779e33c Mark SecondBrain vault commit pushed\nd6ff679 Record research closeout blockers\na7915f7 Link research library closeout handoff"}},"type":"attachment","uuid":"3dd71d2b-3d33-41ba-8036-3e4e500c8bc3","timestamp":"2026-09-28T12:59:27.036Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nAs you answer the user's questions, you can use the following context:\n# userEmail\nThe user's email address is eric.rash11@gmail.com. Use it only to identify the user, such as for authorship, attribution, or filtering their own work. Never send it to an unrelated service, such as in a request header, URL, or payload, unless the user explicitly asks.\n# gitStatus\nThis is the git status at the start of the conversation. Note that this status is a snapshot in time, and will not update during the conversation.\n\nCurrent branch: main\n\nMain branch (you will usually use this for PRs): main\n\nGit user: Eric Rash\n\nStatus:\n(clean)\n\nRecent commits:\nbff0bed Route Flint in RESEARCH.md\n39c188e Record Codex hook timeout diagnosis\n779e33c Mark SecondBrain vault commit pushed\nd6ff679 Record research closeout blockers\na7915f7 Link research library closeout handoff\n\nIMPORTANT: this context may or may not be relevant to your tasks. You should not respond to this context unless it is highly relevant to your task.\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"main"}
