{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-09-28T14:50:46.064Z","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ab3eed17c19656afd</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01BJNZh4xwuXBTMW7Zyqdkp3</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-eric-rash-Desktop-DEV\\e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58\\tasks\\ab3eed17c19656afd.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Find restart commands for stopped jobs\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Only one of the three stopped jobs still needs running: the whole-Drive catalog. A later session re-ran the OCR and the transcription on 2026-09-27, and both finished. Nothing was run or changed during this check.\n\nThe final message (2026-09-27T04:51Z) lists exactly three jobs. All three were killed at 04:51:13–16Z with the note \"system is running low on memory\", and none of them failed on its own. The day-1 handoff also mentions a \"search re-index\" job, but nothing in the transcript shows it being killed.\n\nAll three jobs belong to the **Flint** repo, worktree `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage`, branch `step1-triage`. The worktree still exists. Every command had `cwd` = that worktree. File times below are local time (UTC−5).\n\n| Job (task id) | Original launch | Script (exists?) | Input | Output now | Resume? | Memory / runtime |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **1. OCR chain** (bpns07spf, 02:43Z) | Three `uv run --quiet scripts/ocr_pdf.py \"&lt;pdf&gt;\" --out staging/ocr/&lt;slug&gt;.md` calls chained with `;`: Francis *Training for Power and Strength in Speed*, then CFTS, then Zatsiorsky 2e | `scripts/ocr_pdf.py` (yes). RapidOCR on onnxruntime, CPU, 200 dpi | PDFs on G: (TB snapshot and AP &amp; Sports Science) | **Done.** `francis-training-for-power-and-strength-in-speed.md` (09-26 22:06). `cfts--francis.md` (218 pages in 99 min, finished 09-27 10:06). Zatsiorsky 2e in four chunk files `science-practice-strength-2e--zatsiorsky--p{1-62,63-124,125-186,187-247}.md` (finished 09-27 12:00) | `ocr_pdf.py` only writes when a book is finished. The newer `scripts/ocr_zatsiorsky_2e_chunks.sh` skips chunks that already exist | Moderate: one page at a time. About 20 min per 60 pages; about 2.5 h for CFTS, about 3 h for Zatsiorsky |\n| **2. Landon's CVASPS talk transcription** (b4qvkww1n, 01:56Z) | `uv run --quiet scripts/transcribe.py &lt;Evans file in G:\\My Drive\\00_Primary_Storage_Drive\\09_PRO_DEV\\Conference_Materials\\CVASPS&gt;` | `scripts/transcribe.py` (yes). faster-whisper **medium.en**, CPU, int8, VAD, beam 5 | `staging/audio/landonevansphysicalpreparationinthencaaacomplementaryapproach.wav` (131 MB, present) | **Done.** `staging/transcripts/landonevans...md` (09-27 15:08): 68.3 min of audio, took 90.2 min | Writes only at the end, so a kill means starting over | Heavy: medium model plus beam search. About 3 h before the kill, about 1.5 h when run alone |\n| **3. Whole-Drive catalog** (bpw8yexj6, 01:49Z) | `cd step1-triage; pwsh -NoProfile -File scripts/build_manifest.ps1 *&gt; data/run_wide.log; uv run --quiet scripts/triage_rules.py \\| Select -First 3` | `scripts/build_manifest.ps1` (yes), metadata only through the `gws` Drive API | `config/roots.json`: 24 roots plus the shared-folder patterns | **Not done.** `data/run_wide.log` stops at \"Listing 01_PROJECTS ...\" (last write 09-26 21:06). `data/manifest.csv` and `manifest_summary.md` are still the 09-26 19:33 run (17,857 files, 11,654 unique) | **Starts over.** It collects every row in memory and only writes `manifest.csv` and the summary at the very end. There is no resume | Light on CPU and RAM (API calls plus a list held in PowerShell), but slow: it was still inside 01_PROJECTS after about 2h45m, with 13 roots after it |\n\n**Ready-to-run commands.** Jobs 1 and 2 are already done, so none of these is needed for them. If one ever had to be redone, this is the run-one-at-a-time wrapper already in the repo (it skips finished Zatsiorsky chunks, then transcribes). From Git Bash, cwd `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage`:\n```\nbash scripts/run_ocr_then_transcribe.sh\n```\nThe CFTS book alone (only if it had to be redone), in PowerShell with the same cwd:\n```\n&amp; \"$HOME\\.local\\bin\\uv.exe\" run --quiet scripts/ocr_pdf.py \"G:\\My Drive\\Athletic Performance &amp; Sports Science\\Shared Reference Library\\From Landon Evans\\Eric Files\\Physical Preparation\\Books\\CFTS - Charlie Francis.pdf\" --out staging/ocr/cfts--francis.md *&gt; staging/ocr/_run_cfts.log\n```\nJob 3, the one that is still outstanding, in PowerShell with the same cwd:\n```\npwsh -NoProfile -File scripts/build_manifest.ps1 *&gt; data/run_wide.log\n&amp; \"$HOME\\.local\\bin\\uv.exe\" run --quiet scripts/triage_rules.py\n```\n\n**Running at the same time on a 16 GB laptop with about 2 GB free.** Since only the catalog is left, there is nothing to run alongside it. On its own the catalog is light and should fit in about 2 GB. What really matters is not having several agents and other heavy work open while it runs. If OCR or transcription ever had to be redone, never run them together: medium.en transcription and OCR together are what exhausted memory. `scripts/run_ocr_then_transcribe.sh` already runs them one after the other.\n\n**Before re-running the catalog:**\n- **It replaces the catalog your approval is based on.** It overwrites `data/manifest.csv` and the committed `data/manifest_summary.md`. The follow-on `triage_rules.py` step then rewrites `data/triage.csv`. The Day 3 shortlists awaiting your approval were built from the current triage, so hold the re-run until you have approved the shortlists, or accept that the base changes.\n- **Don't use `-Only` for this.** That flag lists just one root and still overwrites the manifest.\n- **Keep the session open.** Background jobs die with the session (see the STATUS gotchas), and this run could take many hours.\n\n**Still waiting on you, from the final message and `docs/STATUS.md`:**\n- Approve the shortlists, then move the approved sources into `docs/QUEUE.md`.\n- The CVASPS transcript is an unchecked draft. Its 47 flagged speech-recognition spots need listening before anything from it is cited.\n- The OCR script needs better column and rotation handling before the next scanned book. Zatsiorsky 2e is missing about 2 printed pages in Ch10 and most of the Glossary and Suggested Readings.\n- On the Mac: `git pull` in `~/.claude/skills`, then run the sync script.\n- STATUS now answers the worktree question from day 1: enter `step1-triage` by path.\n\nThe day-1 review items listed as open in the closeout (Old Bull, Issurin 1–2, *Supertraining* Ch1 round 2) now have `fixes_*_applied.md` files in `staging/`, so later sessions appear to have closed them.\n\nThe main files are `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\STATUS.md` and the scripts under `...\\step1-triage\\scripts\\` (`build_manifest.ps1`, `ocr_pdf.py`, `transcribe.py`, `run_ocr_then_transcribe.sh`, `ocr_zatsiorsky_2e_chunks.sh`).</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>95280</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>15</tool_uses><duration_ms>163946</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
