{"parentUuid":"0d1eecff-db03-4c04-a895-4eba1b49b035","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"b6ccbd46-52be-47e2-9d53-733ab42f068c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Base directory for this skill: C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\.claude\\skills\\interview-me\n\n# Interview Me\r\n\r\nEnd-of-workday synthesis ritual. The user is a sport-science / performance analyst who writes essays connecting sport science, product strategy, and decision-making. The interview's job is to extract publishable insights, not to record what happened.\r\n\r\n## Vault location\r\n\r\nUse `vault_root` from the selected platform config.\r\n\r\n**Paths:** canonical vault locations live in `~/.claude/skills/_shared/config.<platform>.json` — load it if unsure; if a literal path here ever disagrees, the config wins.\r\n\r\n- `Inbox/` — full transcripts (one per day)\r\n- `Notes/` — atomic permanent notes (one idea per file)\r\n- `Drafts/` — essays in progress\r\n- `Archive/` — published\r\n- `Templates/interview-note.md`, `Templates/permanent-note.md`\r\n- `Inbox/status-log/` — daily supervisor fact logs (Part 0). Path key: `vault_status_log`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Part 0 — Supervisor log (90 seconds, ask first)\r\n\r\n**Three questions, asked before the reflection questions.** Facts are easier to recall\r\nthan insight when the user is tired, and a hard 90-second opener is what makes the ritual\r\nsurvivable nightly. Reflection quality does not suffer from coming second; it suffers\r\nfrom the user never starting.\r\n\r\nThis part exists because `status-report` — the biweekly email to the user's supervisors —\r\nhas a sourcing gap: roughly two thirds of what belongs in that email (equipment arriving,\r\ninstalls, who onboarded onto what, hallway conversations, follow-ups awaiting a reply)\r\nappears in **no** automated source. Without a nightly log, the report has to reconstruct\r\ntwo weeks from memory. With one, it mostly writes itself.\r\n\r\nAsk all three at once. Accept short answers. Do not probe.\r\n\r\n> Quick log first, 90 seconds. Then the usual four.\r\n> 1. Anything arrive, get installed, or change hands today? Who's onboarding onto it?\r\n> 2. Who did you talk to that Kenny or Chris would want to know about? Names and sport.\r\n> 3. Waiting on anyone, or anything about to go sideways?\r\n\r\n**Honest zero rule.** If all three are genuinely \"nothing\", write **no file**. A day with\r\nno operational facts is a real day. Padding this log is worse than skipping it, because\r\n`status-report` trusts these entries verbatim.\r\n\r\n### Output — `{vault_status_log}/YYYY-MM-DD.md`\r\n\r\n```markdown\r\n---\r\ndate: YYYY-MM-DD\r\nsports: [Football, Tennis]      # programs named in the answers; [] if none\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Equipment & installs\r\n- Perch cameras installed at Foster. Football onboarding this week.\r\n\r\n## People & conversations\r\n- Javi (Tennis) — FirstBeat onboarding, wants a one-pager for his staff.\r\n\r\n## Waiting on / risks\r\n- Micah, no reply since 8/17.\r\n```\r\n\r\nUse the user's own words. Do not editorialize, rank, or add commentary — `status-report`\r\ndoes the judgement pass later, and it needs raw facts to judge.\r\n\r\n**Append, never overwrite.** If today's file exists (a second debrief, or a correction),\r\nadd to the existing sections rather than replacing the file.\r\n\r\n**This log never becomes a permanent note.** It is a fact record for one downstream\r\nconsumer. Insight goes in `Notes/` via Part 1, which is why the skill's \"NOT a journaling\r\napp\" rule still holds.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Part 0b — Project block (30 seconds, right after Part 0)\r\n\r\nThe user works a 2-hour calendar block per slate project each week (rules:\r\n`SecondBrain/Projects/Applied-Performance-Operating-Cadence.md`). This question is the only\r\nthing that records whether a block happened; the Friday weekly report counts it, so skipping\r\nit makes a worked week look empty.\r\n\r\nRun `python3 ~/DEV/project-triage/portfolio.py slate-check` first to get the active project\r\nnames. Then ask once:\r\n\r\n> Project block: which slate project did you work on today, what moved, and did you finish a step?\r\n\r\n- Each project named → `python3 ~/DEV/project-triage/portfolio.py add-update <Project> \"[block] <what moved, in the user's words>\"`.\r\n  The exact `[block]` prefix is what the weekly report counts.\r\n- A step finished → confirm the step name back, then `python3 ~/DEV/project-triage/portfolio.py mark-done <Project> --step N`\r\n  (charter order: 1 plan agreed, 2 first draft, 3 final delivered, 4 in use).\r\n- \"None\" is a real answer — write nothing.\r\n- Work on something **not** on the slate is not a block. If it was a new idea, offer\r\n  `/project idea` and say it waits for a free slot.\r\n- Match names case-insensitively; ask if two projects match. On `[error]`, show it verbatim\r\n  and carry on with Part 1.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Part 1 — The interview\r\n\r\n**Time budget:** 10–15 minutes total including Part 0. If the user wants to keep talking, schedule a follow-up session — don't blow the budget.\r\n\r\n**Opening line (verbatim is fine):**\r\n> Now the four. Then I write up anything that earned its place.\r\n\r\n### Question 1 — Orientation (filter)\r\n> What did you spend time on today?\r\n\r\nListen for themes. Don't try to capture everything. Look for: a hard decision, a piece of work that consumed a lot of time, anything that felt different from yesterday.\r\n\r\n### Question 2 — Signal (insight detection)\r\n> What surprised you, frustrated you, or changed your mind?\r\n\r\nThis is the gold question. Genuine surprise, frustration, or updated belief = the moments worth writing down. If the answer is \"nothing,\" probe once: *\"Nothing at all? Even small recalibrations count.\"* If still nothing, accept and move on — not every day produces signal.\r\n\r\n### Question 3 — Pattern (synthesis)\r\n> Does anything from today connect to what you were thinking about earlier this week or recently?\r\n\r\n**Optional aid:** quietly Glob `Notes/*.md` modified in the last 7 days; if you find 2–3 candidates that match today's themes, name them and ask if today's experience updates them.\r\n\r\n### Question 4 — Essay seed (publishable insight)\r\n> If you told a peer one thing about today's work, what would it be?\r\n\r\nThis question converts experience into transferable insight. If the user gives a great one-liner — capture it verbatim as the seed for a permanent note.\r\n\r\n## Optional source pull-ins\r\n\r\nOffer when the user references them. Don't pull unprompted.\r\n\r\n- **Meeting referenced** (\"today's call with X\", \"this morning's standup\"):\r\n  → **First, check `Inbox/meetings/` for a local summary** — Glob for files matching the date or participant. If found, read and quote from the local file (free, fast, no API call).\r\n  → If not found locally, ask: *\"Want me to pull from Fireflies?\"* → invoke the `meeting-sync` skill to sync the latest meetings, then read the local file.\r\n  → Fall back to direct `pp-fireflies` only if `meeting-sync` reports nothing matching.\r\n- **Paper / article referenced:**\r\n  → \"Want me to pull the source from Zotero?\" → use `pp-zotero-research-library` skill.\r\n- **GPS / Catapult data referenced:**\r\n  → \"Want me to pull the underlying metric?\" → use `pp-catapult-openfield-connect` skill.\r\n\r\nQuote sources in the note's frontmatter `source:` field or the **Evidence / origin** section.\r\n\r\n## Output protocol\r\n\r\n### Write the supervisor log (unless all three answers were nothing)\r\n\r\n`Inbox/status-log/YYYY-MM-DD.md` — see Part 0 for the shape. Write this **first**, before\r\nthe transcript, so a session that gets cut short still leaves the report its facts.\r\n\r\n### Always write the transcript\r\n\r\n`Inbox/YYYY-MM-DD-interview.md` — full Q&A, lightly cleaned. Use the `Templates/interview-note.md` skeleton. Fill in `themes`, `notes_produced`, `pulled_sources` in frontmatter.\r\n\r\n### Write 0–3 permanent notes\r\n\r\n**Bias toward fewer notes.** A vault full of mediocre notes is worse than one with three great ones. If nothing crossed the bar today, write zero — say so to the user. They will respect this.\r\n\r\nA note earns its place if it meets at least one of:\r\n- States a transferable claim (works beyond today's context)\r\n- Updates a previously held view (in which case, also update / link the old note)\r\n- Names a pattern across two or more recent days\r\n- Is a clean essay seed the user could draft from later\r\n\r\nEach note goes to `Notes/<concept-title-kebab-case>.md` with the `Templates/permanent-note.md` skeleton filled in:\r\n- `created`: today's date\r\n- `source`: `[[YYYY-MM-DD-interview]]`\r\n- `domain`: pick from `sport-science`, `product`, `decision-making`, `leadership`, `methodology`, `personal`\r\n- `tags`: free-form, but reuse existing tags from other Notes when possible\r\n- Body: claim in 1–2 sentences, why it matters, evidence/origin, connections (use `[[wikilink]]` to related Notes), open questions\r\n\r\n### Memory bridge (when warranted)\r\n\r\nIf the interview surfaces a **durable** fact about the user, their projects, or working style that should persist across Claude sessions:\r\n- Also write a Tier A memory file via the `memory-write` skill (it derives the correct project slug from the current working directory) or update USER.md / project_*.md as appropriate.\r\n- Tell the user explicitly: \"Also saved this to memory as <name>.\"\r\n- Do NOT bridge ephemeral notes. Most permanent notes stay in the vault only.\r\n\r\n## What NOT to do\r\n\r\n- **Don't pad notes with model commentary.** The user's own words and insights are the value. You are a scribe with editorial judgment, not a co-author.\r\n- **Don't ask follow-up questions beyond the four** unless an answer was genuinely ambiguous and one clarification unlocks a much better note.\r\n- **Don't produce a note \"just to have one.\"** Zero notes is an honest output.\r\n- **Don't break the 10–15 min budget.** Offer a follow-up session if needed.\r\n- **Don't write to `Drafts/` from a daily interview.** Drafts come out of weekly synthesis, not daily capture.\r\n- **Don't pad the supervisor log to make the day look productive.** `status-report` copies these entries into an email to the user's supervisors with light editing. An invented install or an inflated conversation becomes a false claim in front of Chris Ruf and Kenny Boyd. No file is the correct output for a quiet day.\r\n- **Don't turn a Part 0 answer into a permanent note** unless it independently clears the Part 1 bar. Operational facts are not insight.\r\n\r\n## Gotchas\r\n\r\n- **Part 0 goes first, and that ordering is the whole design.** The ritual lapsed for 49 days in 2026 because reflection is expensive when tired. Three factual questions is a low enough floor to clear on a bad day, and a partial run that captures only the log still has value downstream. Moving Part 0 after the four questions quietly reverts the fix.\r\n- **`status-report` reads this directory verbatim.** It counts logged days against the period (\"N of 14 days logged\") and surfaces the gap to the user. That visible gap is the intended nudge — do not backfill missing days with guesses to make the count look better.\r\n- **Never hardcode the log path.** It is `vault_status_log` in `~/.claude/skills/_shared/config.<platform>.json`. The vault has already moved once (off the iCloud-synced Desktop, 2026-08-14) and every hardcoded path broke.\r\n- **Sports named in the answers must go in the `sports:` frontmatter array.** The status report's sport coverage panel — the reason that report exists, since it documents involvement beyond football and basketball — is assembled from this field. A conversation logged without its sport is credit the user does not get.\r\n- **Append to today's file, never overwrite it.** A second debrief on the same day is common and overwriting silently deletes the first pass.\r\n\r\n## Weekly synthesis\r\n\r\nWeekly synthesis is NOT this skill's job. If the user says \"weekly synthesis\", \"weekly review\", or asks to look across the week, invoke the `weekly-review` skill — it owns the Friday ritual (themes, project status, decisions, stale action items, essay angles). This skill is strictly the daily debrief.\r\n\r\n## End-of-session sign-off\r\n\r\nAlways end with a brief summary:\r\n- Supervisor log: saved to `Inbox/status-log/YYYY-MM-DD.md`, or \"nothing to log today\"\r\n- Transcript saved: `Inbox/YYYY-MM-DD-interview.md`\r\n- Notes created: list with paths and one-line summaries\r\n- Memory bridge: yes/no, what\r\n- Any open thread to revisit tomorrow\r\n\r\nKeep it tight — three to five lines.\r\n"}]},"isMeta":true,"turnCompanion":true,"uuid":"b5d6f889-bfd2-442f-99e1-2a97bc457715","timestamp":"2026-09-28T14:54:41.962Z","sourceToolUseID":"toolu_01J4dHxPFDr6vqJYpXgsnCSN","session_id":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\TypeSafe_Sandbox\\.claude\\worktrees\\drive-triage-run","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"worktree-drive-triage-run"}
