{"parentUuid":"24834391-9d04-45b7-a829-61ab3ab59e06","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"b6ccbd46-52be-47e2-9d53-733ab42f068c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01N3HXFurcL5SFZ4JWWVGLon","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Flint interview prep: decision rules\n2\t\n3\tPurpose: make Eric's later `/interview-me` session short and pointed. This pulls from `docs/method-baseline.md` — the questions below target places where the documents are silent, where a \"when X, I do Y, because Z\" rule is implied but never stated, where two documents disagree, or where individual authorship of a position (Rash vs. Ruf vs. Wenger) cannot be confirmed from the files alone. It skips questions the documents already answer clearly and confidently.\n4\t\n5\t## Top 8 (highest priority, in order)\n6\t\n7\t1. **Ratifying the course material.** (Settled 2026-09-26: the course is co-taught by Chris Ruf, you and Mike Wenger, and the lectures are split among the three of you. What remains is which positions are yours.) Both HHPR 5355 class transcripts are diarized entirely under one Fireflies speaker label (\"Chris Ruf\") for every line, including the Feb. 5 class the syllabus assigns solo to you. Which specific positions from that course material — the Vermeil hierarchy as your compass, undulating over linear periodization, the Dynamic Correspondence framework, the energy-system priority stack for football — are actually yours, versus material you present because it's the shared course curriculum?\n8\t2. **The mRSI daily-readiness rule.** You state that force-plate mRSI replaced subjective wellness surveys as your daily readiness marker because it's \"objective, consistent, and actionable.\" What value or week-over-week change in mRSI actually triggers a training adjustment, and for whom?\n9\t3. **The test-retirement threshold.** You retired roster-wide NordBord testing and daily wellness surveys, each for a stated reason (time cost and staff turnover; athletes gaming the survey). Is there a general rule you'd apply to the next technology or protocol decision, or is each call made fresh?\n10\t4. **RTP decision ownership and criteria.** The Playbook says Applied Performance \"supports\" Athletic Medicine's RTP decisions. What specific criteria, battery, or sign-off do you actually use or contribute, and where does your input end and Athletic Medicine's begin?\n11\t5. **Department reporting model.** Is Applied Performance a peer discipline that enables Athletics Performance/Athletic Medicine/Nutrition (Playbook framing), or is it in-house programming support for the Athletics Performance staff (course-narration framing: \"helping program and plan the training\")? These may not actually conflict, but the documents state both.\n12\t6. **Coach's-eye versus objective-first.** The course material argues you don't need a fancy test if you have a good coach's eye, and criticizes load-monitoring culture for breeding fear-driven under-training. Your own writing moved the opposite way on daily monitoring, replacing subjective self-report with an objective force-plate number. Where do you actually draw that line?\n13\t7. **GPS/load thresholds per sport.** Your own gap analysis says the data dictionary has no sport-specific, contextualized thresholds yet. Do any exist informally, even if undocumented?\n14\t8. **Assessment Manual currency.** The AP Assessment Manual (V12) references GymAware and a contact-mat CMJ; the current stack centers on Perch and ForceDecks-only CMJ. Is the Manual current, partially superseded, or fully legacy?\n15\t\n16\t---\n17\t\n18\t## Philosophy and department model\n19\t\n20\t*(Q1, Q5 above also belong here.)*\n21\t\n22\t- **Q:** Is there a standing exception to any of the \"five expectations\" culture rules (Golden Rule, Be Disciplined, Be Relentless, Communicate, No Excuses) — a situation where you'd deliberately relax one? **Docs say/imply:** the five expectations are presented as absolute, without a stated exception case [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.1-2]. **Why it matters for Flint:** absolute-sounding culture rules often have an unstated boundary condition; Flint should know it before applying the rule literally in a synthesis note.\n23\t- **Q:** When an outside framework (e.g., from Old Bull or SCN) conflicts with the \"we/our, not me/my\" team-ownership model, how do you handle attribution and credit in practice, especially with staff who are new or on loan from another unit? **Docs say/imply:** the principle is stated as a value, not a practice [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.5]. **Why it matters for Flint:** this shapes how Flint should phrase recommendations that touch on staff roles.\n24\t\n25\t## Testing and assessment\n26\t\n27\t- **Q:** (Priority 8, restated) Is the AP Assessment Manual V12 current, or superseded by the Force Plate Testing V3 protocol and the Perch-centered stack? **Docs say/imply:** the two documents specify different equipment (GymAware/contact-mat vs. ForceDecks-only) for overlapping tests [ap-assessment-manual-draft-v12.md; revised-force-plate-testing-v3.md — Tension 4]. **Why it matters for Flint:** several \"Testing\" and \"Force plate\" bullets in the baseline need relabeling from current practice to legacy if the Manual is out of date.\n28\t- **Q:** What's the actual current testing cadence (not just the battery) for each sport in the V4 Assessment Table — has it changed since that table was built? **Docs say/imply:** the table lists which tests apply to which sport but states no frequency or version date [update-baylor-sport-assessment-table-v4.md]. **Why it matters for Flint:** cadence is as much a decision rule as the battery itself, and it's the part most likely to have drifted.\n29\t- **Q:** What specific training-age or experience marker moves an athlete from repetition-max testing to true 1RM testing? **Docs say/imply:** the rule is stated only as \"intermediate to advanced athletes\" with no defined threshold [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.18]. **Why it matters for Flint:** \"when X, I do Y\" is implied but the X is undefined.\n30\t\n31\t## Force plate and neuromuscular monitoring\n32\t\n33\t*(Q2 above also belongs here.)*\n34\t\n35\t- **Q:** The AP Assessment Manual says \"do not coach the strategy of jumping\" during CMJ testing, but the Force Plate Testing V3 protocol gives specific movement cues (\"keep chest up,\" \"land softly\") during the same family of tests. How do you reconcile the two, or has the newer protocol simply replaced the older instruction? **Docs say/imply:** both documents are silent on each other [ap-assessment-manual-draft-v12.md; revised-force-plate-testing-v3.md — Tension 5]. **Why it matters for Flint:** testing-cue discipline is exactly the kind of granular protocol detail an RTP or testing synthesis note needs to get right.\n36\t\n37\t## Load monitoring and GPS\n38\t\n39\t*(Q7 above also belongs here.)*\n40\t\n41\t- **Q:** Which system is the actual system of record when Kitman Labs and VALD Hub (or OpenField Cloud) disagree on a number? **Docs say/imply:** Kitman Labs is named as the central data store, but each vendor platform also generates its own reports, and no tie-breaker rule is stated [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.7]. **Why it matters for Flint:** any future data-reconciliation question needs a known source of truth.\n42\t- **Q:** Do you use acute:chronic workload ratio (ACWR) or any similar load-based injury-risk metric in practice, or deliberately avoid it given the mixed evidence discussed in the reference (Clemson) curriculum? **Docs say/imply:** ACWR is discussed at length in the Clemson reference material but never mentioned in any Baylor-authored document [intencurriculumn.md pp.140-141 vs. absence elsewhere]. **Why it matters for Flint:** this is a specific, well-known framework outside sources will likely raise; Flint needs to know whether you've adopted, rejected, or never addressed it.\n43\t\n44\t## Speed and sprint\n45\t\n46\t- **Q:** The V4 Assessment Table lists \"Change of Direction\" as an assessment for several sports, but no COD testing protocol appears anywhere in this corpus. What protocol do you actually use, if any? **Docs say/imply:** COD appears only as a column header with no accompanying method [update-baylor-sport-assessment-table-v4.md]. **Why it matters for Flint:** Speed and sprint is otherwise reasonably well covered (linear sprint splits, SmartSpeed); COD is the one clear hole.\n47\t\n48\t## Strength and power\n49\t\n50\t- **Q:** Is undulating (Parker/Miller/Panariello) periodization still your default over linear periodization today, and for which sports or training blocks do you deviate from it? **Docs say/imply:** a strong first-person preference statement exists, but its speaker cannot be confirmed as you specifically [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.24-26 — Tension 9]. **Why it matters for Flint:** this is a named, specific methodological preference that would otherwise anchor every strength-programming synthesis note.\n51\t- **Q:** What's your actual in-season strength-maintenance dosage (sets/reps/%1RM) by sport, beyond the general \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rule? **Docs say/imply:** the rule is stated in general terms only, with example ranges (3-5 sets, 2-6 reps, 50-80%) that aren't tied to a specific sport [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.21; hhpr-5355-class-8-2026.md p.21]. **Why it matters for Flint:** sport-specific numbers, not just the general principle, are what a synthesis note would need to compare against an outside source's in-season recommendations.\n52\t\n53\t## Conditioning and energy systems\n54\t\n55\t- **Q:** Has the football glycolytic-conditioning approach (\"worst case scenario,\" trained incidentally through camp) actually held up in practice, or do you now program dedicated glycolytic work under specific circumstances? **Docs say/imply:** the stated plan treats glycolytic conditioning as something to avoid programming directly [class-4-2026.md pp.34, 39]. **Why it matters for Flint:** this is a specific, falsifiable claim about current practice that's easy to check and update.\n56\t- **Q:** Which anaerobic-threshold heart-rate method do you actually use day to day: the age-based formula or the body-size-based table? Both appear back to back in the same slide deck. **Docs say/imply:** neither slide states which supersedes the other [class-4-2026.md pp.27-29 — Tension 6]. **Why it matters for Flint:** this is a concrete, checkable methodological detail that affects every HR-zone-based conditioning recommendation.\n57\t\n58\t## Rehab and return to play\n59\t\n60\t*(Q4 above also belongs here — this is the thinnest discipline in the corpus.)*\n61\t\n62\t- **Q:** What closed-skill (strength, balance, hop testing) and open-skill (agility) benchmarks do you require, or recommend requiring, before clearing an athlete for full sport-specific work post-injury? **Docs say/imply:** nothing — RTP appears only as a named responsibility, never as a criterion set [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.6]. **Why it matters for Flint:** per the project plan, Rehab and RTP is slated as the first synthesis-note discipline; this baseline currently has nothing to compare an outside source against.\n63\t- **Q:** Is there any written phased return-to-sport protocol, even an informal one, or does RTP get built case-by-case with Athletic Medicine each time? **Docs say/imply:** no such document exists in this corpus. **Why it matters for Flint:** without this, Flint cannot tell whether an outside RTP framework (e.g., Harries) would be filling a real gap or duplicating existing practice.\n64\t- **Q:** In RTP scenarios specifically, how do the retained tools (subjective wellness surveys, targeted NordBord, isometric ForceFrame testing) actually get combined into a single readiness decision? **Docs say/imply:** each tool's retained use is stated individually; none describe how they're integrated [2026-02-19 and 2026-02-20 sportsmith pieces]. **Why it matters for Flint:** integration logic, not just tool inventory, is what a synthesis note needs.\n65\t\n66\t## Injury risk and prevention\n67\t\n68\t- **Q:** Do you use any formal injury-risk model (ACWR, tissue-capacity tracking, or a specific biomechanical screening protocol with defined red flags), or is injury-risk reduction handled entirely through the general progression principles described in class? **Docs say/imply:** only general progression principles appear in Baylor-authored material; formal risk modeling appears only in the Clemson reference curriculum [intencurriculumn.md pp.140-148 vs. absence elsewhere]. **Why it matters for Flint:** this determines whether an outside source's injury-risk framework is new information or a restatement of something already in use.\n69\t- **Q:** In a movement screen (Fusionetics or similar), what specific finding actually changes an athlete's program, versus getting logged and monitored only? **Docs say/imply:** the Assessment Manual describes Fusionetics scoring and corrective-program design in general terms, without a stated action threshold [ap-assessment-manual-draft-v12.md]. **Why it matters for Flint:** \"when X, I do Y\" is implied but the X (the actual trigger) is missing.\n70\t\n71\t## Recovery and sleep\n72\t\n73\t- **Q:** Since daily subjective wellness surveys were dropped, is there any objective recovery or sleep monitoring (HRV, sleep tracking) currently in use, or is recovery managed purely through training-load design? **Docs say/imply:** recovery appears only as a named subcategory of Athletic Performance, with no described protocol or tool [week1-lecture.md p.11]. **Why it matters for Flint:** Recovery and sleep is named in the project plan as a later synthesis-note discipline, and this baseline currently has almost nothing in it.\n74\t\n75\t## Nutrition and body composition\n76\t\n77\t- **Q:** What data, if any, flows between your force-plate/GPS systems and Performance Nutrition's body-composition and fueling decisions? **Docs say/imply:** the two units are described as organizationally separate, with no described data-sharing relationship [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.5]. **Why it matters for Flint:** if there's no real integration today, Flint should say so rather than assume one exists because both units sit under Health & Wellness.\n78\t\n79\t## Coaching, skill acquisition and constraints-led approach\n80\t\n81\t- **Q:** Do you deliberately use a constraints-led or external-cueing coaching approach yourself (as described in the Clemson reference material), or is that reference-only content you don't personally apply the same way? **Docs say/imply:** external vs. internal cueing is discussed at length in the Clemson curriculum, but no Baylor-authored document states a position on it [intencurriculumn.md p.209 vs. absence elsewhere]. **Why it matters for Flint:** Coaching/skill acquisition is one of the thinnest sections in the baseline, and this is a specific, well-known framework likely to come up when comparing against outside coaching sources.\n82\t\n83\t## Periodization and programming\n84\t\n85\t- **Q:** How do you actually sequence glycolytic-specific programming across a full football calendar year today, given the stated plan is to avoid programming it directly and let camp/practice cover it? **Docs say/imply:** the stated plan and the department's operating principle of full-season KPI monitoring across energy systems aren't explicitly reconciled [class-4-2026.md pp.34, 39 vs. baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.6]. **Why it matters for Flint:** this is a concrete case where a stated principle and a stated practice could be in tension, worth resolving directly.\n86\t\n87\t## Data, technology and reporting\n88\t\n89\t- **Q:** What does an actual weekly or monthly coach-facing report look like today, and who builds it? **Docs say/imply:** your own Development Plan flags this as unfinished Phase 2 work, with no example shown anywhere in this corpus [performance-playbook-development-plan.md Phase 2]. **Why it matters for Flint:** reporting format is a concrete, checkable practice, not a principle — worth capturing before it's built out further.\n90\t- **Q:** Is Tensiomyography (TMG) still in active use under your direction, or is it a legacy tool from the Althoff/Nelson era referenced in the SOP? **Docs say/imply:** the only TMG document in this corpus names a prior Director and Associate Director, not you, and gives no indication of current status [technologytemplatesop.md]. **Why it matters for Flint:** if TMG is inactive, it shouldn't be treated as part of the current monitoring toolkit in any synthesis note.\n91\t\n92\t## Education and staff development\n93\t\n94\t- **Q:** Has the standalone 16-week Intern Sport Science Curriculum proposed in the Development Plan actually been built and piloted, and if so, what changed from the Clemson-adapted outline? **Docs say/imply:** the Development Plan proposes the curriculum in detail but this corpus contains no evidence it has been implemented [performance-playbook-development-plan.md Section 5]. **Why it matters for Flint:** if it exists now, it likely contains updated positions (assessment rubrics, portfolio requirements) not yet captured anywhere in this baseline.\n95\t\n96\t## Added 2026-09-26 from the book ingests\n97\t\n98\t- **Residual training effects: which order do you teach?** Your baseline's re-exposure rule (aerobic and strength about 30 days, speed about 5 days) matches Issurin's Table 1.6 (*Block Periodization*, printed p. 24). But the HHPR Class 4 slides (`class-4-2026.md` p. 47) state the opposite order: aerobic lost fastest, alactic slowest. Which is yours, and does it drive how you schedule speed exposures in season?\n99\t  - What your documents say: two conflicting orders (above). Why it matters for Flint: residual effects decide how often each quality must be re-touched in a football week, and the Issurin Chapter 3 peaking entries build on it.\n100\t- **The SJ-CMJ strength deficit: do you use it to choose methods?** *Supertraining* states the rule both ways (printed pp. 9-10 and 21), and the Kozinc 2022 brief in your vault says the gap is not a valid training-need marker. Do you read the gap on ForceDecks for programming, and if so, in which direction?\n101\t  - What your documents say: nothing explicit. Why it matters for Flint: this is a live ForceDecks metric at Baylor.\n102\t"}]},"uuid":"abb46f88-b976-4f00-92c4-efb2a9eb6635","timestamp":"2026-09-28T14:58:48.349Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\interview-prep.md","content":"# Flint interview prep: decision rules\n\nPurpose: make Eric's later `/interview-me` session short and pointed. This pulls from `docs/method-baseline.md` — the questions below target places where the documents are silent, where a \"when X, I do Y, because Z\" rule is implied but never stated, where two documents disagree, or where individual authorship of a position (Rash vs. Ruf vs. Wenger) cannot be confirmed from the files alone. It skips questions the documents already answer clearly and confidently.\n\n## Top 8 (highest priority, in order)\n\n1. **Ratifying the course material.** (Settled 2026-09-26: the course is co-taught by Chris Ruf, you and Mike Wenger, and the lectures are split among the three of you. What remains is which positions are yours.) Both HHPR 5355 class transcripts are diarized entirely under one Fireflies speaker label (\"Chris Ruf\") for every line, including the Feb. 5 class the syllabus assigns solo to you. Which specific positions from that course material — the Vermeil hierarchy as your compass, undulating over linear periodization, the Dynamic Correspondence framework, the energy-system priority stack for football — are actually yours, versus material you present because it's the shared course curriculum?\n2. **The mRSI daily-readiness rule.** You state that force-plate mRSI replaced subjective wellness surveys as your daily readiness marker because it's \"objective, consistent, and actionable.\" What value or week-over-week change in mRSI actually triggers a training adjustment, and for whom?\n3. **The test-retirement threshold.** You retired roster-wide NordBord testing and daily wellness surveys, each for a stated reason (time cost and staff turnover; athletes gaming the survey). Is there a general rule you'd apply to the next technology or protocol decision, or is each call made fresh?\n4. **RTP decision ownership and criteria.** The Playbook says Applied Performance \"supports\" Athletic Medicine's RTP decisions. What specific criteria, battery, or sign-off do you actually use or contribute, and where does your input end and Athletic Medicine's begin?\n5. **Department reporting model.** Is Applied Performance a peer discipline that enables Athletics Performance/Athletic Medicine/Nutrition (Playbook framing), or is it in-house programming support for the Athletics Performance staff (course-narration framing: \"helping program and plan the training\")? These may not actually conflict, but the documents state both.\n6. **Coach's-eye versus objective-first.** The course material argues you don't need a fancy test if you have a good coach's eye, and criticizes load-monitoring culture for breeding fear-driven under-training. Your own writing moved the opposite way on daily monitoring, replacing subjective self-report with an objective force-plate number. Where do you actually draw that line?\n7. **GPS/load thresholds per sport.** Your own gap analysis says the data dictionary has no sport-specific, contextualized thresholds yet. Do any exist informally, even if undocumented?\n8. **Assessment Manual currency.** The AP Assessment Manual (V12) references GymAware and a contact-mat CMJ; the current stack centers on Perch and ForceDecks-only CMJ. Is the Manual current, partially superseded, or fully legacy?\n\n---\n\n## Philosophy and department model\n\n*(Q1, Q5 above also belong here.)*\n\n- **Q:** Is there a standing exception to any of the \"five expectations\" culture rules (Golden Rule, Be Disciplined, Be Relentless, Communicate, No Excuses) — a situation where you'd deliberately relax one? **Docs say/imply:** the five expectations are presented as absolute, without a stated exception case [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.1-2]. **Why it matters for Flint:** absolute-sounding culture rules often have an unstated boundary condition; Flint should know it before applying the rule literally in a synthesis note.\n- **Q:** When an outside framework (e.g., from Old Bull or SCN) conflicts with the \"we/our, not me/my\" team-ownership model, how do you handle attribution and credit in practice, especially with staff who are new or on loan from another unit? **Docs say/imply:** the principle is stated as a value, not a practice [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.5]. **Why it matters for Flint:** this shapes how Flint should phrase recommendations that touch on staff roles.\n\n## Testing and assessment\n\n- **Q:** (Priority 8, restated) Is the AP Assessment Manual V12 current, or superseded by the Force Plate Testing V3 protocol and the Perch-centered stack? **Docs say/imply:** the two documents specify different equipment (GymAware/contact-mat vs. ForceDecks-only) for overlapping tests [ap-assessment-manual-draft-v12.md; revised-force-plate-testing-v3.md — Tension 4]. **Why it matters for Flint:** several \"Testing\" and \"Force plate\" bullets in the baseline need relabeling from current practice to legacy if the Manual is out of date.\n- **Q:** What's the actual current testing cadence (not just the battery) for each sport in the V4 Assessment Table — has it changed since that table was built? **Docs say/imply:** the table lists which tests apply to which sport but states no frequency or version date [update-baylor-sport-assessment-table-v4.md]. **Why it matters for Flint:** cadence is as much a decision rule as the battery itself, and it's the part most likely to have drifted.\n- **Q:** What specific training-age or experience marker moves an athlete from repetition-max testing to true 1RM testing? **Docs say/imply:** the rule is stated only as \"intermediate to advanced athletes\" with no defined threshold [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.18]. **Why it matters for Flint:** \"when X, I do Y\" is implied but the X is undefined.\n\n## Force plate and neuromuscular monitoring\n\n*(Q2 above also belongs here.)*\n\n- **Q:** The AP Assessment Manual says \"do not coach the strategy of jumping\" during CMJ testing, but the Force Plate Testing V3 protocol gives specific movement cues (\"keep chest up,\" \"land softly\") during the same family of tests. How do you reconcile the two, or has the newer protocol simply replaced the older instruction? **Docs say/imply:** both documents are silent on each other [ap-assessment-manual-draft-v12.md; revised-force-plate-testing-v3.md — Tension 5]. **Why it matters for Flint:** testing-cue discipline is exactly the kind of granular protocol detail an RTP or testing synthesis note needs to get right.\n\n## Load monitoring and GPS\n\n*(Q7 above also belongs here.)*\n\n- **Q:** Which system is the actual system of record when Kitman Labs and VALD Hub (or OpenField Cloud) disagree on a number? **Docs say/imply:** Kitman Labs is named as the central data store, but each vendor platform also generates its own reports, and no tie-breaker rule is stated [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.7]. **Why it matters for Flint:** any future data-reconciliation question needs a known source of truth.\n- **Q:** Do you use acute:chronic workload ratio (ACWR) or any similar load-based injury-risk metric in practice, or deliberately avoid it given the mixed evidence discussed in the reference (Clemson) curriculum? **Docs say/imply:** ACWR is discussed at length in the Clemson reference material but never mentioned in any Baylor-authored document [intencurriculumn.md pp.140-141 vs. absence elsewhere]. **Why it matters for Flint:** this is a specific, well-known framework outside sources will likely raise; Flint needs to know whether you've adopted, rejected, or never addressed it.\n\n## Speed and sprint\n\n- **Q:** The V4 Assessment Table lists \"Change of Direction\" as an assessment for several sports, but no COD testing protocol appears anywhere in this corpus. What protocol do you actually use, if any? **Docs say/imply:** COD appears only as a column header with no accompanying method [update-baylor-sport-assessment-table-v4.md]. **Why it matters for Flint:** Speed and sprint is otherwise reasonably well covered (linear sprint splits, SmartSpeed); COD is the one clear hole.\n\n## Strength and power\n\n- **Q:** Is undulating (Parker/Miller/Panariello) periodization still your default over linear periodization today, and for which sports or training blocks do you deviate from it? **Docs say/imply:** a strong first-person preference statement exists, but its speaker cannot be confirmed as you specifically [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.24-26 — Tension 9]. **Why it matters for Flint:** this is a named, specific methodological preference that would otherwise anchor every strength-programming synthesis note.\n- **Q:** What's your actual in-season strength-maintenance dosage (sets/reps/%1RM) by sport, beyond the general \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rule? **Docs say/imply:** the rule is stated in general terms only, with example ranges (3-5 sets, 2-6 reps, 50-80%) that aren't tied to a specific sport [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.21; hhpr-5355-class-8-2026.md p.21]. **Why it matters for Flint:** sport-specific numbers, not just the general principle, are what a synthesis note would need to compare against an outside source's in-season recommendations.\n\n## Conditioning and energy systems\n\n- **Q:** Has the football glycolytic-conditioning approach (\"worst case scenario,\" trained incidentally through camp) actually held up in practice, or do you now program dedicated glycolytic work under specific circumstances? **Docs say/imply:** the stated plan treats glycolytic conditioning as something to avoid programming directly [class-4-2026.md pp.34, 39]. **Why it matters for Flint:** this is a specific, falsifiable claim about current practice that's easy to check and update.\n- **Q:** Which anaerobic-threshold heart-rate method do you actually use day to day: the age-based formula or the body-size-based table? Both appear back to back in the same slide deck. **Docs say/imply:** neither slide states which supersedes the other [class-4-2026.md pp.27-29 — Tension 6]. **Why it matters for Flint:** this is a concrete, checkable methodological detail that affects every HR-zone-based conditioning recommendation.\n\n## Rehab and return to play\n\n*(Q4 above also belongs here — this is the thinnest discipline in the corpus.)*\n\n- **Q:** What closed-skill (strength, balance, hop testing) and open-skill (agility) benchmarks do you require, or recommend requiring, before clearing an athlete for full sport-specific work post-injury? **Docs say/imply:** nothing — RTP appears only as a named responsibility, never as a criterion set [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.6]. **Why it matters for Flint:** per the project plan, Rehab and RTP is slated as the first synthesis-note discipline; this baseline currently has nothing to compare an outside source against.\n- **Q:** Is there any written phased return-to-sport protocol, even an informal one, or does RTP get built case-by-case with Athletic Medicine each time? **Docs say/imply:** no such document exists in this corpus. **Why it matters for Flint:** without this, Flint cannot tell whether an outside RTP framework (e.g., Harries) would be filling a real gap or duplicating existing practice.\n- **Q:** In RTP scenarios specifically, how do the retained tools (subjective wellness surveys, targeted NordBord, isometric ForceFrame testing) actually get combined into a single readiness decision? **Docs say/imply:** each tool's retained use is stated individually; none describe how they're integrated [2026-02-19 and 2026-02-20 sportsmith pieces]. **Why it matters for Flint:** integration logic, not just tool inventory, is what a synthesis note needs.\n\n## Injury risk and prevention\n\n- **Q:** Do you use any formal injury-risk model (ACWR, tissue-capacity tracking, or a specific biomechanical screening protocol with defined red flags), or is injury-risk reduction handled entirely through the general progression principles described in class? **Docs say/imply:** only general progression principles appear in Baylor-authored material; formal risk modeling appears only in the Clemson reference curriculum [intencurriculumn.md pp.140-148 vs. absence elsewhere]. **Why it matters for Flint:** this determines whether an outside source's injury-risk framework is new information or a restatement of something already in use.\n- **Q:** In a movement screen (Fusionetics or similar), what specific finding actually changes an athlete's program, versus getting logged and monitored only? **Docs say/imply:** the Assessment Manual describes Fusionetics scoring and corrective-program design in general terms, without a stated action threshold [ap-assessment-manual-draft-v12.md]. **Why it matters for Flint:** \"when X, I do Y\" is implied but the X (the actual trigger) is missing.\n\n## Recovery and sleep\n\n- **Q:** Since daily subjective wellness surveys were dropped, is there any objective recovery or sleep monitoring (HRV, sleep tracking) currently in use, or is recovery managed purely through training-load design? **Docs say/imply:** recovery appears only as a named subcategory of Athletic Performance, with no described protocol or tool [week1-lecture.md p.11]. **Why it matters for Flint:** Recovery and sleep is named in the project plan as a later synthesis-note discipline, and this baseline currently has almost nothing in it.\n\n## Nutrition and body composition\n\n- **Q:** What data, if any, flows between your force-plate/GPS systems and Performance Nutrition's body-composition and fueling decisions? **Docs say/imply:** the two units are described as organizationally separate, with no described data-sharing relationship [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.5]. **Why it matters for Flint:** if there's no real integration today, Flint should say so rather than assume one exists because both units sit under Health & Wellness.\n\n## Coaching, skill acquisition and constraints-led approach\n\n- **Q:** Do you deliberately use a constraints-led or external-cueing coaching approach yourself (as described in the Clemson reference material), or is that reference-only content you don't personally apply the same way? **Docs say/imply:** external vs. internal cueing is discussed at length in the Clemson curriculum, but no Baylor-authored document states a position on it [intencurriculumn.md p.209 vs. absence elsewhere]. **Why it matters for Flint:** Coaching/skill acquisition is one of the thinnest sections in the baseline, and this is a specific, well-known framework likely to come up when comparing against outside coaching sources.\n\n## Periodization and programming\n\n- **Q:** How do you actually sequence glycolytic-specific programming across a full football calendar year today, given the stated plan is to avoid programming it directly and let camp/practice cover it? **Docs say/imply:** the stated plan and the department's operating principle of full-season KPI monitoring across energy systems aren't explicitly reconciled [class-4-2026.md pp.34, 39 vs. baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.6]. **Why it matters for Flint:** this is a concrete case where a stated principle and a stated practice could be in tension, worth resolving directly.\n\n## Data, technology and reporting\n\n- **Q:** What does an actual weekly or monthly coach-facing report look like today, and who builds it? **Docs say/imply:** your own Development Plan flags this as unfinished Phase 2 work, with no example shown anywhere in this corpus [performance-playbook-development-plan.md Phase 2]. **Why it matters for Flint:** reporting format is a concrete, checkable practice, not a principle — worth capturing before it's built out further.\n- **Q:** Is Tensiomyography (TMG) still in active use under your direction, or is it a legacy tool from the Althoff/Nelson era referenced in the SOP? **Docs say/imply:** the only TMG document in this corpus names a prior Director and Associate Director, not you, and gives no indication of current status [technologytemplatesop.md]. **Why it matters for Flint:** if TMG is inactive, it shouldn't be treated as part of the current monitoring toolkit in any synthesis note.\n\n## Education and staff development\n\n- **Q:** Has the standalone 16-week Intern Sport Science Curriculum proposed in the Development Plan actually been built and piloted, and if so, what changed from the Clemson-adapted outline? **Docs say/imply:** the Development Plan proposes the curriculum in detail but this corpus contains no evidence it has been implemented [performance-playbook-development-plan.md Section 5]. **Why it matters for Flint:** if it exists now, it likely contains updated positions (assessment rubrics, portfolio requirements) not yet captured anywhere in this baseline.\n\n## Added 2026-09-26 from the book ingests\n\n- **Residual training effects: which order do you teach?** Your baseline's re-exposure rule (aerobic and strength about 30 days, speed about 5 days) matches Issurin's Table 1.6 (*Block Periodization*, printed p. 24). But the HHPR Class 4 slides (`class-4-2026.md` p. 47) state the opposite order: aerobic lost fastest, alactic slowest. Which is yours, and does it drive how you schedule speed exposures in season?\n  - What your documents say: two conflicting orders (above). Why it matters for Flint: residual effects decide how often each quality must be re-touched in a football week, and the Issurin Chapter 3 peaking entries build on it.\n- **The SJ-CMJ strength deficit: do you use it to choose methods?** *Supertraining* states the rule both ways (printed pp. 9-10 and 21), and the Kozinc 2022 brief in your vault says the gap is not a valid training-need marker. Do you read the gap on ForceDecks for programming, and if so, in which direction?\n  - What your documents say: nothing explicit. Why it matters for Flint: this is a live ForceDecks metric at Baylor.\n","numLines":102,"startLine":1,"totalLines":102}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"24834391-9d04-45b7-a829-61ab3ab59e06","session_id":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\TypeSafe_Sandbox\\.claude\\worktrees\\drive-triage-run","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"worktree-drive-triage-run"}
