rt":"medium","session_id":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\TypeSafe_Sandbox\\.claude\\worktrees\\drive-triage-run","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"worktree-drive-triage-run"}
{"parentUuid":"9581d0e8-4a09-444b-b304-ade1b3b7f34d","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_non_blocking_error","hookName":"PreToolUse:Bash","toolUseID":"toolu_01UphtRik9Hc9JakprTUFCAG","hookEvent":"PreToolUse","stderr":"Failed with non-blocking status code: /usr/bin/bash: line 1: /Users/kristinarash/.local/bin/dcg: No such file or directory","stdout":"","exitCode":127,"command":"/Users/kristinarash/.local/bin/dcg","durationMs":1968},"type":"attachment","uuid":"3958ddf9-d894-4b8a-89dd-d3c95194698b","timestamp":"2026-09-28T14:59:10.916Z","session_id":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\TypeSafe_Sandbox\\.claude\\worktrees\\drive-triage-run","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"worktree-drive-triage-run"}
{"parentUuid":"9581d0e8-4a09-444b-b304-ade1b3b7f34d","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"b6ccbd46-52be-47e2-9d53-733ab42f068c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UphtRik9Hc9JakprTUFCAG","type":"tool_result","content":"21:## 1. The question\n22-\n23-How should we peak and taper in season? It splits into the game week as a small taper, how long qualities last unexposed, game-week readiness, and game-free windows (byes, camp to week 1, postseason).\n24-\n25-The sources answer a different question: every peaking model in the library comes from individual-event sports (kayak, swimming, track and field). **External:** Pyne, Mujika and Reilly 2009 (abstract): taper research does not yet guide a league season. **External:** Cuthbert et al. 2024 (PDF p. 5): a season-long peak is unrealistic. No meta-analysed study tested whether adaptations peaked when predicted ([[research-papers/afonso-2019-a-systematic-review-of-meta-analyses-comparing-periodized-and-non-periodized-exercise-prog|Afonso 2019]], pp. 1, 4). So: hold readiness weekly, and peak on purpose only in game-free windows.\n26-\n27-## 2. Bottom line\n28-\n29-1. Every game week keeps one heavy lower-body session, load high, sets cut [supported direction; the tested minimum, not Issurin's two-session template; no football trial], and one planned max-velocity exposure [contested; no football data]. Inference: prioritize positions that rarely reach top speed in games.\n30-2. Lift heaviest early; for a Saturday game the last high-volume practice falls by Wednesday, and volume, not intensity, drops from Thursday. [Issurin (rugby league) and one D-I program agree on early-heavy, late-light but differ on the heaviest practice day; descriptive only] Inference: about 48 h from the last heavy session to kickoff, from [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--central-vs-peripheral-fatigue-mechanisms|Verkhoshansky's]] [supported] 48 h recovery after high-CNS work (printed p. 15) and Pritchard 2015's 2 to 4 days of cessation before a strength peak; untested.\n31-3. Read readiness from CMJ strategy metrics (mRSI or RSImod, FT:CT), no arm swing, against each player's norm, not jump height alone or ACWR. They are noisier, so set thresholds from each player's typical error. [supported direction; small football samples] Inference: position and starter splits rest on off-season profiles and one exploratory study.\n32-4. Keep aerobic and repeat-effort conditioning every week; it may be lost fastest. [contested: Eric's baseline and Mujika and Padilla 2000 against Issurin]\n33-5. Save a real taper (about 1 to 3 weeks, volume down 40 to 60%, intensity kept) for one peak after a game-free gap, such as a bowl. [supported in other sports; untested in football]\n34-\n35-## 3. Where the sources agree\n36-\n37-**Cut volume, keep intensity.** Issurin's pre-competitive week cuts volume, keeps frequency and raises the max-speed share ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--pre-competitive-microcycle-tuning|Issurin pre-competitive]] [supported], PDF p. 105). **External:** Mujika and Padilla 2003 (abstract): volume down 60 to 90%, frequency down at most 20%. **External:** Bosquet et al. 2007 (abstract; 27 studies, sports not listed in the abstract, mostly endurance per the evidence file): 2 weeks, volume down 41 to 60%. **External:** Pritchard et al. 2019 (abstract; 11 strength-trained men, 1-week taper, about 70% volume cut): tapers did not differ significantly; only the higher-intensity one showed small effect-size gains in IMTP relative peak force and CMJ FT:CT.\n38-\n39-**Strength holds on a small dose.** Issurin, citing Fleck and Kraemer, says team-sport players hold strength 14 to 16 weeks ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--in-season-team-sport-mini-block-microcycle|Issurin in-season]] [supported], PDF pp. 108-109; secondhand). **External:** Ronnestad et al. 2011 (abstract; pro soccer, n = 7 per group): one session a week kept strength and 40-m sprint for 12 weeks; one every two weeks lost both; jump height did not change in either group. **External:** Spiering et al. 2021 (abstract; mostly non-athletes): one set per exercise per week held strength up to 32 weeks at kept load; too thin for athlete advice, the authors say.\n40-\n41-**Heavy early, light late.** For a Sunday game, Issurin (citing Baker 2001, rugby league) lifts Tuesday and Wednesday mornings, holds the key practice (\"substantial or big\" load) Thursday afternoon, and restricts only the pre-game session ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--in-season-team-sport-mini-block-microcycle|Issurin in-season]] [supported], PDF pp. 108-111). Inference: for Saturday, lifts move to Monday and Tuesday, key practice to Wednesday. **External:** Wellman et al. 2019 (31 D-I players, one program, PDF p. 6): late-season Tuesday and Wednesday loads fell by a planned duration cut; Thursday did not change.\n42-\n43-**Strategy metrics beat output.** Jump height fell only slightly by 24 h while RSImod and FT:CT stayed down ([[research-papers/lewis-undated-athlete-monitoring-in-american-collegiate-football|Lewis]], pp. 96-97; n = 5). At 72 h, flight time moved -0.7% and FT:CT -7.7% ([[research-papers/gathercole-2015-alternative-countermovement-jump-analysis-to-quantify-acute-neuromuscular-fatigue|Gathercole 2015]], p. 7; n = 8). The cost is noise: several phase and RFD variables failed reliability ([[research-papers/heishman-2020-countermovement-jump-reliability-performed-with-and-without-an-arm-swing-in-ncaa-division|Heishman 2020]], pp. 5-12). ACWR did no better than an intercept-only model ([[research-papers/impellizzeri-2020-acute-to-random-workload-ratio-is-as-associated-with-injury-as-acute-to-actual-chronic-wor|Impellizzeri 2020]], pp. 4-5).\n44-\n45-## 4. Where they disagree\n46-\n47-**Heaviest practice day, and the post-game window.** Issurin's key practice is late (Wednesday if shifted for Saturday, Inference). **External:** Wellman's program planned Tuesday as its highest practice (PDF p. 6); Lewis (PDF p. 86) found Tuesday and Wednesday closest to game demands for linemen. Both sit in the post-game window: after AFL matches, IMTP RFD (0 to 50 ms) was still down 17.8% at 72 h and 16.9% at 96 h, CV 16.4% ([[research-papers/norris-2019-recovery-of-forcetime-characteristics-after-australian-rules-football-matches-examining-th|Norris 2019]], p. 2); after soccer, hamstring RTD and sprint were impaired at 72 h ([[research-papers/cosio-2026-transcriptomic-profiling-of-the-biceps-femoris-long-head-72-h-following-a-football-match-a|Cosio 2026]], p. 8; n = 9, no control). After Saturday, Monday is about 48 h, Tuesday 72, Wednesday 96. Unresolved: load incompletely recovered players by design (Issurin), or hold heavy and high-velocity work until Wednesday? Settle with our CMJ and sprint data by hours post-game.\n48-\n49-**Which quality goes first (D-6).** Issurin: speed (5 ± 3 days) before aerobic and strength (30 ± 5), no sample ([[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability|Issurin residuals]] [contested], PDF p. 35). **Eric's baseline (class-4-2026 slides, p. 47):** aerobic first, speed last. **External:** Mujika and Padilla 2000 (abstract): within 4 weeks, trained athletes lose VO2max fast, strength little. Inference: sides with class 4. **External:** Saldanha et al. 2026 (abstract; 17 to 21 semi-pro soccer players, uncontrolled): after 5 days off, repeated-sprint time and RAST peak velocity worsened; jump and hip adductor and abductor strength held. Inference: leans toward Issurin, though RAST is not a max-velocity test. Against: **External:** Ross and Leveritt 2001 (PDF p. 17): short-sprint detraining is \"less severe than may have been reasonably anticipated\". **External:** Bosquet et al. 2013 (abstract; 103 studies): cessation cut maximal power least. **External:** Ronnestad (above) kept sprint without max-velocity work. The residual entry notes these may measure cessation versus a retention dose; Issurin calls residual-only design \"extremely rigid and inflexible\" ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--prolonging-residual-effects-with-mini-blocks|Issurin mini-blocks]] [supported], PDF p. 127). Settle with our data around byes and winter break.\n50-\n51-**Lifting near competition (D-5, D-7).** Platonov and Fesenko say it hurts technique; Bompa and Carrera say it keeps force ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--pre-competitive-microcycle-tuning|Issurin pre-competitive]] [supported], PDF p. 108). **External:** Pritchard et al. 2015 (abstract): 2 to 4 days of training cessation looks optimal for peak strength. Inference: football never stops training, so this only bounds the last lift; the shifted Issurin week puts it 4 days out.\n--\n82:## 8. Questions for Eric\n83-\n84-1. Does \"peak\" mean every Saturday, target games, or only the postseason?\n85-2. Issurin's week, shifted for Saturday (Inference), lifts Monday and Tuesday, 48 and 72 h post-game, while fast force is still down. Where do our heaviest lifts and practice sit, and should heavy and high-velocity work wait until Wednesday?\n86-3. Class-4 (p. 47) says aerobic is lost fastest; the course's 5 and 30 day figures are Issurin's table, and class-1 says aerobic goes quickly too. Is class-4 your view, and are the course in-season rules yours?\n87-4. How are bye weeks used now (restoration, loading, or a speed and strength top-up), and by position?\n88-5. Which mRSI drop, on which day, changes the plan, and do linemen and low-snap players get their own thresholds?\n89-6. Does the postseason get a distinct 1 to 3 week taper?\n90-\n91-## 9. Sources used\n92-\n93-Library entries: [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--realization-mesocycle-taper-prescription|Issurin realization]] [supported]; [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--pre-competitive-microcycle-tuning|Issurin pre-competitive]] [supported]; [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability|Issurin residuals]] [contested]; [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--in-season-team-sport-mini-block-microcycle|Issurin in-season]] [supported]; [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--prolonging-residual-effects-with-mini-blocks|Issurin mini-blocks]] [supported]; [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--central-vs-peripheral-fatigue-mechanisms|Verkhoshansky fatigue]] [supported]; [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing|Verkhoshansky taper]] [supported]; [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation|Verkhoshansky]] [contested]; [[periodization--bompa/principle--supercompensation-cycle|Bompa]] [supported]; [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching|Bompa loading]] [supported]; [[elite-sports-performance--evans/claim--fitness-fatigue-model-is-unvalidated-concept|Evans]] [untested].\n94-\n95-Paper briefs: linked in text.\n96-\n97-External (labelled in text): Bosquet 2007, 2013; Mujika and Padilla 2000, 2003; Vachon 2021; Marrier 2017; Pritchard 2015, 2019; Haugen 2019; Pyne 2009; Ronnestad 2011; Spiering 2021; Cuthbert 2024; Wellman 2019; Hoffman 2003; Peterson; Ross and Leveritt 2001; Saldanha 2026. Baseline: `docs/method-baseline.md`.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"caaf9cb7-6b91-425d-ae4f-38942ed39861","timestamp":"2026-09-28T14:59:19.704Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"21:## 1. The question\n22-\n23-How should we peak and taper in season? It splits into the game week as a small taper, how long qualities last unexposed, game-week readiness, and game-free windows (byes, camp to week 1, postseason).\n24-\n25-The sources answer a different question: every peaking model in the library comes from individual-event sports (kayak, swimming, track and field). **External:** Pyne, Mujika and Reilly 2009 (abstract): taper research does not yet guide a league season. **External:** Cuthbert et al. 2024 (PDF p. 5): a season-long peak is unrealistic. No meta-analysed study tested whether adaptations peaked when predicted ([[research-papers/afonso-2019-a-systematic-review-of-meta-analyses-comparing-periodized-and-non-periodized-exercise-prog|Afonso 2019]], pp. 1, 4). So: hold readiness weekly, and peak on purpose only in game-free windows.\n26-\n27-## 2. Bottom line\n28-\n29-1. Every game week keeps one heavy lower-body session, load high, sets cut [supported direction; the tested minimum, not Issurin's two-session template; no football trial], and one planned max-velocity exposure [contested; no football data]. Inference: prioritize positions that rarely reach top speed in games.\n30-2. Lift heaviest early; for a Saturday game the last high-volume practice falls by Wednesday, and volume, not intensity, drops from Thursday. [Issurin (rugby league) and one D-I program agree on early-heavy, late-light but differ on the heaviest practice day; descriptive only] Inference: about 48 h from the last heavy session to kickoff, from [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--central-vs-peripheral-fatigue-mechanisms|Verkhoshansky's]] [supported] 48 h recovery after high-CNS work (printed p. 15) and Pritchard 2015's 2 to 4 days of cessation before a strength peak; untested.\n31-3. Read readiness from CMJ strategy metrics (mRSI or RSImod, FT:CT), no arm swing, against each player's norm, not jump height alone or ACWR. They are noisier, so set thresholds from each player's typical error. [supported direction; small football samples] Inference: position and starter splits rest on off-season profiles and one exploratory study.\n32-4. Keep aerobic and repeat-effort conditioning every week; it may be lost fastest. [contested: Eric's baseline and Mujika and Padilla 2000 against Issurin]\n33-5. Save a real taper (about 1 to 3 weeks, volume down 40 to 60%, intensity kept) for one peak after a game-free gap, such as a bowl. [supported in other sports; untested in football]\n34-\n35-## 3. Where the sources agree\n36-\n37-**Cut volume, keep intensity.** Issurin's pre-competitive week cuts volume, keeps frequency and raises the max-speed share ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--pre-competitive-microcycle-tuning|Issurin pre-competitive]] [supported], PDF p. 105). **External:** Mujika and Padilla 2003 (abstract): volume down 60 to 90%, frequency down at most 20%. **External:** Bosquet et al. 2007 (abstract; 27 studies, sports not listed in the abstract, mostly endurance per the evidence file): 2 weeks, volume down 41 to 60%. **External:** Pritchard et al. 2019 (abstract; 11 strength-trained men, 1-week taper, about 70% volume cut): tapers did not differ significantly; only the higher-intensity one showed small effect-size gains in IMTP relative peak force and CMJ FT:CT.\n38-\n39-**Strength holds on a small dose.** Issurin, citing Fleck and Kraemer, says team-sport players hold strength 14 to 16 weeks ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--in-season-team-sport-mini-block-microcycle|Issurin in-season]] [supported], PDF pp. 108-109; secondhand). **External:** Ronnestad et al. 2011 (abstract; pro soccer, n = 7 per group): one session a week kept strength and 40-m sprint for 12 weeks; one every two weeks lost both; jump height did not change in either group. **External:** Spiering et al. 2021 (abstract; mostly non-athletes): one set per exercise per week held strength up to 32 weeks at kept load; too thin for athlete advice, the authors say.\n40-\n41-**Heavy early, light late.** For a Sunday game, Issurin (citing Baker 2001, rugby league) lifts Tuesday and Wednesday mornings, holds the key practice (\"substantial or big\" load) Thursday afternoon, and restricts only the pre-game session ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--in-season-team-sport-mini-block-microcycle|Issurin in-season]] [supported], PDF pp. 108-111). Inference: for Saturday, lifts move to Monday and Tuesday, key practice to Wednesday. **External:** Wellman et al. 2019 (31 D-I players, one program, PDF p. 6): late-season Tuesday and Wednesday loads fell by a planned duration cut; Thursday did not change.\n42-\n43-**Strategy metrics beat output.** Jump height fell only slightly by 24 h while RSImod and FT:CT stayed down ([[research-papers/lewis-undated-athlete-monitoring-in-american-collegiate-football|Lewis]], pp. 96-97; n = 5). At 72 h, flight time moved -0.7% and FT:CT -7.7% ([[research-papers/gathercole-2015-alternative-countermovement-jump-analysis-to-quantify-acute-neuromuscular-fatigue|Gathercole 2015]], p. 7; n = 8). The cost is noise: several phase and RFD variables failed reliability ([[research-papers/heishman-2020-countermovement-jump-reliability-performed-with-and-without-an-arm-swing-in-ncaa-division|Heishman 2020]], pp. 5-12). ACWR did no better than an intercept-only model ([[research-papers/impellizzeri-2020-acute-to-random-workload-ratio-is-as-associated-with-injury-as-acute-to-actual-chronic-wor|Impellizzeri 2020]], pp. 4-5).\n44-\n45-## 4. Where they disagree\n46-\n47-**Heaviest practice day, and the post-game window.** Issurin's key practice is late (Wednesday if shifted for Saturday, Inference). **External:** Wellman's program planned Tuesday as its highest practice (PDF p. 6); Lewis (PDF p. 86) found Tuesday and Wednesday closest to game demands for linemen. Both sit in the post-game window: after AFL matches, IMTP RFD (0 to 50 ms) was still down 17.8% at 72 h and 16.9% at 96 h, CV 16.4% ([[research-papers/norris-2019-recovery-of-forcetime-characteristics-after-australian-rules-football-matches-examining-th|Norris 2019]], p. 2); after soccer, hamstring RTD and sprint were impaired at 72 h ([[research-papers/cosio-2026-transcriptomic-profiling-of-the-biceps-femoris-long-head-72-h-following-a-football-match-a|Cosio 2026]], p. 8; n = 9, no control). After Saturday, Monday is about 48 h, Tuesday 72, Wednesday 96. Unresolved: load incompletely recovered players by design (Issurin), or hold heavy and high-velocity work until Wednesday? Settle with our CMJ and sprint data by hours post-game.\n48-\n49-**Which quality goes first (D-6).** Issurin: speed (5 ± 3 days) before aerobic and strength (30 ± 5), no sample ([[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability|Issurin residuals]] [contested], PDF p. 35). **Eric's baseline (class-4-2026 slides, p. 47):** aerobic first, speed last. **External:** Mujika and Padilla 2000 (abstract): within 4 weeks, trained athletes lose VO2max fast, strength little. Inference: sides with class 4. **External:** Saldanha et al. 2026 (abstract; 17 to 21 semi-pro soccer players, uncontrolled): after 5 days off, repeated-sprint time and RAST peak velocity worsened; jump and hip adductor and abductor strength held. Inference: leans toward Issurin, though RAST is not a max-velocity test. Against: **External:** Ross and Leveritt 2001 (PDF p. 17): short-sprint detraining is \"less severe than may have been reasonably anticipated\". **External:** Bosquet et al. 2013 (abstract; 103 studies): cessation cut maximal power least. **External:** Ronnestad (above) kept sprint without max-velocity work. The residual entry notes these may measure cessation versus a retention dose; Issurin calls residual-only design \"extremely rigid and inflexible\" ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--prolonging-residual-effects-with-mini-blocks|Issurin mini-blocks]] [supported], PDF p. 127). Settle with our data around byes and winter break.\n50-\n51-**Lifting near competition (D-5, D-7).** Platonov and Fesenko say it hurts technique; Bompa and Carrera say it keeps force ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--pre-competitive-microcycle-tuning|Issurin pre-competitive]] [supported], PDF p. 108). **External:** Pritchard et al. 2015 (abstract): 2 to 4 days of training cessation looks optimal for peak strength. Inference: football never stops training, so this only bounds the last lift; the shifted Issurin week puts it 4 days out.\n--\n82:## 8. Questions for Eric\n83-\n84-1. Does \"peak\" mean every Saturday, target games, or only the postseason?\n85-2. Issurin's week, shifted for Saturday (Inference), lifts Monday and Tuesday, 48 and 72 h post-game, while fast force is still down. Where do our heaviest lifts and practice sit, and should heavy and high-velocity work wait until Wednesday?\n86-3. Class-4 (p. 47) says aerobic is lost fastest; the course's 5 and 30 day figures are Issurin's table, and class-1 says aerobic goes quickly too. Is class-4 your view, and are the course in-season rules yours?\n87-4. How are bye weeks used now (restoration, loading, or a speed and strength top-up), and by position?\n88-5. Which mRSI drop, on which day, changes the plan, and do linemen and low-snap players get their own thresholds?\n89-6. Does the postseason get a distinct 1 to 3 week taper?\n90-\n91-## 9. Sources used\n92-\n93-Library entries: [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--realization-mesocycle-taper-prescription|Issurin realization]] [supported]; [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--pre-competitive-microcycle-tuning|Issurin pre-competitive]] [supported]; [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability|Issurin residuals]] [contested]; [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--in-season-team-sport-mini-block-microcycle|Issurin in-season]] [supported]; [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--prolonging-residual-effects-with-mini-blocks|Issurin mini-blocks]] [supported]; [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--central-vs-peripheral-fatigue-mechanisms|Verkhoshansky fatigue]] [supported]; [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing|Verkhoshansky taper]] [supported]; [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation|Verkhoshansky]] [contested]; [[periodization--bompa/principle--supercompensation-cycle|Bompa]] [supported]; [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching|Bompa loading]] [supported]; [[elite-sports-performance--evans/claim--fitness-fatigue-model-is-unvalidated-concept|Evans]] [untested].\n94-\n95-Paper briefs: linked in text.\n96-\n97-External (labelled in text): Bosquet 2007, 2013; Mujika and Padilla 2000, 2003; Vachon 2021; Marrier 2017; Pritchard 2015, 2019; Haugen 2019; Pyne 2009; Ronnestad 2011; Spiering 2021; Cuthbert 2024; Wellman 2019; Hoffman 2003; Peterson; Ross and Leveritt 2001; Saldanha 2026. Baseline: `docs/method-baseline.md`.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[],"moreFiles":0,"unavailable":true}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9581d0e8-4a09-444b-b304-ade1b3b7f34d","session_id":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\TypeSafe_Sandbox\\.claude\\worktrees\\drive-triage-run","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"worktree-drive-triage-run"}
