{"parentUuid":"ed74c362-339c-43be-beae-0e448d3082bd","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfWDLwBjoNeRwv38jBHzb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":141134,"cache_read_input_tokens":31717,"output_tokens":1333,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":166},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":141134,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1333,"cache_read_input_tokens":31717,"cache_creation_input_tokens":141134,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":141134},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":{"cache_miss_reason":{"type":"previous_message_not_found"}},"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfWDLv5mPs569N1pfLVYx","type":"assistant","uuid":"91ce8c22-7ff3-478e-8410-3580b6edcea7","timestamp":"2026-09-28T17:14:35.601Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"step1-triage"}
{"parentUuid":"91ce8c22-7ff3-478e-8410-3580b6edcea7","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfWDLwBjoNeRwv38jBHzb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FPivasP8GwX4xUNEn8XGfP","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\interview\\2026-09-28-method-interview.md","old_string":"> There is a place for the coach's eye: when something looks off, it can direct you where to\n> look and gather data to investigate further.\n","new_string":"> There is a place for the coach's eye: when something looks off, it can direct you where to\n> look and gather data to investigate further.\n\n**2a. Follow-up: individualized against what?**\n> Percent change from baseline or a rolling average. We've used standard deviations and SWC to\n> diagnose signal versus noise.\n\n**4a. Follow-up: where is the written RTP protocol?**\n> `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Downloads\\Baylor UE Rehab Program Template.docx` and\n> `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Downloads\\Baylor LE Rehab Program Template.docx`\n\n(Not yet staged. Next step: copy both to Drive and stage a text copy for the RTP synthesis.)\n\n## Round 2: testing, force plate, load\n\n**7. GPS and load thresholds per sport.** Do any exist informally?\n> They do not exist formally.\n\n**8. Assessment Manual V12.** Current, partly replaced, or legacy? (Includes the CMJ cue\nconflict with Force Plate Testing V3.)\n> It is not current. We are in the process of developing a new, more robust version that will\n> incorporate the NCAA technology/sport science recommendations.\n\n**9. Testing cadence per sport.** Changed since the V4 Assessment Table?\n> That assessment schedule was never used. We currently don't have a consistent cadence across\n> all sports. Individual sports have their own unique schedules.\n\n**10. Rep-max to true 1RM.** What marker moves an athlete to true 1RM testing?\n> No sports perform true 1RM testing. Instead things are ballparked based on other information\n> we're grabbing on the athlete: watching them move in real time, then using historical context\n> to make future programming decisions around projected 1RMs.\n\n**11. System of record.** Kitman Labs versus VALD Hub or OpenField.\n> Each system has its own unique data. All our force plate and force-measuring data lives in\n> VALD. All our information gets aggregated to Kitman Labs AMS, and our Catapult information\n> lives in OpenField. They're separate entities; one doesn't overrule the other. If Kitman had\n> wrong numbers from VALD or OpenField, we would go to those sources to correct it: the source\n> systems overrule any errors Kitman made bringing data into the platform.\n\n**12. Injury-risk model, ACWR, movement screens.**\n> We dabble with ACWR but I don't personally trust it for anything. I think it's raw, has error,\n> is misleading, and lacks context generally. We don't have any formal risk model that we follow.\n> We've avoided them up to this point, but there's not a really good reason why. From a movement\n> screen standpoint, there isn't anything that changes decisions made to a program; it's more or\n> less just collected and referred to for future needs.\n\n**13. Change of direction.**\n> We don't test change of direction in most sports. If the data is captured, it's on an\n> individual sport basis and that information lives with that coach.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":141134,"cache_read_input_tokens":31717,"output_tokens":1333,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":166},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":141134,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1333,"cache_read_input_tokens":31717,"cache_creation_input_tokens":141134,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":141134},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":{"cache_miss_reason":{"type":"previous_message_not_found"}},"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01FPivasP8GwX4xUNEn8XGfP":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\interview\\2026-09-28-method-interview.md","old_string":"> There is a place for the coach's eye: when something looks off, it can direct you where to\n> look and gather data to investigate further.\n","new_string":"> There is a place for the coach's eye: when something looks off, it can direct you where to\n> look and gather data to investigate further.\n\n**2a. Follow-up: individualized against what?**\n> Percent change from baseline or a rolling average. We've used standard deviations and SWC to\n> diagnose signal versus noise.\n\n**4a. Follow-up: where is the written RTP protocol?**\n> `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Downloads\\Baylor UE Rehab Program Template.docx` and\n> `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Downloads\\Baylor LE Rehab Program Template.docx`\n\n(Not yet staged. Next step: copy both to Drive and stage a text copy for the RTP synthesis.)\n\n## Round 2: testing, force plate, load\n\n**7. GPS and load thresholds per sport.** Do any exist informally?\n> They do not exist formally.\n\n**8. Assessment Manual V12.** Current, partly replaced, or legacy? (Includes the CMJ cue\nconflict with Force Plate Testing V3.)\n> It is not current. We are in the process of developing a new, more robust version that will\n> incorporate the NCAA technology/sport science recommendations.\n\n**9. Testing cadence per sport.** Changed since the V4 Assessment Table?\n> That assessment schedule was never used. We currently don't have a consistent cadence across\n> all sports. Individual sports have their own unique schedules.\n\n**10. Rep-max to true 1RM.** What marker moves an athlete to true 1RM testing?\n> No sports perform true 1RM testing. Instead things are ballparked based on other information\n> we're grabbing on the athlete: watching them move in real time, then using historical context\n> to make future programming decisions around projected 1RMs.\n\n**11. System of record.** Kitman Labs versus VALD Hub or OpenField.\n> Each system has its own unique data. All our force plate and force-measuring data lives in\n> VALD. All our information gets aggregated to Kitman Labs AMS, and our Catapult information\n> lives in OpenField. They're separate entities; one doesn't overrule the other. If Kitman had\n> wrong numbers from VALD or OpenField, we would go to those sources to correct it: the source\n> systems overrule any errors Kitman made bringing data into the platform.\n\n**12. Injury-risk model, ACWR, movement screens.**\n> We dabble with ACWR but I don't personally trust it for anything. I think it's raw, has error,\n> is misleading, and lacks context generally. We don't have any formal risk model that we follow.\n> We've avoided them up to this point, but there's not a really good reason why. From a movement\n> screen standpoint, there isn't anything that changes decisions made to a program; it's more or\n> less just collected and referred to for future needs.\n\n**13. Change of direction.**\n> We don't test change of direction in most sports. If the data is captured, it's on an\n> individual sport basis and that information lives with that coach.\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CfWDLv5mPs569N1pfLVYx","type":"assistant","uuid":"22d53a8c-3ec7-4ba5-a78f-1c4746091dd3","timestamp":"2026-09-28T17:14:43.802Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"step1-triage"}
{"parentUuid":"22d53a8c-3ec7-4ba5-a78f-1c4746091dd3","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4f6c7a9e-0bae-4a0a-9385-aa382b7eba14","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FPivasP8GwX4xUNEn8XGfP","type":"tool_result","content":"The file C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\interview\\2026-09-28-method-interview.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"7a7ecc84-d84b-44ba-991b-ae3b8b4386fa","timestamp":"2026-09-28T17:14:44.202Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\interview\\2026-09-28-method-interview.md","oldString":"> There is a place for the coach's eye: when something looks off, it can direct you where to\n> look and gather data to investigate further.\n","newString":"> There is a place for the coach's eye: when something looks off, it can direct you where to\n> look and gather data to investigate further.\n\n**2a. Follow-up: individualized against what?**\n> Percent change from baseline or a rolling average. We've used standard deviations and SWC to\n> diagnose signal versus noise.\n\n**4a. Follow-up: where is the written RTP protocol?**\n> `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Downloads\\Baylor UE Rehab Program Template.docx` and\n> `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Downloads\\Baylor LE Rehab Program Template.docx`\n\n(Not yet staged. Next step: copy both to Drive and stage a text copy for the RTP synthesis.)\n\n## Round 2: testing, force plate, load\n\n**7. GPS and load thresholds per sport.** Do any exist informally?\n> They do not exist formally.\n\n**8. Assessment Manual V12.** Current, partly replaced, or legacy? (Includes the CMJ cue\nconflict with Force Plate Testing V3.)\n> It is not current. We are in the process of developing a new, more robust version that will\n> incorporate the NCAA technology/sport science recommendations.\n\n**9. Testing cadence per sport.** Changed since the V4 Assessment Table?\n> That assessment schedule was never used. We currently don't have a consistent cadence across\n> all sports. Individual sports have their own unique schedules.\n\n**10. Rep-max to true 1RM.** What marker moves an athlete to true 1RM testing?\n> No sports perform true 1RM testing. Instead things are ballparked based on other information\n> we're grabbing on the athlete: watching them move in real time, then using historical context\n> to make future programming decisions around projected 1RMs.\n\n**11. System of record.** Kitman Labs versus VALD Hub or OpenField.\n> Each system has its own unique data. All our force plate and force-measuring data lives in\n> VALD. All our information gets aggregated to Kitman Labs AMS, and our Catapult information\n> lives in OpenField. They're separate entities; one doesn't overrule the other. If Kitman had\n> wrong numbers from VALD or OpenField, we would go to those sources to correct it: the source\n> systems overrule any errors Kitman made bringing data into the platform.\n\n**12. Injury-risk model, ACWR, movement screens.**\n> We dabble with ACWR but I don't personally trust it for anything. I think it's raw, has error,\n> is misleading, and lacks context generally. We don't have any formal risk model that we follow.\n> We've avoided them up to this point, but there's not a really good reason why. From a movement\n> screen standpoint, there isn't anything that changes decisions made to a program; it's more or\n> less just collected and referred to for future needs.\n\n**13. Change of direction.**\n> We don't test change of direction in most sports. If the data is captured, it's on an\n> individual sport basis and that information lives with that coach.\n","originalFile":"---\ndate: 2026-09-28\nsource: live /interview-me session (Claude Code)\nquestions: docs/interview-prep.md (27) + docs/synthesis/peaking-and-taper-in-season-football.md §8 (6); overlaps merged\nstatus: in progress\n---\n\n# Flint method interview, 2026-09-28\n\nEric's answers, in his words (typos fixed only). Each answer turns the matching\n`method-baseline.md` position from Inferred to Explicit, or records what he said instead.\n\n## Round 1: the load-bearing ones\n\n**1. Course material (HHPR 5355): which positions are yours?** Vermeil hierarchy as compass;\nundulating over linear periodization; Dynamic Correspondence; the football energy-system\npriority stack.\n> All of them.\n\n**2. mRSI readiness rule** (also peaking Q5). Which drop, on which day, changes the plan?\nSeparate thresholds for linemen and low-snap players?\n> Wednesdays, the thresholds should be individualized.\n\n**3. Retiring a test or technology.** General rule, or a fresh call each time?\n> If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a direct need, then it doesn't have a place.\n\n**4. Return to play.** What Applied Performance contributes, where its input ends; is there a\nwritten phased protocol?\n> Testing and progression. I collaborate on loading and progression decisions when needed.\n> It doesn't really end. There is a written protocol, I can provide it.\n\n**5. Department model.** Peer discipline enabling Athletic Performance, Athletic Medicine and\nNutrition, or in-house programming support for Athletic Performance?\n> Both.\n\n**6. Coach's eye versus objective data.** Where is the line?\n> There is a place for the coach's eye: when something looks off, it can direct you where to\n> look and gather data to investigate further.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":36,"oldLines":3,"newStart":36,"newLines":50,"lines":[" **6. Coach's eye versus objective data.** Where is the line?"," > There is a place for the coach's eye: when something looks off, it can direct you where to"," > look and gather data to investigate further.","+","+**2a. Follow-up: individualized against what?**","+> Percent change from baseline or a rolling average. We've used standard deviations and SWC to","+> diagnose signal versus noise.","+","+**4a. Follow-up: where is the written RTP protocol?**","+> `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Downloads\\Baylor UE Rehab Program Template.docx` and","+> `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Downloads\\Baylor LE Rehab Program Template.docx`","+","+(Not yet staged. Next step: copy both to Drive and stage a text copy for the RTP synthesis.)","+","+## Round 2: testing, force plate, load","+","+**7. GPS and load thresholds per sport.** Do any exist informally?","+> They do not exist formally.","+","+**8. Assessment Manual V12.** Current, partly replaced, or legacy? (Includes the CMJ cue","+conflict with Force Plate Testing V3.)","+> It is not current. We are in the process of developing a new, more robust version that will","+> incorporate the NCAA technology/sport science recommendations.","+","+**9. Testing cadence per sport.** Changed since the V4 Assessment Table?","+> That assessment schedule was never used. We currently don't have a consistent cadence across","+> all sports. Individual sports have their own unique schedules.","+","+**10. Rep-max to true 1RM.** What marker moves an athlete to true 1RM testing?","+> No sports perform true 1RM testing. Instead things are ballparked based on other information","+> we're grabbing on the athlete: watching them move in real time, then using historical context","+> to make future programming decisions around projected 1RMs.","+","+**11. System of record.** Kitman Labs versus VALD Hub or OpenField.","+> Each system has its own unique data. All our force plate and force-measuring data lives in","+> VALD. All our information gets aggregated to Kitman Labs AMS, and our Catapult information","+> lives in OpenField. They're separate entities; one doesn't overrule the other. If Kitman had","+> wrong numbers from VALD or OpenField, we would go to those sources to correct it: the source","+> systems overrule any errors Kitman made bringing data into the platform.","+","+**12. Injury-risk model, ACWR, movement screens.**","+> We dabble with ACWR but I don't personally trust it for anything. I think it's raw, has error,","+> is misleading, and lacks context generally. We don't have any formal risk model that we follow.","+> We've avoided them up to this point, but there's not a really good reason why. From a movement","+> screen standpoint, there isn't anything that changes decisions made to a program; it's more or","+> less just collected and referred to for future needs.","+","+**13. Change of direction.**","+> We don't test change of direction in most sports. If the data is captured, it's on an","+> individual sport basis and that information lives with that coach."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"22d53a8c-3ec7-4ba5-a78f-1c4746091dd3","session_id":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"step1-triage"}
