{"parentUuid":"340dcb5d-8a62-4e78-8468-797efe17818c","isSidechain":false,"type":"system","subtype":"turn_duration","durationMs":38288,"messageCount":368,"timestamp":"2026-09-28T17:15:07.747Z","uuid":"8bf28152-740d-4fad-a63c-708d68b88525","isMeta":false,"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"step1-triage"}
{"parentUuid":"8bf28152-740d-4fad-a63c-708d68b88525","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"fac415c0-c953-45da-9324-fcb73ab7e8f4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"14. Yes in football, you want to peak every Saturday but definitely throughout the month of November as you get closer to postseason, bowl eligibility, things like that. If you're in the upper echelon of programs, then that's the push for the college football playoff. That's definitely when players need to be at or near their peak as they head into postseason and playoffs. \n15. \n\n<pasted_content id=\"163a\">\n- Sunday is the off day.\n- Monday is a heavy lift day.\n- Tuesday and Wednesday are heavy practice days and then there's a lighter lift on Wednesday.\n- The games are played on Saturday so 72 hours before the game is kind of where the lifting ends and the hard practices end.\n</pasted_content id=\"163a\">\n\n16. I believe Issurin, it does drive our speed programming in the offseason and what drives sprinting recommendations in season.\n17. The bye weeks usually consist of three practice days. There's extra rest and recovery scheduled. The three practice days, I would say, are probably slightly on the higher-volume side than they would be if there was a game being played that weekend but there's definitely a recovery focus along with speed and strength top up, It's more based on who's playing. Guys that aren't getting a ton of play will probably practice harder that week than guys that have gotten a ton of snaps in games up to that point.\n18. I dont have the specific sets and reps.\n19. It does hold up but our staff does program it in the off season.\n20. Age based.\n21. We use a version of it but we incorporate the countermovement jump and the isometric mid-thigh pull to get a dynamic strength index (to determine if we need to do more plyometric training or we need to do more max strength training)."},"uuid":"80ccf9df-1cb0-4eb9-8ccc-3fa1df838440","timestamp":"2026-09-28T17:35:49.726Z","permissionMode":"bypassPermissions","origin":{"kind":"human"},"promptSource":"typed","turnOrigin":"human","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"step1-triage"}
