{"parentUuid":"b35a8b83-92e3-481a-bc65-5b34c2b23e26","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfWFqxkYrLDo5GKqDtPKN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8550,"cache_read_input_tokens":179208,"output_tokens":2017,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":171},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8550,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2017,"cache_read_input_tokens":179208,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8550,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8550},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfWFqwVeTSWgBHSwyt4Td","type":"assistant","uuid":"dfc6d728-e768-47b7-8f4b-a9ed38ea2dcf","timestamp":"2026-09-28T17:47:21.614Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"step1-triage"}
{"parentUuid":"dfc6d728-e768-47b7-8f4b-a9ed38ea2dcf","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfWFqxkYrLDo5GKqDtPKN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SgEF7UcHJqKbBYNHYQzyeA","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\interview\\2026-09-28-method-interview.md","old_string":"The *Supertraining* entries and the Kozinc 2022 brief address SJ-CMJ; DSI needs its own\nevidence check before any synthesis cites it.\n","new_string":"The *Supertraining* entries and the Kozinc 2022 brief address SJ-CMJ; DSI needs its own\nevidence check before any synthesis cites it.\n\n## Round 4: RTP, recovery, nutrition, coaching, staff, reporting\n\n**22. RTP: how do wellness, NordBord and ForceFrame combine into one readiness decision?**\n> We don't assess readiness. The template answers that.\n\n**23. Recovery and sleep monitoring.**\n> It's handled through training-load design alone. We're not tracking anything objective.\n\n**24. Force-plate or GPS data to Performance Nutrition.**\n> It does not. Only if there is an issue will bodyweight be consulted. Nothing proactive.\n\n**25. Constraints-led or external-cue coaching.**\n> I don't apply it, as I don't fully understand it. I want to know more.\n\n**26. The five expectations: any deliberate exception?**\n> These are more a relic of another coaching era that has held over. I would say none, but\n> they are rarely mentioned around here any more.\n\n**27. Attribution when outside frameworks meet \"we/our, not me/my\".**\n> We fold it into the mantra of \"this is how we do our business.\"\n\n**28. Coach-facing reports: what they look like, who builds them.**\n> Strength staff will build it most likely; we don't have sport science support for most teams.\n> When applicable, sport science staff would provide it.\n\n**29. TMG.**\n> Only Chris Ruf with Track still uses it.\n\n**30. The 16-week Intern Sport Science Curriculum.**\n> It has not been built yet.\n\n---\n\n## What changes in Flint (to apply; not yet applied)\n\nBaseline (`method-baseline.md`):\n- Course positions (Vermeil, undulating periodization, Dynamic Correspondence, football\n  energy-system stack): Inferred to **Explicit, Eric's**.\n- Testing: Assessment Manual V12, the V4 assessment table and its schedule are **legacy**; a new\n  manual built on the NCAA technology and sport-science recommendations is in progress. No\n  common cadence; each sport sets its own. **No true 1RM testing** anywhere; maxes are\n  projected from observation plus history. COD is not tested in most sports.\n- Monitoring: mRSI read on **Wednesdays**, individualized thresholds (percent change from\n  baseline or rolling average; SD and SWC for signal versus noise). No formal GPS thresholds.\n  **DSI** (CMJ vs IMTP) chooses plyometric versus max-strength emphasis.\n- Data: VALD holds force data, OpenField holds Catapult, Kitman AMS aggregates; **the source\n  system wins** when Kitman disagrees.\n- Injury risk: ACWR distrusted (\"raw, has error, misleading, lacks context\"); no formal risk\n  model; movement screens are logged, not acted on.\n- Recovery: load design only, nothing objective tracked. Nutrition: no proactive data flow.\n- RTP: Applied Performance owns testing and progression and collaborates on loading; no\n  readiness assessment; the written protocol is the UE and LE rehab program templates.\n- Conditioning: age-based anaerobic-threshold HR; glycolytic work programmed in the off-season\n  only. TMG: Chris Ruf with Track only. Intern curriculum: not built.\n- Decision rule for any test or technology: \"If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a\n  direct need, then it doesn't have a place.\"\n- Coach's eye: flags where to look; data then investigates.\n- Five expectations: legacy culture language, rarely used.\n\nPeaking synthesis (`docs/synthesis/peaking-and-taper-in-season-football.md`):\n- Peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window.\n- Real week: Sun off, Mon heavy lift, Tue and Wed heavy practice plus a lighter Wed lift; heavy\n  work ends **72 h** before kickoff (the synthesis inferred 48 h).\n- Residual effects: Eric sides with **Issurin** (speed fades first), against the Class 4 slide.\n- Byes: three practices, slightly higher volume, recovery focus plus speed and strength\n  top-up, harder for low-snap players.\n\nOpen for Eric: in-season strength sets and reps (Q18, not available); stage the two RTP\ntemplates; constraints-led coaching is a learning request (Q25), a candidate for a primer.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8550,"cache_read_input_tokens":179208,"output_tokens":2017,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":171},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8550,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2017,"cache_read_input_tokens":179208,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8550,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8550},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01SgEF7UcHJqKbBYNHYQzyeA":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\interview\\2026-09-28-method-interview.md","old_string":"The *Supertraining* entries and the Kozinc 2022 brief address SJ-CMJ; DSI needs its own\nevidence check before any synthesis cites it.\n","new_string":"The *Supertraining* entries and the Kozinc 2022 brief address SJ-CMJ; DSI needs its own\nevidence check before any synthesis cites it.\n\n## Round 4: RTP, recovery, nutrition, coaching, staff, reporting\n\n**22. RTP: how do wellness, NordBord and ForceFrame combine into one readiness decision?**\n> We don't assess readiness. The template answers that.\n\n**23. Recovery and sleep monitoring.**\n> It's handled through training-load design alone. We're not tracking anything objective.\n\n**24. Force-plate or GPS data to Performance Nutrition.**\n> It does not. Only if there is an issue will bodyweight be consulted. Nothing proactive.\n\n**25. Constraints-led or external-cue coaching.**\n> I don't apply it, as I don't fully understand it. I want to know more.\n\n**26. The five expectations: any deliberate exception?**\n> These are more a relic of another coaching era that has held over. I would say none, but\n> they are rarely mentioned around here any more.\n\n**27. Attribution when outside frameworks meet \"we/our, not me/my\".**\n> We fold it into the mantra of \"this is how we do our business.\"\n\n**28. Coach-facing reports: what they look like, who builds them.**\n> Strength staff will build it most likely; we don't have sport science support for most teams.\n> When applicable, sport science staff would provide it.\n\n**29. TMG.**\n> Only Chris Ruf with Track still uses it.\n\n**30. The 16-week Intern Sport Science Curriculum.**\n> It has not been built yet.\n\n---\n\n## What changes in Flint (to apply; not yet applied)\n\nBaseline (`method-baseline.md`):\n- Course positions (Vermeil, undulating periodization, Dynamic Correspondence, football\n  energy-system stack): Inferred to **Explicit, Eric's**.\n- Testing: Assessment Manual V12, the V4 assessment table and its schedule are **legacy**; a new\n  manual built on the NCAA technology and sport-science recommendations is in progress. No\n  common cadence; each sport sets its own. **No true 1RM testing** anywhere; maxes are\n  projected from observation plus history. COD is not tested in most sports.\n- Monitoring: mRSI read on **Wednesdays**, individualized thresholds (percent change from\n  baseline or rolling average; SD and SWC for signal versus noise). No formal GPS thresholds.\n  **DSI** (CMJ vs IMTP) chooses plyometric versus max-strength emphasis.\n- Data: VALD holds force data, OpenField holds Catapult, Kitman AMS aggregates; **the source\n  system wins** when Kitman disagrees.\n- Injury risk: ACWR distrusted (\"raw, has error, misleading, lacks context\"); no formal risk\n  model; movement screens are logged, not acted on.\n- Recovery: load design only, nothing objective tracked. Nutrition: no proactive data flow.\n- RTP: Applied Performance owns testing and progression and collaborates on loading; no\n  readiness assessment; the written protocol is the UE and LE rehab program templates.\n- Conditioning: age-based anaerobic-threshold HR; glycolytic work programmed in the off-season\n  only. TMG: Chris Ruf with Track only. Intern curriculum: not built.\n- Decision rule for any test or technology: \"If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a\n  direct need, then it doesn't have a place.\"\n- Coach's eye: flags where to look; data then investigates.\n- Five expectations: legacy culture language, rarely used.\n\nPeaking synthesis (`docs/synthesis/peaking-and-taper-in-season-football.md`):\n- Peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window.\n- Real week: Sun off, Mon heavy lift, Tue and Wed heavy practice plus a lighter Wed lift; heavy\n  work ends **72 h** before kickoff (the synthesis inferred 48 h).\n- Residual effects: Eric sides with **Issurin** (speed fades first), against the Class 4 slide.\n- Byes: three practices, slightly higher volume, recovery focus plus speed and strength\n  top-up, harder for low-snap players.\n\nOpen for Eric: in-season strength sets and reps (Q18, not available); stage the two RTP\ntemplates; constraints-led coaching is a learning request (Q25), a candidate for a primer.\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CfWFqwVeTSWgBHSwyt4Td","type":"assistant","uuid":"45650673-5f97-4888-b8ec-6b24dd14be95","timestamp":"2026-09-28T17:47:37.559Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"step1-triage"}
{"parentUuid":"45650673-5f97-4888-b8ec-6b24dd14be95","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfWFqxkYrLDo5GKqDtPKN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013hCKEyVDKeBCLoCk3hyP8n","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -i 's/^status: in progress$/status: complete (30 of 30 answered)/' docs/interview/2026-09-28-method-interview.md && sed -n 1,20p docs/STATUS.md","description":"Mark interview complete and view STATUS head"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8550,"cache_read_input_tokens":179208,"output_tokens":2017,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":171},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8550,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2017,"cache_read_input_tokens":179208,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8550,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8550},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_013hCKEyVDKeBCLoCk3hyP8n":{"command":"sed -i 's/^status: in progress$/status: complete (30 of 30 answered)/' docs/interview/2026-09-28-method-interview.md && sed -n 1,20p docs/STATUS.md","description":"Mark interview complete and view STATUS head"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CfWFqwVeTSWgBHSwyt4Td","type":"assistant","uuid":"71d8bf99-1ebf-48ca-a453-c301504c7e1f","timestamp":"2026-09-28T17:47:37.693Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"step1-triage"}
{"parentUuid":"45650673-5f97-4888-b8ec-6b24dd14be95","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"0346e074-6d88-4c4e-a924-8e369320fddb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SgEF7UcHJqKbBYNHYQzyeA","type":"tool_result","content":"The file C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\interview\\2026-09-28-method-interview.md has been updated successfully."}]},"uuid":"fdfd9bd2-fefa-4ecb-b8fa-86d24092d6dd","timestamp":"2026-09-28T17:47:39.358Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\interview\\2026-09-28-method-interview.md","oldString":"The *Supertraining* entries and the Kozinc 2022 brief address SJ-CMJ; DSI needs its own\nevidence check before any synthesis cites it.\n","newString":"The *Supertraining* entries and the Kozinc 2022 brief address SJ-CMJ; DSI needs its own\nevidence check before any synthesis cites it.\n\n## Round 4: RTP, recovery, nutrition, coaching, staff, reporting\n\n**22. RTP: how do wellness, NordBord and ForceFrame combine into one readiness decision?**\n> We don't assess readiness. The template answers that.\n\n**23. Recovery and sleep monitoring.**\n> It's handled through training-load design alone. We're not tracking anything objective.\n\n**24. Force-plate or GPS data to Performance Nutrition.**\n> It does not. Only if there is an issue will bodyweight be consulted. Nothing proactive.\n\n**25. Constraints-led or external-cue coaching.**\n> I don't apply it, as I don't fully understand it. I want to know more.\n\n**26. The five expectations: any deliberate exception?**\n> These are more a relic of another coaching era that has held over. I would say none, but\n> they are rarely mentioned around here any more.\n\n**27. Attribution when outside frameworks meet \"we/our, not me/my\".**\n> We fold it into the mantra of \"this is how we do our business.\"\n\n**28. Coach-facing reports: what they look like, who builds them.**\n> Strength staff will build it most likely; we don't have sport science support for most teams.\n> When applicable, sport science staff would provide it.\n\n**29. TMG.**\n> Only Chris Ruf with Track still uses it.\n\n**30. The 16-week Intern Sport Science Curriculum.**\n> It has not been built yet.\n\n---\n\n## What changes in Flint (to apply; not yet applied)\n\nBaseline (`method-baseline.md`):\n- Course positions (Vermeil, undulating periodization, Dynamic Correspondence, football\n  energy-system stack): Inferred to **Explicit, Eric's**.\n- Testing: Assessment Manual V12, the V4 assessment table and its schedule are **legacy**; a new\n  manual built on the NCAA technology and sport-science recommendations is in progress. No\n  common cadence; each sport sets its own. **No true 1RM testing** anywhere; maxes are\n  projected from observation plus history. COD is not tested in most sports.\n- Monitoring: mRSI read on **Wednesdays**, individualized thresholds (percent change from\n  baseline or rolling average; SD and SWC for signal versus noise). No formal GPS thresholds.\n  **DSI** (CMJ vs IMTP) chooses plyometric versus max-strength emphasis.\n- Data: VALD holds force data, OpenField holds Catapult, Kitman AMS aggregates; **the source\n  system wins** when Kitman disagrees.\n- Injury risk: ACWR distrusted (\"raw, has error, misleading, lacks context\"); no formal risk\n  model; movement screens are logged, not acted on.\n- Recovery: load design only, nothing objective tracked. Nutrition: no proactive data flow.\n- RTP: Applied Performance owns testing and progression and collaborates on loading; no\n  readiness assessment; the written protocol is the UE and LE rehab program templates.\n- Conditioning: age-based anaerobic-threshold HR; glycolytic work programmed in the off-season\n  only. TMG: Chris Ruf with Track only. Intern curriculum: not built.\n- Decision rule for any test or technology: \"If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a\n  direct need, then it doesn't have a place.\"\n- Coach's eye: flags where to look; data then investigates.\n- Five expectations: legacy culture language, rarely used.\n\nPeaking synthesis (`docs/synthesis/peaking-and-taper-in-season-football.md`):\n- Peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window.\n- Real week: Sun off, Mon heavy lift, Tue and Wed heavy practice plus a lighter Wed lift; heavy\n  work ends **72 h** before kickoff (the synthesis inferred 48 h).\n- Residual effects: Eric sides with **Issurin** (speed fades first), against the Class 4 slide.\n- Byes: three practices, slightly higher volume, recovery focus plus speed and strength\n  top-up, harder for low-snap players.\n\nOpen for Eric: in-season strength sets and reps (Q18, not available); stage the two RTP\ntemplates; constraints-led coaching is a learning request (Q25), a candidate for a primer.\n","originalFile":"---\ndate: 2026-09-28\nsource: live /interview-me session (Claude Code)\nquestions: docs/interview-prep.md (27) + docs/synthesis/peaking-and-taper-in-season-football.md §8 (6); overlaps merged\nstatus: in progress\n---\n\n# Flint method interview, 2026-09-28\n\nEric's answers, in his words (typos fixed only). Each answer turns the matching\n`method-baseline.md` position from Inferred to Explicit, or records what he said instead.\n\n## Round 1: the load-bearing ones\n\n**1. Course material (HHPR 5355): which positions are yours?** Vermeil hierarchy as compass;\nundulating over linear periodization; Dynamic Correspondence; the football energy-system\npriority stack.\n> All of them.\n\n**2. mRSI readiness rule** (also peaking Q5). Which drop, on which day, changes the plan?\nSeparate thresholds for linemen and low-snap players?\n> Wednesdays, the thresholds should be individualized.\n\n**3. Retiring a test or technology.** General rule, or a fresh call each time?\n> If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a direct need, then it doesn't have a place.\n\n**4. Return to play.** What Applied Performance contributes, where its input ends; is there a\nwritten phased protocol?\n> Testing and progression. I collaborate on loading and progression decisions when needed.\n> It doesn't really end. There is a written protocol, I can provide it.\n\n**5. Department model.** Peer discipline enabling Athletic Performance, Athletic Medicine and\nNutrition, or in-house programming support for Athletic Performance?\n> Both.\n\n**6. Coach's eye versus objective data.** Where is the line?\n> There is a place for the coach's eye: when something looks off, it can direct you where to\n> look and gather data to investigate further.\n\n**2a. Follow-up: individualized against what?**\n> Percent change from baseline or a rolling average. We've used standard deviations and SWC to\n> diagnose signal versus noise.\n\n**4a. Follow-up: where is the written RTP protocol?**\n> `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Downloads\\Baylor UE Rehab Program Template.docx` and\n> `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Downloads\\Baylor LE Rehab Program Template.docx`\n\n(Not yet staged. Next step: copy both to Drive and stage a text copy for the RTP synthesis.)\n\n## Round 2: testing, force plate, load\n\n**7. GPS and load thresholds per sport.** Do any exist informally?\n> They do not exist formally.\n\n**8. Assessment Manual V12.** Current, partly replaced, or legacy? (Includes the CMJ cue\nconflict with Force Plate Testing V3.)\n> It is not current. We are in the process of developing a new, more robust version that will\n> incorporate the NCAA technology/sport science recommendations.\n\n**9. Testing cadence per sport.** Changed since the V4 Assessment Table?\n> That assessment schedule was never used. We currently don't have a consistent cadence across\n> all sports. Individual sports have their own unique schedules.\n\n**10. Rep-max to true 1RM.** What marker moves an athlete to true 1RM testing?\n> No sports perform true 1RM testing. Instead things are ballparked based on other information\n> we're grabbing on the athlete: watching them move in real time, then using historical context\n> to make future programming decisions around projected 1RMs.\n\n**11. System of record.** Kitman Labs versus VALD Hub or OpenField.\n> Each system has its own unique data. All our force plate and force-measuring data lives in\n> VALD. All our information gets aggregated to Kitman Labs AMS, and our Catapult information\n> lives in OpenField. They're separate entities; one doesn't overrule the other. If Kitman had\n> wrong numbers from VALD or OpenField, we would go to those sources to correct it: the source\n> systems overrule any errors Kitman made bringing data into the platform.\n\n**12. Injury-risk model, ACWR, movement screens.**\n> We dabble with ACWR but I don't personally trust it for anything. I think it's raw, has error,\n> is misleading, and lacks context generally. We don't have any formal risk model that we follow.\n> We've avoided them up to this point, but there's not a really good reason why. From a movement\n> screen standpoint, there isn't anything that changes decisions made to a program; it's more or\n> less just collected and referred to for future needs.\n\n**13. Change of direction.**\n> We don't test change of direction in most sports. If the data is captured, it's on an\n> individual sport basis and that information lives with that coach.\n\n## Round 3: strength, conditioning, peaking\n\n**14. What \"peak\" means in football; a distinct postseason taper?** (peaking Q1 and Q6)\n> Yes, in football you want to peak every Saturday, but definitely throughout the month of\n> November as you get closer to the postseason, bowl eligibility, things like that. If you're in\n> the upper echelon of programs, that's the push for the College Football Playoff. That's when\n> players need to be at or near their peak as they head into the postseason and playoffs.\n\n**15. The week after a game: where do the heaviest lifts and practices sit?** (peaking Q2)\n> - Sunday is the off day.\n> - Monday is a heavy lift day.\n> - Tuesday and Wednesday are heavy practice days, and there's a lighter lift on Wednesday.\n> - Games are played on Saturday, so 72 hours before the game is where the lifting ends and the\n>   hard practices end.\n\n**16. Which quality fades first when untrained?** (peaking Q3; Issurin vs the Class 4 slides)\n> I believe Issurin. It drives our speed programming in the off-season and what drives\n> sprinting recommendations in season.\n\nNote: this settles the residual-effects conflict for Eric (speed fades fastest), against the\nClass 4 slide (p. 47). The synthesis's bottom line 4 and the `contested` tag on the Issurin\nresiduals entry should record Eric's position.\n\n**17. Bye weeks.** (peaking Q4)\n> The bye weeks usually have three practice days. There's extra rest and recovery scheduled.\n> The three practice days are probably slightly on the higher-volume side compared with a game\n> week, but there's definitely a recovery focus, along with a speed and strength top-up. It's\n> more based on who's playing: guys that aren't getting a ton of play will probably practice\n> harder that week than guys that have gotten a ton of snaps in games up to that point.\n\n**18. In-season strength dose (sets, reps, load).**\n> I don't have the specific sets and reps.\n\n**19. Glycolytic conditioning in football.** Has \"don't program it directly\" held up?\n> It does hold up, but our staff does program it in the off-season.\n\n**20. Anaerobic-threshold heart rate method.**\n> Age-based.\n\n**21. SJ-CMJ gap on ForceDecks.**\n> We use a version of it, but we incorporate the countermovement jump and the isometric\n> mid-thigh pull to get a dynamic strength index, to determine whether we need more plyometric\n> training or more max-strength training.\n\nNote: this is the dynamic strength index (CMJ against IMTP peak force), not the SJ-CMJ gap.\nThe *Supertraining* entries and the Kozinc 2022 brief address SJ-CMJ; DSI needs its own\nevidence check before any synthesis cites it.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":131,"oldLines":3,"newStart":131,"newLines":73,"lines":[" Note: this is the dynamic strength index (CMJ against IMTP peak force), not the SJ-CMJ gap."," The *Supertraining* entries and the Kozinc 2022 brief address SJ-CMJ; DSI needs its own"," evidence check before any synthesis cites it.","+","+## Round 4: RTP, recovery, nutrition, coaching, staff, reporting","+","+**22. RTP: how do wellness, NordBord and ForceFrame combine into one readiness decision?**","+> We don't assess readiness. The template answers that.","+","+**23. Recovery and sleep monitoring.**","+> It's handled through training-load design alone. We're not tracking anything objective.","+","+**24. Force-plate or GPS data to Performance Nutrition.**","+> It does not. Only if there is an issue will bodyweight be consulted. Nothing proactive.","+","+**25. Constraints-led or external-cue coaching.**","+> I don't apply it, as I don't fully understand it. I want to know more.","+","+**26. The five expectations: any deliberate exception?**","+> These are more a relic of another coaching era that has held over. I would say none, but","+> they are rarely mentioned around here any more.","+","+**27. Attribution when outside frameworks meet \"we/our, not me/my\".**","+> We fold it into the mantra of \"this is how we do our business.\"","+","+**28. Coach-facing reports: what they look like, who builds them.**","+> Strength staff will build it most likely; we don't have sport science support for most teams.","+> When applicable, sport science staff would provide it.","+","+**29. TMG.**","+> Only Chris Ruf with Track still uses it.","+","+**30. The 16-week Intern Sport Science Curriculum.**","+> It has not been built yet.","+","+---","+","+## What changes in Flint (to apply; not yet applied)","+","+Baseline (`method-baseline.md`):","+- Course positions (Vermeil, undulating periodization, Dynamic Correspondence, football","+  energy-system stack): Inferred to **Explicit, Eric's**.","+- Testing: Assessment Manual V12, the V4 assessment table and its schedule are **legacy**; a new","+  manual built on the NCAA technology and sport-science recommendations is in progress. No","+  common cadence; each sport sets its own. **No true 1RM testing** anywhere; maxes are","+  projected from observation plus history. COD is not tested in most sports.","+- Monitoring: mRSI read on **Wednesdays**, individualized thresholds (percent change from","+  baseline or rolling average; SD and SWC for signal versus noise). No formal GPS thresholds.","+  **DSI** (CMJ vs IMTP) chooses plyometric versus max-strength emphasis.","+- Data: VALD holds force data, OpenField holds Catapult, Kitman AMS aggregates; **the source","+  system wins** when Kitman disagrees.","+- Injury risk: ACWR distrusted (\"raw, has error, misleading, lacks context\"); no formal risk","+  model; movement screens are logged, not acted on.","+- Recovery: load design only, nothing objective tracked. Nutrition: no proactive data flow.","+- RTP: Applied Performance owns testing and progression and collaborates on loading; no","+  readiness assessment; the written protocol is the UE and LE rehab program templates.","+- Conditioning: age-based anaerobic-threshold HR; glycolytic work programmed in the off-season","+  only. TMG: Chris Ruf with Track only. Intern curriculum: not built.","+- Decision rule for any test or technology: \"If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a","+  direct need, then it doesn't have a place.\"","+- Coach's eye: flags where to look; data then investigates.","+- Five expectations: legacy culture language, rarely used.","+","+Peaking synthesis (`docs/synthesis/peaking-and-taper-in-season-football.md`):","+- Peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window.","+- Real week: Sun off, Mon heavy lift, Tue and Wed heavy practice plus a lighter Wed lift; heavy","+  work ends **72 h** before kickoff (the synthesis inferred 48 h).","+- Residual effects: Eric sides with **Issurin** (speed fades first), against the Class 4 slide.","+- Byes: three practices, slightly higher volume, recovery focus plus speed and strength","+  top-up, harder for low-snap players.","+","+Open for Eric: in-season strength sets and reps (Q18, not available); stage the two RTP","+templates; constraints-led coaching is a learning request (Q25), a candidate for a primer."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"45650673-5f97-4888-b8ec-6b24dd14be95","session_id":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"step1-triage"}
