{"parentUuid":"76cf63cc-31c2-4e48-ad45-27f491b7cf02","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PreToolUse:Agent","toolUseID":"toolu_019zU1dFDrcR39ihx59KNuds","hookEvent":"PreToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"PreToolUse\",\"updatedInput\":{\"description\":\"Apply interview answers to Flint docs\",\"prompt\":\"You are working in the Flint repo worktree at C:\\\\Users\\\\eric_rash\\\\Desktop\\\\DEV\\\\Flint\\\\.claude\\\\worktrees\\\\step1-triage (branch step1-triage). Read AGENTS.md there first and follow its conventions exactly: every voice labelled (source authors plain; Eric's judgments as \\\"Eric's review:\\\" or here \\\"Eric (interview 2026-09-28):\\\"; your own reasoning as \\\"Inference:\\\"), no unlabelled judgments, no em dashes, plain direct writing, and every number carries its page or source.\\n\\nMachine note: this is a Windows laptop with little free RAM. Do NOT start background jobs, OCR, transcription, memsearch index, or any heavy process. Editing files and git only.\\n\\nTASK: Eric finished his method interview today. His answers are in docs/interview/2026-09-28-method-interview.md, and its final section \\\"What changes in Flint\\\" lists every change. Apply them.\\n\\n1. docs/method-baseline.md\\n   - For each position the interview settles, update the bullet: change the tag from Inferred to Explicit where Eric claimed it (Q1: Vermeil hierarchy, undulating periodization, Dynamic Correspondence, the football energy-system priority stack are all his), or rewrite it to what he actually said, citing \\\"interview 2026-09-28, Qn\\\".\\n   - Relabel as legacy what he said is legacy: Assessment Manual V12, the V4 assessment table and its schedule, 1RM-testing assumptions, TMG (only Chris Ruf with Track). Keep the old content, marked legacy, rather than deleting it.\\n   - Add new positions he stated that the baseline lacks (test or technology rule Q3; coach's eye Q6; mRSI Wednesday reads with individualized thresholds Q2/2a; DSI Q21; system of record Q11; ACWR distrust Q12; recovery through load design Q23; no nutrition data flow Q24; RTP role Q4/Q22 with the UE and LE rehab templates as the written protocol; age-based AT HR Q20; glycolytic work off-season only Q19; five expectations legacy Q26; attribution Q27; reports built by strength staff Q28; intern curriculum not built Q30; COD not tested Q13; no formal GPS thresholds Q7; no common testing cadence Q9).\\n   - Where the baseline had a \\\"Tensions\\\" list, mark each tension the interview resolved and how.\\n   - Update any \\\"thin\\\" discipline flags if the interview now answers them (often the answer is \\\"nothing formal\\\": say so explicitly, which is itself a finding).\\n\\n2. docs/synthesis/peaking-and-taper-in-season-football.md: make rev 3.\\n   - Read docs/synthesis/_TEMPLATE.md and the file's own revision notes first; keep the structure.\\n   - Replace section 8 \\\"Questions for Eric\\\" with Eric's answers (Q14-Q17, Q2/2a, Q16 settles the residual-effects conflict in favour of Issurin for Eric).\\n   - Correct the bottom line to his real week: Sun off, Mon heavy lift, Tue and Wed heavy practice plus a lighter Wed lift, heavy work ends 72 h before a Saturday kickoff (rev 2 inferred about 48 h). Peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window. Byes: three practices, slightly higher volume, recovery focus plus speed and strength top-up, harder for low-snap players.\\n   - Record where his practice now agrees or disagrees with the sources (e.g. Monday heavy lift sits at about 48 h post-game, inside the window where Norris 2019 and Cosio 2026 report fast-force and hamstring deficits; state it as a tension with labels, not a verdict). Bottom line 4 (aerobic kept weekly, \\\"contested\\\") needs rewriting given he sides with Issurin; keep the evidence both ways.\\n   - Update the date/revision marker to rev 3, 2026-09-28.\\n   - Do not invent evidence. Use only sources already cited in the file or in the vault/staging library entries it links.\\n\\n3. docs/interview-prep.md: add a one-line note at the top that the interview was completed 2026-09-28 with a pointer to the answers file.\\n\\n4. Commit on step1-triage with a clear message (end the message with these two lines exactly:\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Tr551YYoFPCB1DSy2iUtz5\\n). Do not push; do not touch any other branch or repo; do not edit the SecondBrain vault.\\n\\nReturn: a short list of what changed in each file (counts of bullets relabelled, added, legacy), any interview answer you could not map cleanly, and any contradiction you noticed between his answers and the documents or between two of his answers. Keep it under 400 words.\\n<context_window_protection>\\n  <priority_instructions>\\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\\n  </priority_instructions>\\n\\n  <deferred_tool_bootstrap>\\n    The context-mode tools below may be DEFERRED in your harness — their schemas are not loaded yet, so calling them directly fails (e.g. \\\"tool not found\\\" / InputValidationError). Load them ONCE before your first ctx_* call:\\n    ToolSearch(query: \\\"select:mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index\\\")\\n    After that they are callable. If any ctx_* call fails as not-found, ToolSearch it and retry — do NOT fall back to Bash/Read just because the schema was not loaded yet.\\n  </deferred_tool_bootstrap>\\n\\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \\\"timeline\\\")\\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\\n       - Each command: {label: \\\"section header\\\", command: \\\"shell command\\\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\\\"q1\\\", \\\"q2\\\", ...])\\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n\\n  <when_not_to_use>\\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\\n  </when_not_to_use>\\n\\n  <file_writing_policy>\\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\\n  </file_writing_policy>\\n\\n  <output_constraints>\\n    <artifact_policy>\\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\\n    </artifact_policy>\\n  </output_constraints>\\n  <session_continuity>\\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\\n  </session_continuity>\\n\\n</context_window_protection>\",\"subagent_type\":\"general-purpose\"}}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"\"C:/Program Files/nodejs/node.exe\" \"C:/Users/eric_rash/.claude/plugins/cache/context-mode/context-mode/1.0.169/hooks/pretooluse.mjs\"","durationMs":167},"type":"attachment","uuid":"57c23f37-4d26-4d33-8603-eea0f4e33870","timestamp":"2026-09-28T18:00:43.022Z","session_id":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"step1-triage"}
