{"parentUuid":"1c6f80d2-274e-47eb-a7f4-8acbd4316a09","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfWPLp9zrdgbwsQjshxSu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KEAvgAeZ9m4nnSr4NfP2oD","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Write constraints-led coaching primer","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"Write a primer on the constraints-led approach (CLA) to coaching for Eric Rash, Director of Applied Performance at Baylor Athletics. In his method interview today (Flint repo, docs/interview/2026-09-28-method-interview.md, Q25) he said: \"I don't apply it, as I don't fully understand it. I want to know more.\"\n\nContext you must use:\n- Work in the Flint worktree C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage. Read its AGENTS.md first and follow the conventions: every voice labelled (source authors plain; your reasoning \"Inference:\"), every number or claim carries a page, timestamp or citation, no em dashes, plain direct writing.\n- Library first. Invoke the Skill tool with skill \"sports-science-research-grounding\" and follow it: search Eric's Zotero library (zotero-research MCP tools, loaded via ToolSearch) before any external source, and label external sources explicitly.\n- His SecondBrain vault is at C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain. Its Knowledge/library folder holds atomized course and book entries, including the SCN sprint course (Moyer/Natera), Harries, Evans, Old Bull and others. Search it with Grep for constraints-led, ecological dynamics, affordance, representative design, nonlinear pedagogy, \"repetition without repetition\", external focus, and differential learning.\n- He is ALSO building an \"RTP Constraints-Led Approach\" course: C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\RTP_Constraints_Course (read its docs/ and CLAUDE.md; READ ONLY, do not edit that repo). The primer should connect to it. Note the tension plainly and kindly: he is authoring a CLA course but says he doesn't fully understand CLA. Frame the primer as what he needs to own that course.\n- Do NOT run memsearch index or any heavy background process (the laptop has about 1 GB free RAM). memsearch search is allowed but may time out; skip it if it does.\n\nPrimer content (aim for 1,500 to 2,500 words, in docs/primers/constraints-led-approach.md):\n1. CLA in one paragraph, then the core ideas: Newell's constraints (performer, task, environment), ecological dynamics, affordances, self-organisation, representative learning design, degeneracy and \"repetition without repetition\" (Bernstein), variability, nonlinear pedagogy. Plain definitions.\n2. How it differs from what he already does: traditional drill-and-cue, internal vs external focus of attention (Wulf), and where the evidence is strong vs thin. Be honest about the evidence base (much is theoretical or small-sample; say so with citations).\n3. What CLA looks like in an Athletic Performance and RTP setting: sprint and change-of-direction work, rehab reintegration (his LE and UE rehab templates are staged at staging/rtp/*.md in the Flint worktree; map CLA onto their Reintegration and Evaluation phases), and the weight room (where it applies less).\n4. Five to eight concrete \"when X, I do Y, because Z\" example rules he could adopt or reject.\n5. The main critiques and limits.\n6. A short reading path: the three to five best sources to read first, from his library where possible.\n7. Three questions for Eric to answer that would turn this into his own position.\n\nCommit the file on branch step1-triage (message ending with these two lines exactly:\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Tr551YYoFPCB1DSy2iUtz5\n). Do not push. Do not edit any other repo or the vault.\n\nReturn in under 250 words: the file path, which library sources you used (Zotero vs vault vs external counts), the strongest and weakest evidence points, and anything notable about the RTP CLA course's current state.","run_in_background":"true"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":832,"cache_read_input_tokens":227595,"output_tokens":1464,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":832,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1464,"cache_read_input_tokens":227595,"cache_creation_input_tokens":832,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":832},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01KEAvgAeZ9m4nnSr4NfP2oD":{"description":"Write constraints-led coaching primer","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"Write a primer on the constraints-led approach (CLA) to coaching for Eric Rash, Director of Applied Performance at Baylor Athletics. In his method interview today (Flint repo, docs/interview/2026-09-28-method-interview.md, Q25) he said: \"I don't apply it, as I don't fully understand it. I want to know more.\"\n\nContext you must use:\n- Work in the Flint worktree C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage. Read its AGENTS.md first and follow the conventions: every voice labelled (source authors plain; your reasoning \"Inference:\"), every number or claim carries a page, timestamp or citation, no em dashes, plain direct writing.\n- Library first. Invoke the Skill tool with skill \"sports-science-research-grounding\" and follow it: search Eric's Zotero library (zotero-research MCP tools, loaded via ToolSearch) before any external source, and label external sources explicitly.\n- His SecondBrain vault is at C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain. Its Knowledge/library folder holds atomized course and book entries, including the SCN sprint course (Moyer/Natera), Harries, Evans, Old Bull and others. Search it with Grep for constraints-led, ecological dynamics, affordance, representative design, nonlinear pedagogy, \"repetition without repetition\", external focus, and differential learning.\n- He is ALSO building an \"RTP Constraints-Led Approach\" course: C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\RTP_Constraints_Course (read its docs/ and CLAUDE.md; READ ONLY, do not edit that repo). The primer should connect to it. Note the tension plainly and kindly: he is authoring a CLA course but says he doesn't fully understand CLA. Frame the primer as what he needs to own that course.\n- Do NOT run memsearch index or any heavy background process (the laptop has about 1 GB free RAM). memsearch search is allowed but may time out; skip it if it does.\n\nPrimer content (aim for 1,500 to 2,500 words, in docs/primers/constraints-led-approach.md):\n1. CLA in one paragraph, then the core ideas: Newell's constraints (performer, task, environment), ecological dynamics, affordances, self-organisation, representative learning design, degeneracy and \"repetition without repetition\" (Bernstein), variability, nonlinear pedagogy. Plain definitions.\n2. How it differs from what he already does: traditional drill-and-cue, internal vs external focus of attention (Wulf), and where the evidence is strong vs thin. Be honest about the evidence base (much is theoretical or small-sample; say so with citations).\n3. What CLA looks like in an Athletic Performance and RTP setting: sprint and change-of-direction work, rehab reintegration (his LE and UE rehab templates are staged at staging/rtp/*.md in the Flint worktree; map CLA onto their Reintegration and Evaluation phases), and the weight room (where it applies less).\n4. Five to eight concrete \"when X, I do Y, because Z\" example rules he could adopt or reject.\n5. The main critiques and limits.\n6. A short reading path: the three to five best sources to read first, from his library where possible.\n7. Three questions for Eric to answer that would turn this into his own position.\n\nCommit the file on branch step1-triage (message ending with these two lines exactly:\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Tr551YYoFPCB1DSy2iUtz5\n). Do not push. Do not edit any other repo or the vault.\n\nReturn in under 250 words: the file path, which library sources you used (Zotero vs vault vs external counts), the strongest and weakest evidence points, and anything notable about the RTP CLA course's current state.","run_in_background":"true"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfWPLoDBrUshe8PLbLuNN","type":"assistant","uuid":"bb597c66-b143-4fde-a0a6-2a26d541ac7c","timestamp":"2026-09-28T19:25:53.845Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"step1-triage"}
