{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-09-28T19:33:24.651Z","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a1113ba0d4b3d6617</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01KEAvgAeZ9m4nnSr4NfP2oD</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-eric-rash-Desktop-DEV-Flint--claude-worktrees-step1-triage\\e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58\\tasks\\a1113ba0d4b3d6617.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Write constraints-led coaching primer\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I wrote the primer and committed it on step1-triage in two commits (eae21b4, then 8c16789 for citation fixes). Nothing was pushed. It runs about 2,790 words, over the 2,500 target, mostly because of inline citations.\n\n**File:** `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\primers\\constraints-led-approach.md`\n\n**Sources used:**\n- **Zotero:** 11 papers read at page level (7M8YQMKU, CA2CRR6Z, XZ8KI5V7, WENT2642, T4HB866Z, CBJGJQX5, 2MPW3ZQ3, EYKZD68B, SK32YKG2, G5X9J23E, BJMBTJ8L). Davids 2008, Renshaw 2019 and Chua 2021 are there as records only, with no full text.\n- **Vault:** about 17 entries: 12 from Myszka, 2 from Harries, 3 from Moyer.\n- **External:** 1, the Bobrownicki critique's abstract.\n- **Not read, cited second-hand:** Bernstein, McKay 2024, Choo 2024.\n- The Zotero tool rated coverage \"thin\" only because none of the papers are about collegiate athletes. The library actually covers CLA well.\n\n**Strongest evidence:**\n- Young 2015: physical qualities explained 56.7% of the variance in pre-planned change-of-direction speed but only 14.2% in reactive agility. So the Evaluation-phase tests in the rehab templates (10-yard sprint, 5-10-5) certify speed, not agility.\n- Winkelman 2017: external cues beat internal cues in collegiate soccer players but made no difference in experienced sprinters.\n\n**Weakest evidence:** No CLA trial exists in collegiate football or return to play. The main supporting study (Lee 2014) is 24 ten-year-old girls learning a tennis forehand. The ACL differential-learning study is one athlete. Singh 2021 is a level-5 commentary.\n\n**RTP course:** Eric is the course's sponsor. Joe Smith is the learner, and every \"ask the agent\" in Joe's package becomes \"ask Eric\". The primer frames the gap that way.\n- Two of eight lessons are built.\n- There's no sign Joe has done the lesson 0001 field task.\n- Gray 2015 has only been read as an abstract.\n- Bobrownicki, the anchor for lesson 8, isn't in Zotero.\n- The Myszka PDF that `_book.md` and the course's CLAUDE.md place in `sources/` isn't there.\n\nFlint's `docs/STATUS.md` is untouched.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>167824</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>59</tool_uses><duration_ms>450618</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
