{"parentUuid":"9d4596aa-4b98-4b51-af22-1486edabbe76","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"28ec5541-f125-4afa-94de-6e3e5c2897ff","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VN2aHVbzNeDeDXE3SAaznh","type":"tool_result","content":"_run_cvasps.log\nlandonevansphysicalpreparationinthencaaacomplementaryapproach.md\n/c/Users/eric_rash/Desktop/DEV/Flint/.claude/worktrees/step1-triage/docs/cvasps-corroboration-proposals.md\n0","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"74608682-f20e-4df8-97a2-ffb8b21ebed4","timestamp":"2026-09-28T20:23:13.018Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"_run_cvasps.log\nlandonevansphysicalpreparationinthencaaacomplementaryapproach.md\n/c/Users/eric_rash/Desktop/DEV/Flint/.claude/worktrees/step1-triage/docs/cvasps-corroboration-proposals.md\n0","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"returnCodeInterpretation":"No matches found","noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9d4596aa-4b98-4b51-af22-1486edabbe76","session_id":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"step1-triage"}
{"parentUuid":"589ec0c6-18d3-4f1f-b174-b86a5fe34175","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"28ec5541-f125-4afa-94de-6e3e5c2897ff","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Kisp6zFUanRSKSMWNJ2MGn","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: memsearch-hook-timeout-cap\n3\tdescription: \"the SessionEnd memsearch hook's fixed 45s cap was outrun by corpus growth, so every session-end index timed out and incremental recall silently degraded to the 09:00 nightly; fixed 2026-09-02 by detaching the indexer\"\n4\tmetadata: \n5\t  node_type: memory\n6\t  type: project\n7\t  modified: 2026-09-02T12:33:29.526Z\n8\t  originSessionId: 00fe7fc2-f53c-471d-b407-c777e8cc679c\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t`~/.claude/hooks/memsearch-sync.js` (SessionEnd) ran `memsearch index <10 paths>` through\n12\t`spawnSync` with a **45s cap**, sized in July against a \"~17s fixed floor, ~5s incremental\"\n13\tmeasurement. Measured again 2026-09-02 against the current corpus (~1,450 files;\n14\t`SecondBrain/Inbox/meetings` alone is 593 and `SecondBrain/Knowledge` 334):\n15\t\n16\t| run | time |\n17\t|---|---|\n18\t| no-op, nothing new to embed | **33s** (was ~17s) |\n19\t| one day of accumulated content | **278s** (232 chunks, ~1s/chunk) |\n20\t\n21\tSo the cap left ~12s of embedding budget — under a dozen new chunks. **Every ordinary\n22\tsession-end index was timing out.** Incremental indexing had quietly degraded to \"whatever\n23\tthe 09:00 nightly full reindex picks up\", leaving search up to 24h stale.\n24\t\n25\t**Why it stayed invisible for weeks.** Three dampers stacked:\n26\t`daily-backup.ps1` resets `.memsearch-timeout-streak` every morning, the escalation\n27\tthreshold is 6 consecutive timeouts, and the hook writes only to `.memsearch-index.log`\n28\tbelow that. So a normal day of 2-4 session-ends never reached the threshold and never\n29\tsurfaced. It took **six session-ends inside 47 minutes** (2026-09-01, 21:09-21:56) to\n30\tproduce a 7-long streak and one `.errors.log` line. The alarm fired on burst *frequency*,\n31\tnever on the underlying constant failure.\n32\t\n33\t**The misread that line invites:** ETIMEDOUT + a clustered burst pattern-matches\n34\t[[milvus-lock-misdiagnosis]] (session-end lock contention), and the hook's own comments\n35\tsay exactly that. It was not contention — `pgrep`/`lsof` showed no holder and the DB was\n36\thealthy. **Time the actual command before believing the comment.**\n37\t\n38\t**Fix (2026-09-02): detached background worker.** The hook now re-execs itself as\n39\t`node memsearch-sync.js --worker` with `detached: true, stdio: 'ignore'` and `.unref()`,\n40\tthen exits — measured **42ms**. The worker keeps the entire original body, so it owns the\n41\tPID lock (clustered session-ends still elect one indexer), writes `INDEX_LOG`, and runs\n42\tevery error branch. Its `spawnSync` cap is now 600s: a runaway guard, not a budget, so an\n43\tETIMEDOUT there genuinely means \">10 minutes\".\n44\t\n45\t- `stdio: 'ignore'` is **not optional** — inherited or piped stdio keeps the harness's pipe\n46\t  open and it waits for the child anyway, rebuilding the timeout with extra steps.\n47\t- Self-respawn, **not** an `sh -c` wrapper: the wrapper would discard the lock ownership\n48\t  and all four error-classification branches.\n49\t- The old header justified staying synchronous with a **Windows** process-group race.\n50\t  That rationale died with the 2026-08-05 migration; the comment now says so, because the\n51\t  next reader would otherwise \"restore\" the bug.\n52\t\n53\t**How to apply:**\n54\t- A fixed timeout sized against a measured corpus is a **dated** number. When the corpus is\n55\t  something that grows every day (the vault, meetings, dailies), the cap has an expiry date\n56\t  nobody writes down. Prefer detaching over raising a cap; if a cap must stay, record the\n57\t  measurement and the date beside it so drift is visible.\n58\t- Never size a SessionEnd hook to block. Session teardown cannot wait minutes, so any\n59\t  design where \"correct\" means minutes must be asynchronous.\n60\t- Both harnesses wire their own copy. `~/.codex/hooks/memsearch-sync.js` is now a\n61\t  **symlink** to the Claude copy (2026-09-02) so the pair cannot drift again — same fix as\n62\t  `~/.codex/AGENTS.md`. Before \"fixing a hook\", ask which copy the harness wires; see\n63\t  [[memsearch-index-path-drift]] for the four-copy history.\n64\t- `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/codex-hooks/memsearch-sync.ps1` still has a 45s\n65\t  `WaitForExit(45000)`, but nothing wires it (Codex uses `hooks.json` → the `.js`). Dead\n66\t  Windows-era code; left alone deliberately.\n67\t\n68\tRelated: [[milvus-lock-misdiagnosis]], [[memsearch-index-path-drift]],\n69\t[[memory-compiler-pipeline]], [[backup-sync-system]], [[keystoneos-verification-habits]]\n70\t\n71\t**2026-09-22 — a slow nightly index was the Mac sleeping, not memsearch.** The daily backup's\n72\tfull index took 18 min for 0 new chunks (09-21 09:44) and 15 min for 309 chunks (09-21 18:12).\n73\tTimed while awake: no-op ~9s fixed startup, `dev-docs` (763 files) no-op 26s, full set 83s with\n74\t79 new chunks (~0.7s/chunk). `pmset -g log` showed 5-second DarkWakes in the morning window and\n75\tIdle Sleep twice mid-run in the evening. Fix: launchd `dailybackup` + the three data pulls now run\n76\tunder `/usr/bin/caffeinate -i -s` (`-s` holds only on AC; lid-shut on battery still sleeps).\n77\t**How to apply:** before tuning memsearch, line the slow window up against `pmset -g log`.\n78\tSame session: `daily-backup.ps1` now resolves `{DEV}` in index paths (it had skipped\n79\t`KeystoneOS/decisions`). Jev/TypeSafe does not help indexing: it judges, it does not embed.\n80\t\n81\t**2026-09-28 (Windows laptop) — the 600s cap is machine-speed-dependent, and headless sessions fanned it out.**\n82\t`memsearch-sync.js` ran back-to-back 600s-killed runs all day (streak 33), each peaking 2.7–3.1 GB, and\n83\tClaude Code's low-memory reaper killed other sessions' background jobs. Not a full reindex — memsearch is\n84\tincremental per file. Two causes stacked:\n85\t- A **10,138-chunk never-indexed backlog** (all of `transcripts/`, plus `dev-docs` sorted after\n86\t  `printing-press-library/…`). bge-m3 on this CPU embeds ~5 s/chunk (Mac 0.7–1 s), so each run cleared ~110.\n87\t- **Headless TypeSafe drive-triage `claude -p` sessions** (entrypoint `sdk-cli`) ended every ~30s — 257\n88\t  SessionEnds Sun 19–22h — plus every `flush.py` SDK session (the hook lacked session-end.py's\n89\t  `CLAUDE_INVOKED_BY` guard). The PID lock serialised them into a continuous chain; it bounds overlap, not load.\n90\t\n91\tWindows has **no nightly index**: `KeystoneOS-DailyBackup` is disabled and `Backup AI Sessions to Drive`\n92\tonly harvests dev-docs. So nothing drains a backlog and nothing resets the streak.\n93\t\n94\tFix: the hook skips `CLAUDE_INVOKED_BY` / entrypoint `sdk*`, with a 30-min start cooldown\n95\t(`transcripts/.memsearch-last-run`, checked while holding the lock); `~/.memsearch/config.toml`\n96\t`batch_size = 8` (peak measured 921 MB vs 3.1 GB); one-off drain task `\\Eric\\memsearch-drain-once` →\n97\t`~/.claude/scripts/memsearch-drain.ps1`, log `~/.claude/scripts/logs/memsearch-drain.log`.\n98\tStill open: the Codex Stop hook `KeystoneOS/scripts/codex-hooks/memsearch-sync.ps1` (per turn, 45s) has no cooldown.\n99\t**How to apply:** \"memsearch eats RAM\" on Windows → check the streak in `.errors.log` and do a read-only backlog\n100\tdry run (memsearch's scanner + chunker + `compute_chunk_id` vs Milvus `chunk_hash`) before blaming Milvus.\n101\t"}]},"uuid":"c36a811c-76d9-481c-8099-f1fd9a740342","timestamp":"2026-09-28T20:23:13.540Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\.claude\\projects\\C--Users-eric-rash-Desktop-DEV\\memory\\project_memsearch-hook-timeout-cap.md","content":"---\nname: memsearch-hook-timeout-cap\ndescription: \"the SessionEnd memsearch hook's fixed 45s cap was outrun by corpus growth, so every session-end index timed out and incremental recall silently degraded to the 09:00 nightly; fixed 2026-09-02 by detaching the indexer\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  modified: 2026-09-02T12:33:29.526Z\n  originSessionId: 00fe7fc2-f53c-471d-b407-c777e8cc679c\n---\n\n`~/.claude/hooks/memsearch-sync.js` (SessionEnd) ran `memsearch index <10 paths>` through\n`spawnSync` with a **45s cap**, sized in July against a \"~17s fixed floor, ~5s incremental\"\nmeasurement. Measured again 2026-09-02 against the current corpus (~1,450 files;\n`SecondBrain/Inbox/meetings` alone is 593 and `SecondBrain/Knowledge` 334):\n\n| run | time |\n|---|---|\n| no-op, nothing new to embed | **33s** (was ~17s) |\n| one day of accumulated content | **278s** (232 chunks, ~1s/chunk) |\n\nSo the cap left ~12s of embedding budget — under a dozen new chunks. **Every ordinary\nsession-end index was timing out.** Incremental indexing had quietly degraded to \"whatever\nthe 09:00 nightly full reindex picks up\", leaving search up to 24h stale.\n\n**Why it stayed invisible for weeks.** Three dampers stacked:\n`daily-backup.ps1` resets `.memsearch-timeout-streak` every morning, the escalation\nthreshold is 6 consecutive timeouts, and the hook writes only to `.memsearch-index.log`\nbelow that. So a normal day of 2-4 session-ends never reached the threshold and never\nsurfaced. It took **six session-ends inside 47 minutes** (2026-09-01, 21:09-21:56) to\nproduce a 7-long streak and one `.errors.log` line. The alarm fired on burst *frequency*,\nnever on the underlying constant failure.\n\n**The misread that line invites:** ETIMEDOUT + a clustered burst pattern-matches\n[[milvus-lock-misdiagnosis]] (session-end lock contention), and the hook's own comments\nsay exactly that. It was not contention — `pgrep`/`lsof` showed no holder and the DB was\nhealthy. **Time the actual command before believing the comment.**\n\n**Fix (2026-09-02): detached background worker.** The hook now re-execs itself as\n`node memsearch-sync.js --worker` with `detached: true, stdio: 'ignore'` and `.unref()`,\nthen exits — measured **42ms**. The worker keeps the entire original body, so it owns the\nPID lock (clustered session-ends still elect one indexer), writes `INDEX_LOG`, and runs\nevery error branch. Its `spawnSync` cap is now 600s: a runaway guard, not a budget, so an\nETIMEDOUT there genuinely means \">10 minutes\".\n\n- `stdio: 'ignore'` is **not optional** — inherited or piped stdio keeps the harness's pipe\n  open and it waits for the child anyway, rebuilding the timeout with extra steps.\n- Self-respawn, **not** an `sh -c` wrapper: the wrapper would discard the lock ownership\n  and all four error-classification branches.\n- The old header justified staying synchronous with a **Windows** process-group race.\n  That rationale died with the 2026-08-05 migration; the comment now says so, because the\n  next reader would otherwise \"restore\" the bug.\n\n**How to apply:**\n- A fixed timeout sized against a measured corpus is a **dated** number. When the corpus is\n  something that grows every day (the vault, meetings, dailies), the cap has an expiry date\n  nobody writes down. Prefer detaching over raising a cap; if a cap must stay, record the\n  measurement and the date beside it so drift is visible.\n- Never size a SessionEnd hook to block. Session teardown cannot wait minutes, so any\n  design where \"correct\" means minutes must be asynchronous.\n- Both harnesses wire their own copy. `~/.codex/hooks/memsearch-sync.js` is now a\n  **symlink** to the Claude copy (2026-09-02) so the pair cannot drift again — same fix as\n  `~/.codex/AGENTS.md`. Before \"fixing a hook\", ask which copy the harness wires; see\n  [[memsearch-index-path-drift]] for the four-copy history.\n- `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/codex-hooks/memsearch-sync.ps1` still has a 45s\n  `WaitForExit(45000)`, but nothing wires it (Codex uses `hooks.json` → the `.js`). Dead\n  Windows-era code; left alone deliberately.\n\nRelated: [[milvus-lock-misdiagnosis]], [[memsearch-index-path-drift]],\n[[memory-compiler-pipeline]], [[backup-sync-system]], [[keystoneos-verification-habits]]\n\n**2026-09-22 — a slow nightly index was the Mac sleeping, not memsearch.** The daily backup's\nfull index took 18 min for 0 new chunks (09-21 09:44) and 15 min for 309 chunks (09-21 18:12).\nTimed while awake: no-op ~9s fixed startup, `dev-docs` (763 files) no-op 26s, full set 83s with\n79 new chunks (~0.7s/chunk). `pmset -g log` showed 5-second DarkWakes in the morning window and\nIdle Sleep twice mid-run in the evening. Fix: launchd `dailybackup` + the three data pulls now run\nunder `/usr/bin/caffeinate -i -s` (`-s` holds only on AC; lid-shut on battery still sleeps).\n**How to apply:** before tuning memsearch, line the slow window up against `pmset -g log`.\nSame session: `daily-backup.ps1` now resolves `{DEV}` in index paths (it had skipped\n`KeystoneOS/decisions`). Jev/TypeSafe does not help indexing: it judges, it does not embed.\n\n**2026-09-28 (Windows laptop) — the 600s cap is machine-speed-dependent, and headless sessions fanned it out.**\n`memsearch-sync.js` ran back-to-back 600s-killed runs all day (streak 33), each peaking 2.7–3.1 GB, and\nClaude Code's low-memory reaper killed other sessions' background jobs. Not a full reindex — memsearch is\nincremental per file. Two causes stacked:\n- A **10,138-chunk never-indexed backlog** (all of `transcripts/`, plus `dev-docs` sorted after\n  `printing-press-library/…`). bge-m3 on this CPU embeds ~5 s/chunk (Mac 0.7–1 s), so each run cleared ~110.\n- **Headless TypeSafe drive-triage `claude -p` sessions** (entrypoint `sdk-cli`) ended every ~30s — 257\n  SessionEnds Sun 19–22h — plus every `flush.py` SDK session (the hook lacked session-end.py's\n  `CLAUDE_INVOKED_BY` guard). The PID lock serialised them into a continuous chain; it bounds overlap, not load.\n\nWindows has **no nightly index**: `KeystoneOS-DailyBackup` is disabled and `Backup AI Sessions to Drive`\nonly harvests dev-docs. So nothing drains a backlog and nothing resets the streak.\n\nFix: the hook skips `CLAUDE_INVOKED_BY` / entrypoint `sdk*`, with a 30-min start cooldown\n(`transcripts/.memsearch-last-run`, checked while holding the lock); `~/.memsearch/config.toml`\n`batch_size = 8` (peak measured 921 MB vs 3.1 GB); one-off drain task `\\Eric\\memsearch-drain-once` →\n`~/.claude/scripts/memsearch-drain.ps1`, log `~/.claude/scripts/logs/memsearch-drain.log`.\nStill open: the Codex Stop hook `KeystoneOS/scripts/codex-hooks/memsearch-sync.ps1` (per turn, 45s) has no cooldown.\n**How to apply:** \"memsearch eats RAM\" on Windows → check the streak in `.errors.log` and do a read-only backlog\ndry run (memsearch's scanner + chunker + `compute_chunk_id` vs Milvus `chunk_hash`) before blaming Milvus.\n","numLines":101,"startLine":1,"totalLines":101}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"589ec0c6-18d3-4f1f-b174-b86a5fe34175","session_id":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"step1-triage"}
