{"parentUuid":"c93d26b0-8974-4e33-abd5-5fc833339687","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"28ec5541-f125-4afa-94de-6e3e5c2897ff","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0197fyQSbZLNGhPDAmttMBgW","type":"tool_result","content":"3:Source: `staging/transcripts/landonevansphysicalpreparationinthencaaacomplementaryapproach.md`, Landon Evans, \"Physical preparation in the NCAA: a complementary approach\" (CVASPS). Machine draft: faster-whisper medium.en, 68.3 min, 1,481 segments. Timestamps below are segment start times from that file.\n9:Nobody has listened back to this audio or checked it against captions. The spot-check below was done by reading the text only. It can flag words that look wrong; it cannot confirm any word, name or number. **Nothing in this transcript should be cited, and no entry should be raised on its strength, until Eric or another listener has confirmed the flagged spots (at minimum every row marked \"caveat: ASR\" in section 2).** After that, stage 2 (Codex falsification review) still applies to any entry change.\n19:| # | Timestamps | Short quote (as transcribed) | Judgment |\n31:| Timestamp | As transcribed | Likely intended (Inference) | Confidence |\n81:Count: 5 sample passages; 47 flagged rows in the scan table (several rows cover repeat instances of the same error). High-confidence fixes (Pfaff, Issurin, Bondarchuk, Omegawave, NCAA, fast-twitch, Schexnayder, SFMA) are safe to correct once a listener confirms. Everything marked low needs the audio.\n85:All 34 entries are `untested`. \"Caveat: ASR\" means the matching passage contains or sits next to a flagged spot. Strength scale: **same claim** (he says essentially what the entry says), **consistent** (he says something that fits it), **partial** (fits one part only).\n91:| Entry | Current | Talk timestamps | Short quote | Strength | Proposed change | Caveat |\n93:| `framework--adime-monitoring-process.md` | untested | 18:52 to 19:15 | \"the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics ... They have a process. It's called ADIME. It's a part of their NCP. ADIME means assessment, diagnose, intervention, and monitoring and eval ... We can more or less do the same thing.\" | same claim | untested -> supported (locator: CVASPS 18:52-19:15). Also: the entry's Inference that ADIME comes from clinical dietetics can be replaced by Evans's own statement with this timestamp. | \"NCP\" and \"diagnose\" are near-certain; listener should confirm \"NCP\". |\n109:| `framework--evans-history-lineage-of-sport-science-giants.md` | untested | 05:29; 16:14 to 16:29; 18:28 | Names Charlie Francis, Dan Pfaff, Henk (Kraaijenhof), Yuri (Verkhoshansky), Issurin, Bondarchuk as influences. | partial (6 of the 15 slide names appear; he also credits people not on the slide: Val, Boo Schexnayder, Marco Cardinale, Mark McLaughlin) | Keep untested; add locators. | Every name here is ASR-flagged (Paff, Hank, Isherin, Bonnerchuk). |\n110:| `claim--sympathetic-vs-parasympathetic-overtraining-and-omegawave-readiness-case.md` | untested | 06:04 to 06:17; 60:33 to 60:42; 63:37 | \"PTC also has an Omega Wave. So that ... afforded me the opportunity to challenge a lot of the things that I do with athletes, and then see what the biological outcomes of that were.\" | partial (confirms his Omegawave use; nothing on SNS versus PSNS overwork) | Keep untested; add locator for his Omegawave background only. | \"Omega Wave\" / \"Mega Wave\" flagged. |\n116:| Entry | Talk timestamps | What the talk says | Nature |\n121:| `claim--fitness-fatigue-model-is-unvalidated-concept.md` and `framework--verkhoshansky-activation-levels-and-threshold-of-adaptability.md` | 46:37 to 47:05 | \"how you deplete them to then have them adapt to that and super compensate ... So smaller depletes with smaller rises. I'm not looking for a big deplete with a big rise.\" | Tension, not contradiction. Inference: he dismisses the fitness-fatigue model as \"JUST A CONCEPT\" on the slide but plans with deplete-and-supercompensate language in the talk; that language fits the Verkhoshansky supercompensation slide better. Flag for Eric; do not resolve silently. \"I compensate\" at 46:27 is likely misheard. |\n---\r\nsource: staging\\audio\\landonevansphysicalpreparationinthencaaacomplementaryapproach.wav\r\nmodel: faster-whisper medium.en (CPU, int8, VAD)\r\nduration_min: 68.3\r\nchecked: no (machine transcript, nobody listened back)\r\n---\r\n\r\n[00:20] So the goal of this piece is to more or less kind of bring a lot of the pieces that we've shared today\r\n[00:28] into the system of how I kind of view it and how I work with a lot of these elements within the NCAA.\r\n[00:37] This is an interesting presentation to put together just because this is something that I try to instill\r\n[00:45] within our GAs, our interns, maybe an assistant that's just coming in.\r\n[00:51] Anyone that I'm just on the phone with, just setting up the framework\r\n[00:57] and then being able to work within that framework because a lot of the times\r\n[01:01] I think we're asking the wrong questions to one another and then we're still kind of left with what to do.\r\n[01:08] So without further ado, first off, I just put this because we have a lot of coaches at ISU.\r\n[01:18] We have a lot of philosophical differences, but at the end of the day the most important thing is the kids.\r\n[01:25] So that is our one goal. We do agree on a lot of things, but there are some things that we don't.\r\n[01:32] So I don't want to pigeonhole ISU athletics within what I'm going to be speaking about.\r\n[01:38] Now just a quick background. So I started in computer engineering for three years.\r\n[01:46] And in fact, if you have your computers on right now, I likely have access to your computer already.\r\n[01:52] Joking, of course. But that was a reality of my life early on, even in high school.\r\n[02:02] You know, I was a creature of curiosity, hacked a lot of the phone systems at the time,\r\n[02:08] because it was an analog world. It's kind of easy to intrude on that.\r\n[02:12] But it was more for the sake of just, again, just curiosity.\r\n[02:17] A lot of the pieces within engineering just really developed my thinking process as well.\r\n[02:23] Now I asked a question later on within my program, like, can I do this for the rest of my life?\r\n[02:31] And now I kind of kicked myself a little bit because how this thing has exploded.\r\n[02:37] I didn't even know about half the possibilities that people are doing right now.\r\n[02:41] But I said no, so then I jumped ship.\r\n[02:45] My wife was on the other side of campus at ISU.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"66b87937-62a1-4a60-bd86-545c23e6ffed","timestamp":"2026-09-28T20:23:20.813Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"3:Source: `staging/transcripts/landonevansphysicalpreparationinthencaaacomplementaryapproach.md`, Landon Evans, \"Physical preparation in the NCAA: a complementary approach\" (CVASPS). Machine draft: faster-whisper medium.en, 68.3 min, 1,481 segments. Timestamps below are segment start times from that file.\n9:Nobody has listened back to this audio or checked it against captions. The spot-check below was done by reading the text only. It can flag words that look wrong; it cannot confirm any word, name or number. **Nothing in this transcript should be cited, and no entry should be raised on its strength, until Eric or another listener has confirmed the flagged spots (at minimum every row marked \"caveat: ASR\" in section 2).** After that, stage 2 (Codex falsification review) still applies to any entry change.\n19:| # | Timestamps | Short quote (as transcribed) | Judgment |\n31:| Timestamp | As transcribed | Likely intended (Inference) | Confidence |\n81:Count: 5 sample passages; 47 flagged rows in the scan table (several rows cover repeat instances of the same error). High-confidence fixes (Pfaff, Issurin, Bondarchuk, Omegawave, NCAA, fast-twitch, Schexnayder, SFMA) are safe to correct once a listener confirms. Everything marked low needs the audio.\n85:All 34 entries are `untested`. \"Caveat: ASR\" means the matching passage contains or sits next to a flagged spot. Strength scale: **same claim** (he says essentially what the entry says), **consistent** (he says something that fits it), **partial** (fits one part only).\n91:| Entry | Current | Talk timestamps | Short quote | Strength | Proposed change | Caveat |\n93:| `framework--adime-monitoring-process.md` | untested | 18:52 to 19:15 | \"the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics ... They have a process. It's called ADIME. It's a part of their NCP. ADIME means assessment, diagnose, intervention, and monitoring and eval ... We can more or less do the same thing.\" | same claim | untested -> supported (locator: CVASPS 18:52-19:15). Also: the entry's Inference that ADIME comes from clinical dietetics can be replaced by Evans's own statement with this timestamp. | \"NCP\" and \"diagnose\" are near-certain; listener should confirm \"NCP\". |\n109:| `framework--evans-history-lineage-of-sport-science-giants.md` | untested | 05:29; 16:14 to 16:29; 18:28 | Names Charlie Francis, Dan Pfaff, Henk (Kraaijenhof), Yuri (Verkhoshansky), Issurin, Bondarchuk as influences. | partial (6 of the 15 slide names appear; he also credits people not on the slide: Val, Boo Schexnayder, Marco Cardinale, Mark McLaughlin) | Keep untested; add locators. | Every name here is ASR-flagged (Paff, Hank, Isherin, Bonnerchuk). |\n110:| `claim--sympathetic-vs-parasympathetic-overtraining-and-omegawave-readiness-case.md` | untested | 06:04 to 06:17; 60:33 to 60:42; 63:37 | \"PTC also has an Omega Wave. So that ... afforded me the opportunity to challenge a lot of the things that I do with athletes, and then see what the biological outcomes of that were.\" | partial (confirms his Omegawave use; nothing on SNS versus PSNS overwork) | Keep untested; add locator for his Omegawave background only. | \"Omega Wave\" / \"Mega Wave\" flagged. |\n116:| Entry | Talk timestamps | What the talk says | Nature |\n121:| `claim--fitness-fatigue-model-is-unvalidated-concept.md` and `framework--verkhoshansky-activation-levels-and-threshold-of-adaptability.md` | 46:37 to 47:05 | \"how you deplete them to then have them adapt to that and super compensate ... So smaller depletes with smaller rises. I'm not looking for a big deplete with a big rise.\" | Tension, not contradiction. Inference: he dismisses the fitness-fatigue model as \"JUST A CONCEPT\" on the slide but plans with deplete-and-supercompensate language in the talk; that language fits the Verkhoshansky supercompensation slide better. Flag for Eric; do not resolve silently. \"I compensate\" at 46:27 is likely misheard. |\n---\r\nsource: staging\\audio\\landonevansphysicalpreparationinthencaaacomplementaryapproach.wav\r\nmodel: faster-whisper medium.en (CPU, int8, VAD)\r\nduration_min: 68.3\r\nchecked: no (machine transcript, nobody listened back)\r\n---\r\n\r\n[00:20] So the goal of this piece is to more or less kind of bring a lot of the pieces that we've shared today\r\n[00:28] into the system of how I kind of view it and how I work with a lot of these elements within the NCAA.\r\n[00:37] This is an interesting presentation to put together just because this is something that I try to instill\r\n[00:45] within our GAs, our interns, maybe an assistant that's just coming in.\r\n[00:51] Anyone that I'm just on the phone with, just setting up the framework\r\n[00:57] and then being able to work within that framework because a lot of the times\r\n[01:01] I think we're asking the wrong questions to one another and then we're still kind of left with what to do.\r\n[01:08] So without further ado, first off, I just put this because we have a lot of coaches at ISU.\r\n[01:18] We have a lot of philosophical differences, but at the end of the day the most important thing is the kids.\r\n[01:25] So that is our one goal. We do agree on a lot of things, but there are some things that we don't.\r\n[01:32] So I don't want to pigeonhole ISU athletics within what I'm going to be speaking about.\r\n[01:38] Now just a quick background. So I started in computer engineering for three years.\r\n[01:46] And in fact, if you have your computers on right now, I likely have access to your computer already.\r\n[01:52] Joking, of course. But that was a reality of my life early on, even in high school.\r\n[02:02] You know, I was a creature of curiosity, hacked a lot of the phone systems at the time,\r\n[02:08] because it was an analog world. It's kind of easy to intrude on that.\r\n[02:12] But it was more for the sake of just, again, just curiosity.\r\n[02:17] A lot of the pieces within engineering just really developed my thinking process as well.\r\n[02:23] Now I asked a question later on within my program, like, can I do this for the rest of my life?\r\n[02:31] And now I kind of kicked myself a little bit because how this thing has exploded.\r\n[02:37] I didn't even know about half the possibilities that people are doing right now.\r\n[02:41] But I said no, so then I jumped ship.\r\n[02:45] My wife was on the other side of campus at ISU.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c93d26b0-8974-4e33-abd5-5fc833339687","session_id":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage","sessionId":"e054b45d-33cd-4562-af51-7c3370696c58","version":"2.1.283","gitBranch":"step1-triage"}
